      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>WARRINGTON GO-OPERATIVE SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON GO-OPERATIVE SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>A Satisfactory Report</p>
        <p>The quarterly meeting of the Warrington Equitable Industrial Co-operative Society, Limited, was held in the Co-operative Hall, Cairo-street on Monday evening, Mr.T. Birchall, J.P. (president) presiding over a good attendance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>LETTER FROM THE AUDITOR..</head>
        <p>LETTER FROM THE AUDITOR..</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN read a letter from the auditor (Mr. J. Shaw Green) regretting his inability to be present. He was now entering upon the 18th week of enforced confinement at home, but he was thankful to say that his strength was returning and he had hopes of being about again after a while. (Applause.) The chairman said he was sure they all most heartily sympathised with Mr. Green in his long affliction, and hoped that he might soon be restored to health. Continuing he said it was a , great pteasure for them to have with them that evening Mr. F. Monks, who was the first secretary of the society—(applause)--and who worked heart and soul for sears to make the undertaking a success.</p>
        <p>THE QUARTER'S PROGRESS. A DIVIDEND ON SUGAR.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said he was glad to repeat the steriotyped phrase that they had had another satis- factory quarter. He said it with more emphasis on that occasion because he had heard so many complaints among tradesmen in the town of the unsatisfactory and unre- munerative condition of business. There was no doubt that.things were not as bright as they used to be, and while the society had made progress, the deposits were not as great as usual—because the members were not able to save quite as much—and the withdrawals were more numerous than they generally were. Still their. sales had increased during the past quarter by nearly £l,OOO. They were making satisfactory progress and the committee had come to the conclusion, after care- ful consideration of the subject, for the first time in 43 years to pay a dividend on sugar. That meant that a dividend would be paid on everything they sold and the addition, would be equivalent to more than two pence in the £. This would require 'a considerable sum—probably. £1,200 per annum—but thanks to their careful financing in the past they felt confident that they would be able to pay it. The society was able to make the proud boast that for twenty-five years it had never paid a dividend of less than3s.in the £. He did not knowa single society in the district that was able to say the 4ame thing. Some of their mem- bers who perhaps thought that the Society had been unduly careful in the matter would be surprised to know that the Society was depreciating less than any important society in the district. They had had some sad revelations in Warrington during the early part of the year, of dividends that ought never to have been paid, of reckless financing and dishonesty which had had very sad and distressful effects. There was a stupendous responsibility resting upon men who took charge of the savings of artisans and poor men, and so long as the present committee had any say in the matter, the members of that Society wouldnot have anything of the kind he had mentioned to trouble them. The committee were determined that if they erred at all they would err on the side of safety. (Applause.) The balance sheet/Was submitted and showed that the sales for the quarter amounted to £32,730 is. 5d., which were the highest sales ever recorded, and in coAnparison with the corresponding quarter of last year, showed a substantial increase of ,£1,73035. 4d. The profits, after providing for all liabilities, depreciation, interest on capital, Scc., would allow a dividend of 3s. in the on members' purchases. The balance sheet was adopted. THE PROPOSED COAL WHARF.</p>
        <p>The Committee had considered the proposal to establish a coal 'wharf so as to be able to supply coal to the members in small quantities. Arrangements could be made with a firm to supply orders of five bags and upwards and it was considered that in face of the greater responsibility, risks &amp;c., which the establishment of a wharf would entail, it would be desirable to continue the present system. Mr. CLXRE spoke in favour of the proposal to acquire a wharf and said he should move a resolution on the matter at a future meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>REMUNERATION OF THE COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>THE REMUNERATION OF THE COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>At the lazt quarterly meeting it was suggested that £lO 10s. should be granted to each member et._ the com- mittee as some remuneration for his services during the past four years. The committee had since considered the matter and had come to the conclusion that while it had always been their desire to benefit their fellow members, so long as they had their confidence, if the funds permitted a small remuneration it was perfectly right. All they wished for was the confidence of the members. MAYBURX said he had been given to understand that it would be illezal to pay the committee. The CHAIRMAN : It is not illegal and most societies pay their committees. Mr. MAYBURY intimated that at the next meeting he would propose that each member of the committee be paid £2 10s. per year. The minutes were approved and the committee will receive £lO 10s. each for their services during the past four years.</p>
        <p>THE BEWSF.Y ROAD PROPERTY.</p>
        <p>At a committee rhieting on May 6th, it was resolved that the seal of the society he attached to the deeds of the Bewsey-road property,that the secretary he instructed to sign the transfer of the property, and that a cheque for £750 he handed to Messrs Davies and Co, acting on behalf of Mr. Smethurst, in payment for the property. _ . . .</p>
        <p>_ In reply to Mr. Crowther ihi3 chairman said ito, time would be lost in opening the premises. The SECREURY (Mr. A. itathbone) said the com- mittee were Waiting for the opening of the new works at Bewsey before forming the bew branch.</p>
        <p>PROPOSED ESL AR GEM ENT 'OP THE COM M ITTEE. Acting upon a suggestion that the committee should be enlarged the committee had ascertained that an alteration of rules would be necessitated before such a proposal could be carried out. A special resolution would have to be passed by the Society authorising the Secretary to apply to the Registrar for such alteraion to be made. . . .</p>
        <p>Mr. B. FARR A.R, who .vas unable to attend owing to indisposition, wrote strongly advocating an enlargement of the committee. _ _ _ .</p>
        <p>Mr. MAYBURY intimated that he would give notice that at the annual meeting he would imse that the coal- =Wee be enlarged to twelve members.</p>
        <p>THE EARLY DAYS OF THE SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The SECRETARY submitted an interesting statement showing the extent of the trade of the Society in its early days and the steady progress which had been made. lie 'had a list of fourteen societies and not one, of them was paying a divide:ad of 3s. in the pound, (Applause). Only one society showed so small a depre- ciation as their's.</p>
        <p>THE RECENT STORM.</p>
        <p>Mr. CROWTHER pointed out that during the recent severe storm the cellar of the Lovely-lane branch was flooded. He contended that the Corporation could be made responsible and that they ought to be warned. Mr. G. WALLINGTON said nearly all the cellars in the tOwn were flooded because the sewers could not carry off all the water. The matter then dropped. THE COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The following rdtiring members of the committee were re-elected :—Messrs. William Jeffrey, 'l'. Wads- worth, E. Williams, and J. Albinson. This concluded the business.</p>
        <p>The sou of a Newtown-Butler, county Fermanagh, farmer, while bathing in Lough Erne on Thursday, dis- covered a peculiar-looking sword. The handle bears the date 1640, and it is surmised that the sword was the property of Viscount M'Carthy,• who led an army under King James, and was defeated in the battle of New- town-Butler.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The visit of Queen Victoria to Ireland, not long before her death, proved most welcome to the Irish people, and the enthusiasm with which they greeted her late Majesty was a guarantee for the warmth of the reception that has been extended to King Edward and his Consort. Some of the earlier arrangements were modified, and the Royal visits to the Lord Lieutenant and Lady Dudley at Rockingham Castle and to the Duke and Duchess of Devonshire at Lismore Castle,had to be given up, but their Majesties have had a succession of engagements to fulfil and will not leave Ireland until Saturday the Ist August. After visiting Cork, the King and Queen will depart on board the Royal yacht for Cowes, where a brilliant gathering is expected for the Regatta week. Their Majesties will probably attend the christening of the infant son of Prince and Princess Charles of Denmark, before going abroad, and the King is expected to make a three weeks stay at Marienbad before proceeding to Balmoral in the Autumn, There are people living who can recollect the time when George IV. visited Ireland in 1821, when modes of travelling were very different from what they are now. King George went by way of Portsmouth, where he embarked on board the Royal yacht on August Ist, and arrived at Holyhead on August 7th. There he was detained for nearly a week by stress of weather, and finally crossed to Dublin on a steam packet which was then, regularly crossing the channel. It was the first time that a British Sovereign had travelled by steam, either on land or sea, but even so it took King George about two weeks to make the journey between i,ondon and Dublin, whereas now one may breakfast in one city and have an early dinner in the other. It was whilst staying at Holyhead that George IV. heard of the death of the Queen in London, but he was so little upset by the news that when he arrived in Ireland he told the people in greeting them that it was one of the happiest days of his life. Dr. Lunn, who may be considered an authority on the "Art of Holiday-making" says that pleasant com- panionship is very essential, and in his judgment four is the ideal number for a holiday party. If there are only two; each is unduly dependent upon the other, and may become mutually bored with each other's society. Three will not do, as this leaves an odd one whereas with four there is a possibility Of constant change of companionship. As a rule advice on the subject of holidays is not of much value, as most people have their own ideas of enjoyment, but Dr. Lunn makes some use- ful suggestions. A person with an outward nervous system is apt to think that high bracing air is the most desirable change, but it is no use to go to a hilly or mountainous district if, as is not unlikely, the over- tired one is unable to sleep well in high altitudes above sea level. It would be-better to go to Holland,or for a cruise on the Norfolk Broads. Then, whilst a sea voyage may be good for the atimmic person, it is clearly not the thing for those who are inclined to over-eating, and to take too little exercise.</p>
        <p>A Lincolnshire Vicar has joined in the many protests which have been made against the custom of throwing rice or confetti at weddings. In London it is usually easy to sweep up the litter thus made round the doors of the church, but in the country, where the rice or con- fetti generally falls on the gravel path, it is very diffi- cult to clear it away, and another objection is that it is liable to litter the graves, which in most country churchyards, line the path to the church door. The rail- way companies have also objected to have their stations strewn in an unseemly manner with confetti, and some of them have not only made it an indictable offence against wedding parties, but there have been one or two instances in which all sentiment has been outraged by the police being called in. Lace mittens, either black or white, are being much worn by the modish woman, whose fingers nowadays are loaded with rings, and the effect does not always give one the idea of good taste. Many brides have this year gone to the altar in mittens, but in such instances there is much to be said in favour of the fashion. The diffi- culty of getting on and off a well-fitting glove has long been felt by brides, and the mitten, white of course, generally Brussels applique, the design orange blossoms, heather, and myrtle, with true lover's knots, affords an excellent and desirable substitute.</p>
        <p>Corn yellow and pink, is a very modish combination, but one that needs the most careful consideration. To be quite on the safe side we should do well to .go to nature. A tea rose for example, is an excellent guide its yellows mid pinks are most beautifully blended, so also are the tones of the peach. It is perhaps because the French go so much more directly to Nature for in- spiration than our designers,that they are such superior colorists. The colorist who is not constantly con- sulting Nature gets crude in his or her contrasts just as the artist does, who paints a landscape indoors, and puts in the finish from his own imagination. A reaction in favour of tightly fitting coats seems probable, and after so much looseness this will prove a welcome change. The tailors have by no means approved of the long spell of favour the.sac coat has enjoyed, and are doing their best to revive the tightly fitting jackets and coat, in the building of which their skill is absolutely necessary if the creation is to be a success.. The Norfolk jacket, finishing at the waist, where it disappears into a belt, is in great favour for holiday wear, and very neat and trim it looks on a slender figure. In note paper the fashionable tint is pale grey blue, the address, and monogram or crest, being in white. Blotting pads may be had in the same colour to accord with it. Against this delicate, neutral 'tint little can ba said, though pure white or cream is perhaps in really better taste. Her Majesty the Queen, prefers and uses notepaper in palest cream, stamped in red, and the Princess of \Vales has a similar preference in this direction.</p>
        <p>The craze for decorating dinner tables, with hideous and creepy insects, lighted by means of electricity, has unfortunately not yet died opt, and the nerves of many guests are frequently tried by discovering some horrible, creeping thing apparently about to alight upon their unhlcky heads. Not long since a well-known society woman was dining at a table upon which some very natural looking spiders had found a place. A sudden movement caused one to drop from some highly arranged foliage almost into the lady's plate, and such is her horror of this 'particular insect, that she had to be _removed in an almost fainting condition from the room. Hostesses who are given rather to eccentricity than the care for the nerves of the guests, will do well to bear this incident in mind. Cats with gleaming eyes, and owls with blazing yellow orbs, are familiar objects upon electrically lighted, tables, as also are birds of divers kinds, but flowers lighted up with beautiful luminous colours are much to be preferred from every point of view.</p>
        <p>MADGE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>AGAIN, AND AGAIN, AND AGAIN.</head>
        <p>AGAIN, AND AGAIN, AND AGAIN.</p>
        <p>Again we ask you to drink Vi-Cocoa. Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa isneither a medicine nor a mere thirst-assuager. It is a food at the same time that it is a beverage, And thus answers a double purpose in the building up of the human constitution., You can try it free.of expense. Merit alone is what is claimed for Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa, and the Proprie- tors are prepared to send to any reader who names this Journal a dainty sample tin of Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa. free and post paid, upon receipt of a post card to the Head Office, 60, Bunhill Row, London, E.C. ; or you can purchase a 6cl. packet or 9d. or ls. 6d. tin from any grocer or stores. Vi-Cocoa is the cheapest and best food beverage in the world.</p>
        <p>COMMERCIAL FA ILlTREs.—According to "Kemp ' Mercantile Gazette," the number of new bankruptc proceedings in England and Wales gazetted during t h week ending July 18th, was 101. The number in t h corresponding week of .last year was 74, showing an i n crease of 27, being a net decrease in 1903, to dote, 0122</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>FASHIONS OF TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>FASHIONS OF TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>July is the month par excellence for gardert• parties and all outdoor social functions, and therefore dainty frocks and fascinating hats are in great request. Those who cannot afford a great variety in their dresses will find this season many etceteras Of the toilette which can be introduced at different times, when quite a change will be wrought in the general appearance. There are pretty ruffles of all sorts, which look very charming and smarten up a plain toilette wonderfully. One can also make a pleasing addition in the form of a lace coffee coat worn over a silk blouse. These little coatees are truly very charming, and are of infinite comfort to the</p>
        <p>wearer during the height of summer, They look equally well with a long trailing much gauged and gathered skirt, or a short walk- ing skirt of some very light-coloured fabric. Very fine net embroidered with a floral pat- tern of wreaths or nosegays in a soft ecru tone is. often used in the construction of such coats—which it is hardly necessary, I sup- pose, to mention are all made loose and of the sac order, with a deep collar edged with a lace frill, which is very often inter-threaded with very narrow silk or satin ribbon. Sometimes very 'narrow black velvet is used, and offers a pleasing contrast, being every now and again made into little bows caught with tiny buckles of cut steel. Nothing for wear on a hot summer's afternoon could be more fascinating and suitable than this shady and picturesque hat with its draperies of creamy lace falling over the brim in front and forming a deeper fall at the back. One or two very pale pink roses apparently catch the lace up towards the crown, and a single string of soft pink tulle comes from the back and passes round the left side to the right shoulder, where it is fastened in a careless bow. Worn with a pink or ecru muslin gown, the effect is really charming, and anyone possessed of clever and skilful fingers can easily turn a last summer's hat into a very fair imitation of</p>
        <p>this hat. It only requires a couple of yards of good lace or of an imitation of a good pattern, together wish some pearl-headed pins such as may be )ought for a couple of pence a dozen, the h. e can soon be arranged with satisfactitm to the wearer. •</p>
        <p>I notice that some of the smartest black picture hats are trimmed with coloured or shaded ostrich feathers with a wonderfully stylish effect. For instance, wie worn with a black lacey gown had some very handsome cerise ostrich plumes curling round the brim on to the back of the coiffinie.</p>
        <p>The smart costume depicted, is . for sea- side or river wear, and is made of fine cream serge with a short pleated skirt arranged into a shaped and close-fitted band, which covers the hips and extends in a panel down the front. The upper part of the sleeves are tucked, and the little bolero has pleats inserted at intervals, finished by cordings in scarlet. The cream silk collar is also finished by scarlet silk cord and scallops, the latter being worked in black. These em- broidered black scallops indeed give the gown its cachet, and are used at different points of the gown. The pleats in the bolero are of scarlet silk, and a black silk scarf With scarlet anchors worked on it is loosely</p>
