      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>RT COATEE,</head>
        <p>RT COATEE,</p>
        <p>knotted in front. Worn with a scarlet cloth peaked cap, the effect is decidedly chic, and quite out of the common. The new serges are exceedingly nice to wear, being light and at the same time warm, and lend themselves readily to pleatings, while .they keep their shape and place when other materials look limp and unsightly. Serge is undoubtedly the material for sea- side wear, for it stands such a lot of hard wear and careless treatment, and always looks smart and business-like. The smart little coatee depicted is of scarlet linen, for this is the latest mode. It is lined with white silk, and has a collar of very coarse ecru lace. The fronts can be worn turned back so as to show the lining, or closed as may be desired: The little tabs or tails are of stitched linen covered with lace.</p>
        <p>Linen this year is much in demand for morning gowns, and in the best _qualities does-not crush easily, which is a great point in its favour, for nothing looks so horrid as a crumpled cotton or linen skirt. Linen em- broideries are also much in request for trim- ming linen or canvas gowns as well as other dresses, and also supply some of the newest and prettiest pelerines which look so well for seaside or country wear. Linen, whether used to make an entire dress or in the 'form of embroidery is a very good investment, for it always does up nicely and looks specially well when trimmed with coarse lace. Crash or linen ..owns seem particularly well suited for every-Clay wear, and are this season to be got in almost any shade or colour, so that no one should find any difficulty in finding something to suit them, whether they be young or old. Maltese lace is sometimes used to trim these gowns, and looks very smart- "</p>
