      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>ARRIVAL OF THE VICTORIA AND ALBERT WITH NAVAL ESCORT IN KZNGSTOWN HARBOURI</head>
        <p>THE ARRIVAL OF THE VICTORIA AND ALBERT WITH NAVAL ESCORT IN KZNGSTOWN HARBOURI</p>
        <p>THE XING IN IRELAND.</p>
        <p>• n</p>
        <p>Taw.</p>
        <p>I_. -</p>
        <p>His subjects cannot help admiring and marvelling at the activity of the King, and certainly few of them have got through more during the present summer than he has. In addition to a brilliant London season and a series of Courts in the metropolis, a great Continental tour, and the magnificent recep- tion of the French President, His Majesty has devoted a week to Scotland and royal functions in the North; and now has come what may be termed the Irish fortnight. It is so many years since the King visited Ireland, and many more since a Court was held at Dublin Castle, and the Irish capital has been en fete for the occasion. The decorations were superb and most beautiful, flowers pre- dominating everywhere over bunting, and lending a most charming appearance to the whole.town. Grafton Street especially was the object of general admiration, and it is impossi- ble to imagine anything finer—it looked just like a tunnel cut out of flowers. At night the illuminations were equally brilliant. It is an open secret that the King is delighted at his reception, and the outburst of loyalty was spontaneous and • enthusiastic, except in the instance of a few cranks and extremists. The progress of the Land Bill and the repeal of the Crimes Act have gone far to convince the Irish as a nation of the goodwill borne towards them across St. George's Channel, and by none more than by the royal family</p>
        <p>--.BE VICE-REGAL LODGE, WHERE THEIR MAJESTIES STAYED I DUBLIN. • themselves. The King and Queen left Buck- ingham Palace on Monday morning and drove to Euston, leaving about one o'clock. Holy- head was reached in the late afternoon, and the royal party embarked on the Victoria and Albert, which was in waiting. The royal yacht with its escort arrived off Kingston the following morning, and their Majesties landed about an hour before noon and were met by the Duke of Connaught, who is in command of the troops in Ireland. The royal party then drove through the streets of Dublin to the Vice-regal Lodge, which had been placed at their disposal by the Lord-Lieutenant, the 'Earl of Dudley. According to the programme, which was a very full one, Wednesday morn- ing was devoted to the presentation of more than a hundred addresses from various public bodies in the throne room, while in the after- noon the King held a levee at Dublin Castle at noon. On Thursday the King presents colours to the Royal Hybernian Military School, and held a review in Phcenix Park. In the evening there was a Court at Dublin Castle.</p>
