      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>ABDICANT BISHOP.</head>
        <p>THE ABDICANT BISHOP.</p>
        <p>The announcement of the resignation of the Bishop of Manchester has caused general regret, not only in church and local circles, but also amonsgst all interested in philan- thropic and charitable matters. Dr. Moor- 'use, who was born in 1826, and is, therefore, a very old man, is a pre- late who is on every side respec- ted for his breadth of mind and his determination of character; and the diocese of Man- chester, in particu- lar, will miss him.</p>
        <p>uring his seven- teen years' occupa- tion of the See, he las been an ener- etic worker, not nly in connection ,-ith the Church of England, but in any .movements to promote the gen- eral well-being of the neighbour- DR. MOORHOUSE, WHO HAS hood ; and, as a RESIGNED THE BISHOPRIC powerful organiser, OF MANCHESTER. as well as an</p>
        <p>able preacher, he nas left his mark behind him on every side. His resignation, though handed in to the Prime Minister last week, does not take affect until October 31st. The Right Rev. Dr. Moorhouse, was the son of a Sheffield cutler, and was originally intended for his father's business. He went up to Cambridge in 1846, and graduated in 1849; and then, after a short experience of business life, he decided to take holy orders. After his. ordination, he was fo,r many years a hard- 'working London' clergyman; but his ability was quickly recognised, and promotion soon came. In 1876, he was appointed to the Bishopric of Melbourne ; and the next decade was spent in the Colony. Original in his methods, out there he would at times throw aside his ecclesiastical garb, and go in for a short spell of bush life, teaching religion in a straight-forward fashion over a pipe and a camp fire. He displayed keen interest in sport, art, and the theatre; .and one of his first acts in the Coloiw was to .remove the embargo against the clergy of the diocese playing cricket. In 1886, he was Amade bishop of Manchester, and quickly worked his way into the hearts of the Lancashire people. Dr. Moorhouse has never been a voluminious writer, or a keen Parliamentarian. However, in the latter capacity his notable appearance last year during the debate on the Education Bill will long be remembered, when he carried his "wear and tear" amendment by 26 votes; ' His 'resignation removes a notable figure 'from the Church.</p>
