      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>TRURO CATHEDRAL.</head>
        <p>TRURO CATHEDRAL.</p>
        <p>A great deal of interest attaches to the visit of the Prince and Princess of Wales to the West of England, as Cornwall may, so to ,speak, be described as their own country; .that is to say, the visit is really being paid in the Prince's capacity of Duke of Cornwall. 'This maiden visit of their new over lord has been received by the Cornish with the utmost enthusiasm, and the Prince of Wales bids fair to become as popular with them as his illus- trious father has been for so many years. Starting auspiciously with a record run to Plymouth, their Royal Highnesses went on to -Trogothnan, where Lord and Lady Falmouth were their hosts. The principal function of •Che visit was the day at Truro, the principal city of the duchy, on Wednesday night, when -the nave of the new Cathedral, the first which has been built in England since the Reforma- tion, was consecrated: This Cathedral really owes its existence to the efforts of the late Dr. Benson, the first Bishop of Truro, after-</p>
        <p>wards Archbishop of Canterbury, by whose untiring work the scheme was first got afloat. Twenty-three years ago, on May 20th, 1880, the King and Queen, in their capacities of Duke and Duchess of Cornwall, laid the foundation stone at a memorable gathering, and it was fitting that the Prince and Princess of Wales should upon an occw.ion equally, memorable crown the work his father had begun. The Prince, or the Duke of Cornwall</p>
        <p>as he was for the day, after the reception of an address from the Mayor of Truro, attended the service of consecration, which was a very impressive sight. The Archbishop of Canfer- bury and more than twenty bishops were present iu full canonicals, in addition to all the Cathedral and diocesan clergy, and all the leading people of the county; and the Archbishop preached from-%Romans xv., 13. Afterwards a great luncheon was held in the Market Hall, at which Lord Mount-Edgecumbe presided; and the Prince. of Wales, in reply to his health, made a very popular speech, dwelling upon his association with Cornwall as heir to the throne.</p>
        <p>11 A' , '!:1124 ,i111..i! '16.1.,,,,a• - , mit v,•.,----- . : 11l "--•-•i&amp;-c----------,..- . • 1, LHt tr;ts, - '9,,,___. _- 4r:.--.„....,F4......,.....„..,,,9 !,. il ilkiitigkiNOßlb 1.-2111M1111111 1---ithp" kithgliViis[4o)hi&gt; t- .-----7-,,,,,-.7..,..,firg-7,_:,,,,get-,;,,.,-„ } "I I"o(EiiiiiiMil 01_01,4Ago, fulft —— - --'—.- —''''''.....Ar-52:'-'7:-_•71.4:4774'4:-.Z.-_'---7:7"- i'...'"'77:.=-1-'-'---- . - ---..</p>
        <p>TRURO CATHEDRAL AS IT WILL APPEAR WHEN THE IMPROVEMENTS HAVE BEEN EFFECTED.</p>
