      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>LORD MOUNT-EDGECUMBE AND HIS FAMOUS PILE.</head>
        <p>LORD MOUNT-EDGECUMBE AND HIS FAMOUS PILE.</p>
        <p>In his speech at Truro, the Prince of Wales expressed his intention of seeing as much of the Duchy of Cornwall as possible during his stay, and this he did by touring. with the Princess on a motor and visiting the ledßtng county magnates and their seats under the auspices of Lord Falmouth, his . host, Every possible place of interest and importance were visited; and on Monday the Prince and Prin- cess went to stay with Lord Mount-Edge- cumbe, the Lord-Lieutenant of the Counts, and an old favourite at Court. . Lord Mount- Edgecumbe, though over seventy years of age, is hale and hearty, and a fine type of old English gentleman, and his Court connection dates back many years. In the earlier Admin- istration of Lord Salisbury he was invariably Lord Steward of the Houselfold, and thus came into very intimate touch with all the .members of the royal family. Recently he has lived a more retired life down at his beautiful home; but seldom, if ever, do any of the royal family travel westward without visiting him. Mount Edgecumbe is an his. 4,,,-</p>
        <p>SA3 111,1 -t• $e efi</p>
        <p>it; , Pedk!- -1/4_</p>
        <p>MOUNT EDGECUNIBE, WHERE THE PRINCE AND PRINCESS HAVE BEEN STAYING THIS WEEK.</p>
        <p>toric house; and this was the stately demesne which the commander of the Armada pro- posed in his arrogante to allot to himself as a reward for his self-assured success, which proved such a notable failure. Thus, the Duke of Medina-Sidonia never had the option he had counted on, and it remained in the hands of its original owners. Lord Mount- Edgecumbe's principal hobby is lanlscape- gardening, and Nature has been prodigal with the material which she has supplied him with on his own estate, than which there is none fairer in the whole British Isles. The stately house stands on the Crown of a promontory, and is a fine specimen of architecture. Sucb a family naturally has many legends and tradi- tions connected with t; and of them the most interesting is that of the Lady Mount-Edge- cumbe who was buried alive; An avaricious butler noticed that her rings had been buried with her, and went to rob the tomb at night. By tugging at her hands he awakened her, to his horror, and she returned to her !husband to live many years and to bear him a son and heir.</p>
