      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>GREATEST BATTLESHIP.</head>
        <p>THE GREATEST BATTLESHIP.</p>
        <p>Another important function with which the Prince and Princess of Wales are con- nected this week is the launching of the new battleship on Thursday. This battleship is named after the King, being called H.M.S. King Edward VII., and will be the most mag- nificent man-o'-war afloat. The design is almost exactly that of the famous Royal Sovereign, though, of. course, King Edward VII• has numerous exclusive improvements, and is to be finished with an amount of care to detail' that has not, even in the world of perfection of a modern naval dock.</p>
        <p>KING EDWARD VII., LAUNCHED ON THURSDAY, WILL MARK AN EPOCH IN BRITISH NAVAL</p>
        <p>CONSTRUCTION.</p>
        <p>yard, vet been b-stowed upon any battle- ship. In fact, as is suggested by the name it has been decijed to give her, she is to be the premier boat in the Navy, and an example to other nations of what Great Britain can do in the way of modern naval construction. Such boats as King Edward VII. are what Britain wants to-day to place her in that position. in the estimation .ot others that it is doubtful she has held since the clays when we were so justly proud of our British oak. Ring Edward VII. was launched by the Princess of Wales at Devon. port, and right heartily was the whole district en fete in honour of such an histories.) event.</p>
