      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>WARRINGTON EDUCATION COMMITTEE. THE NEW SECONDARY SCHOOL. Amount of the Fee Discussed.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON EDUCATION COMMITTEE. THE NEW SECONDARY SCHOOL. Amount of the Fee Discussed.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. MONKS presided at the mohthly meeting of the Warrington Education Committee which was held in the Council Chamber at the Town Hall on Monday evening. There was also present :—Aldermen Bennett, and Smethurst, Councillors Tinnion, Pierpoint, Dodds, John Roberts, Hulse,. Davies, Upson, Brophy, Brough, and Ellison, the Revs E. J. Willcocks, John Yonge, and Father Wilson, Major J. D. Buckton, Messrs J. Fulton Smith, R. T. Fairclougb, A. J. Jolley, E. Plinston, G, Walker, T. May, and J. Moore Murray, (director of education).</p>
        <p>THE DIRECTOR'S SALARY. DISCUSSION ON TEIE APPORTIONMENT OF</p>
        <p>SOFIOOL FEES„</p>
        <p>COUNCILLOR CRITICISM. At a meeting of the Finance Committee held on the 26th June, it was resolved that the following accounts be passed for payment :—Teachers' salaries, £1,573 15s. 3d.; school attendance officers' salaries, B:c.. £Bl 3s. 6d.; general account, £324 Os. Bd.; that room No 6 in the Technical Institute be converted into an office for the use of the clerks in the education department and for the school attendance officers, and that the director be empowered to obtain tenders for the necessary furnish- ing of the office. A resolution passed by the Associa- tion of Foundation Managers of the Church of England Day Schools with reference to the apportionment of school fees having been read, it was resolved that for the year ending March 31st, 1901, one quarter of the fees received in each school charging fees be repaid by the Education Committee to the managers of such schools in half-yearly instalments.— At a meeting on the 6th of July, the committee having interviewed thti selected candidates for the positiens of chief clerk and stationery and stores clerk, it was resolved that Mr. R. B. Roberts, of the Liverpool School Board be appointed chief clerk and assistant to the Director, and that Mr. A. S. Elliott, of the Bradford School Board be appointed stationery and stores clerk.—At a meeting on the 13th July, it was resolved that the following accounts be passed for payment:—Teachers' salaries, £1,582 3s. id.; school attendance officers' salaries, &amp;c., £Bl 10s. id.; general account, £555 14s. Bd. The Borough Treasurer reported that the account for the Director's salary for June was passed by the Finance Committee of the local authority, but that he was instructed to make further inquiries as to the date on which the increase of £25 shoald begin, when it MS decided that it be a recommendation to the Education Committee that the increase of the Director's salary commence from April Ist, 1903.—A resolution of the School Attendance Com- mittee with reference to the office accommodation was considered, and it was resolved that the Chairman (Mr. A. J. Jolley), Mr. Councillor Pierpoint and Mr. R. T. Fairclough be appointed to consider with three mem- bers of the School Attendance Committee, the present and future office requirements, and to report to this committee; and that the tender of Mr. Ralph Johnson, amounting to £6l 3s. 6d., for office furniture, according to specification be accepted. Mr. A. J. JOLLEY moved the adoption of the minutes and said the apportionment of fees was a matter de- pendent on the directioa of the local authority and it was thought that in view of the expenditure which the managers would have to face, and taking into account the general local circumstances, it was reasonable and equitable that the apportionment of one quarter of the fees should be allotted to the managers of the schools in which fees were charged. In regard to the Director's salary, the minute appeared on the agenda in conse- quence of inquiries which had been addressed to them by the Finance Committee of the Town Council. The resolution was unanimously passed at a well attended meeting of the Finance Committee. Councillor DODDS said he was not quite sure whether to pass the resolution would be suffi?ient. There was a resolution now standing which was passed by the Council to the effect that „the salary remain at £3OO a year for at least twelve months. An amendment was also passed but it did not in any way invalidatel or interfere with the resolution. That amendment provided that the salaryshould be increased by yearly increments of £25 until the maximum of £5OO was reached and he took it that the advance was to commence in twelve months time. He did not say he objected to the increase but he certainly felt that the officer of the Local Govern- ment Board -would not pass the addition so long as the resolution that the Salary stand at £3OO for twelve months remained upon the minutes. He had asked the opinion of the Town Clerk who replied that he (the speaker) was absolutely right and the increase ought not to begin until April, 1901. He (Councillor Dodds) was of the opinion that before the present reso- lution was passed his motion ought to be rescinded. He would like the ruling of the Town Clerk on the matter. The CHAIRMAN t We can pass the minutes as moved by Mr. Jolley, and if it is found necessary to rescind it we can do so. This would be in:the nature of an under- standing that the Finance Committee of the Council should pay Mr. Murray's salary from the Ist of April. If it is necessary to rescind the resolution then do so. Councillor DODDS : If it was the intention of those who voted for the increase that it shauld begin at once, I will not stand in the way. I appreciate Mr. Murray's services as much as anyone else. The CHAIRMAN: Mr. Guest has authorized me to say that that was his intention. We have not to deal with the intention of Mr. Guest so much as with the intention of the committee.</p>
        <p>Councillor TINNION pointed out that the resolution would only be a recommendation with the Town Council and that body could rescind Councillor Dodds' resolu- tion if necessary.</p>
        <p>-.- The TOWN CL,ERK : The resolution referring to Mr. Murray's salary is silent as to the date on which the increase commences. It is open to the construction Councillor Dodds has put upon it, but the resolution proposed to-night does not necessitate the rescinding of the previous resoluti ma. It is only an addition to it. Councillor DODDS : It is quite contrary to it. The TOWN CLERK: I beg to differ on that point. Councillor DODDS : My resolution said the salary should remain at £3OO for at least one year. The REV. JOHN YONGE asked what Mr. E. J. Guest intended in the matter.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN: That the salary should commence on the first April last. The Rev. J. YonGE : I seconded that resolution,but I did not understand Mr. Guest in that way, and I did not second it in'that way. (Laughter.) lam perfectly willing, however, that it should be that way; more than willing, anions. As a matter of absolute fact I second- ed it as one way out of the difficult corner we were in. I wanted Mr. Guest to make the increase £5O instead of £25, but he would not. (Laughter.) I certainly under- stood the increase was to begin next year. Mr. PLLNSTON thought it would have been better to the Finance Committee to have appealed to the Educa- tion Committee as to what was the real meaning of the original resolution before passing the account for £25. The CHAIRMAN: If we confirm the minutes it is quite clear that the mind of the Committee is that the increase should commence on the Ist April last. If it is necessary for the Finance Committee to protect itself by rescinding the resolution of Councillor Dodds we are quite open to do so. Mr. PLINSTON thought it would be better for an appeal to be made to the Finance Committee before passing the account. Councillor DODDS : As a member of the Finance Committee, I don't wish to he in the position of having the accounts surcharged by the auditor. Mr. THOMAS MAY: This is a recommendation, and unless it is specially passed it will not stand good. The CHAIRMAN: .If you pass the minutes you authorize the Committee to act upon theni. The Rev. J. YONG?: : I think Mr. May is right with all respect to the Chairman. In case of a recommenda- tion it means that the Committee. can act upon it if thought fit. I don't think that merely confirming the minutes will confirm the recommendation. , Mr. MAY: The minutes are only a record. _ _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Councillor PIERPOINT thought tfiat if they dated the increase in Mr. Murray's salary from the Ist April they would only be doing justice to him. The matter would work itself out. Councillor UPSON' called attention to the decision of the committee in regard to the apportionment of school , fees. The Act said in section 11 that a portion of the fees should be returned t o the managers as might be agreed upon. That he took to mean that managers must be approached by that authority to see what their needs were, and what proportion of the fees they re-ti quired. So far as he knew no body of managers had been approached, and the fact that they had a hard and fast rule as to one quarter of the fees seemed to be ridiculous. Some figures were quoted; and Councillor Upson contended that the minute on the face of it was absurd. One might receive £6OO a year in fees and be re-paid £l5O, whereas another school of practically the same size might have only £l2O in fees and consequently the manager's proportion would amount to £3O. Another school might receive only £5. The thing on the face of it was absurd. (Hear, hear.) If a school had been receiving £6OO for years past it would need the money even less than the poorer school. The cem- mittee could equalize the matter to some extent. The wealthy school ought to be content to take one fourth or one fifth whilst the poorer, school should have at least one half. The managers had a right to appeal to the Board of Education before the committee compelled them to take any proportion of the fees. " The Rev JOHN YONGE supported what Councillor Upson had said, because it . appeared to him that a quarter of the fees would in some cases he very sufficient for the requirements. He moved that the matter be referred back to the Finance Committee for re-consideration, in order that a more just arrangement might be come to. . _ _</p>
        <p>1 1 douncillor UPdoN seconded. I Mr. A. J. JOLLEY said the matter did not arise in section 11 of the Act but section 14. The Finance Committee did not seek specially to bring the matter forward. It came before the Education Committee in a resolution communicated by a recognized body in the t town and was referred to the Finance Committee for i consideration. It was quite open to the Educa- . a</p>
        <p>tion Committee to give a lead in other direc- tions if they were not satisfied. But, he con- tended, the Finance Committee had not acted altogether unwisely in making the proposition. If they had suggested a differential rate they would have been accused of favouring sectarianism and making better grants to one school than another. He contended that the proportion was a reasonable one. The com- mittee wanted to keep out any chance of being accused of being actuated by sectarian motives. Whatever proportion was fixed, there would be some hardship. borne schools did not charge any fees and they had the most ground of complaint because they had to meet equal responsibilities. Councillor UPSON thought Mr. Jolley had very suc- cessfully drawn the red herring across the- trail. The argument that some schools had no fees at all was altogether beside the question. It was an absolute injustice that one school should retain £l5O when another only received £2O. On a vote being taken the amendment was lost and the minutes were confirmed. THE NEW SECONDARY SCHOOL. SALARIES OF' THE STAFF. AMOUNT OF THE FEE DECIDED. At a meeting of the School Management Committee on the 25th June it was resolved that permission be given for the following appointments :—St. Barnabas' National, certificated master; Heatlaside National (boys), certificated master; Ladies' School of Industry certificated mistress; St. Alban's Roman Catholic certificated mistress. That permission be given to the managers of the St. Barnabas' National School to appoint a trained certificated head mistress for the Infants' Department, and that the salary be £lOO per annum until a scale of salaries for head mistresses is adopted • that consent be given to the appointment of the following teacher: Mr. W. S. Fairbrotlaer, Heathside National (boys), salary accord- ing to scale • that the managers be required to give the Education Committee at least 14 days' notice of their intention to close the schools for bazaars, tea parties, &amp;c.; and that when the schools are closed by the head teachers on account of H.M. Inspector's visits or other reasons the head teacher must send word to the Direc- tor of education. The report of the sub-committee appointed to consider the applications for the position of assistant cookery mistress was considered, and it was resolved that Miss B. M. Watkins, of Newton-le-Willows, be appointed assistant mistress of cookery; also that evening schools (for reading, writing, arithmetic elementary drawing, common knowledge, elementary shorthand) be held as in previous years, at the following schools :—Warrington B (mixed), Hamilton street N. (boys), Warrington P. (boys), Heath Side, N. (boys), Fairfield, N. (girls) ; that the appointments of super- . . .</p>
        <p>intendents orthe evening schoOfs be offered to the terchers who have hitherto acted as such; that an evening school for girls be held in the Wycliffe B. school, and that an advertisement be issued for the superintendent of the same; that the superintendent be paid 10s 6d per evening together with a bonus of 2s 6d for each scholar who makes 75 per cent of the possible attendances. That the salaries of the assistant teachers be 5s per evening together with a bonus of 3s 6d for each scholar in their classes who makes 75 per cent. of the possible attendances; that the superintendents be allowed to nominate one assistant for every 35 scholars attending the schools. At a meet- ing on July 10th it was resolved that the following accounts be passed for payment :--General account, £329 95.; buildings account, £204 95.; Middleton scholarship account, £9 6s. Bd.•' that permission be given to the managers of the St. Mary's (R.C.) school to appoint one certificated assistant master and one uncertificated assistant mistress; that consent be given to the appointment of the follow- ing teachers :—Miss K. Robinson, Sankey Bridges Wesleyan; Miss Frances Bolton St. Barna.bas' (infants) ; Miss C. Grounds, St. Barnabas (mixed) ; Miss Annie Burton, Hamilton-street (girls); Miss Susan Hehir, St, Alban's (mixed); Miss A, E. Richardson' St. Mary's R.C. ; Mr. S. Price, St. Mary's R.C.; and that the students of the pupil teachers' classes who were successful in passing the candidates' examination, but are-of age not to be apprenticed as pupil teachers until August, 1904, he offered scholarships in the secondary day school. A report on the work of students at the. Pupil Teachers' Centre who failed at the recent candi- date's examination having been considered, it was re- solved that the managers of the schools from vihich these children are candidates be informed that the com- mittee cannot any longer recognize them astandidates ; also, that the managers of the Sankey Bridges Wesleyan School be requested to appoint a certificated assistant mistress; that the ccminittee cannot recognize any agreements entered into by the managers which increase the salaries of teachers above the scale adopted by the committee on the 18th May last. A letter was read from the Board of Education stating that the Board had notified the Teachers' Registration Council that the Warrington pupil teachers' classes are recognized for the purpose of registration on. column B. of the register of teachers, when it was re- solved that the following arrangements for the secondary day school be agreed to :—(a) That the school year be divided into three terms, and that the fees be at the rate of 9d per week, paid terminally 10s. 6d. per term, or annually 30s. per annum. (b) That the parents of children entering the school be required to give an undertaking of their intention to send their children _ _</p>
        <p>to the school regularly for at least 12 months. (c) That the subjects of instruction be :—Mathematics' English, history, geography, French, physics, chemistry, manual instruction in needlework and housewifery (girls), geometry, freehand, model drawing, music and drill, manual instruction in wood and drawing (boys.) (d) That the first and second year pupil teachers must attend the secondary school six half days per week in- cluding Saturday mornings. (e) That the scholar- ship candidates must attend five half days including Saturday mornings. (f) That advertisements be issued for the appointment of the following staff :—Head- master, at a salary of £250; male assistant, at a salary of £l2O ; two female assistants, at a salary of £llO each ; chemistryassistant, at a salary of £140; physics assistant, at a salary of Zl4O ; manual instructor, at, a . _ _</p>
        <p>Councillor TLNNION said he thought Alderman Monks had at a previous meeting fairly and clearly stated that the expenditure was not intended for 100 scholars only. That did not seem to have satisfied the whole of the ratepayers, and after all, the committee wanted to satisfy them because they wished to havetheir co-opera- i tion n carrying out the Act. In reference to the advertisements about the headmaster in particular and some of the assistants, it was stipulated that they should have had some experience in a secondary school. He thought it would be rather unfortunate if the committee adhered to that, because he did not think that those schools had been going long—since the Cockerton judgment. Any experience which teachers might have obtained in such schools could not be of very much &amp;ore value than that of a good schoolmaster who had some experience in a Technical school. Such a man ought not to be excluded. Councillor UPSON said there was a slight misunder- standing as to the intention of the School Management Committee. They did not want to run counter to the general opiniop ; they wanted the full approval of the majority of the ratepayers upon what they were about to do. He noticed from the local press on Saturday that there was a disposition to look askance at the scale of salaries and the staff which the Committee wished to appoint to the school.lEle did not think it was clearly understood that the teachers had not only to teach 100 children. but 125 pupil teachers. Then again, many of them would have charge of the night classes, and there would be a certain number of teachers who had in the past been engaged in the Technical School who would not be reuired, and their salaries would be set off against this sBrale. As to the scale itself, most of the persons they wished to appoint would probably be men with scientific degrees. He wished the commit- tee to fairly consider whether a man who had been to a university, and who had studied many years to qualify himself for taking a scientific degree, was not worth at least £3 a week. (Hear, hear.) The highest salary offered next to that of the headmaster was £l4O, and there were many skilled artizans in Warrington who- were getting better pay than that. To cavil at these sums seemed to him to be rather monstrous. Mr. May had said that this was not for Warrington. Who was it for then? (Hear, hear, and a Voice : "Outside.") If sufficient children applied for places in the school those outside the county borough of Warrington would not be admitted. He pointed out that girls could not attend the Grammar School, and in conclusion hoped that on due consideration the members of the Education Com- mittee would agree that the committee had done the best they possibly could to bring before their notice for their approval the scheme for the secondary school and for the pupil teachers. Colonel 11</p>
        <p>oloneA-UCKTON said there were to be evening schools in six schools only, land they were not widely dispersed. There was not one in Latchford, He con- tended that the subject of the evening schools should be taken up as seriously as any other subject. There were tbousands of children leaving school at 13 or 14 years of age, and they would never go to school again unless evening schools were provided and they were encouraged to go to them. The speaker spoke in favour of girls being taught cookery and sewing. Mr. PLINSTON moved an amendment that the fee for the secondsry schoal be sixpence instead of ninenence as praposed. He slid that at Msnchester asd Widnes', which he believed ware booth very successful secondary schools, the fee was sixpence. He could not see any reason why they should pay 3d. per week more in War-</p>
        <p>• tt</p>
        <p>hardship on any poor boy. At People's College School a technical sense, but in the broadest and best sense the fees reached about sixpence a week, and he took it of the word. (Hear, hear,) that the parents of the children who attended the Mr. R. T. PAIRCLOUGH said that seeing the town would be put to a large expenditure in teaching the secondary school would have no difficulty in affording ninepence. pupil tea,chers it was only right that the ratepayers Colonel BUCKTON was in favour of the resolution as should be safe-guarded in regaid to teachers continuing it stood. He believed the parents could pay and did in the profession. If they did not,the town would lose not fully appreciate anything they got cheap. a considerable sum. He asked if the condition in re- The CHAIRMAN hoped that in voting they would con- gard to the forfeiture of £5 in case of non-attendance sider the interests of education. It was all very well to would apply to pupil teachers as well as to scholars. talkabout "the poor working man." From his exper-1 Mr. A. J. JOLLEY : A separate clause would be ience he found that if the working man wished to spend necessary for pupil teachers. Again replying to Mr. a shilling a week on anything he was quite willing and I Fairclough, Mr. Jolley said an agreement would have to able to do so. The did not find that the very poor paid be entered into in accordance with the new regulations. much to the rates. Let them give the best educationl Mr. MAY thought it would be a scandal on the part they could to the children they wanted to educate and of the committee tc* pass such a scale of salaries as that give every child in Warrington a fair chance without I referred to in the minutes with reference to the staff. It amounted to a large sum per annum. it seemed to any favour. He was quite sure theparents were willing to support the secondary school and would pay nine- him that the committee were trying to act in a liberal pence as cheerfully as they paid the sums at present manner but he had a very strong opinion that in dealing charged. with public money one had no right to be liberal.("Oh") Mr. JOLLY said there seemed to be a radical mis- A great many people seemed to think that tb is committee tuiderstanding of the position. underlying several of were providing for the education of Warrington. For the speeches. That committee and the local autbority those who were able and willing to pay sufficient pro- were not started for the administation of o it-relief. vision was made already in the exdellent school at the They were there as the education authority of the town. east end of the town for the education of young men for (Hear, hear.) all sorts of professions. They in that committee were ii,x. MAY:ni One of them. not providing for the education of Warrington, and were 1 Mr. JOLLEY : We are the education authority, and it using funds taken by force from rich and poor, mostly is our duty to deal with education in all its branches. ' from the poor, and applying them first of all to provide Continuing, he said he considered they had made very leducation for the better and the richer of the working satisfactory progress and he wanted to clear away the classes—in a very liberal manner, too—before they pro- misapprehensionthat it was a question of charity or vided for the extremely poor. In his profession he had anything of that kind. It was said that the reduction always been taught to suggest to the Government every would only mean 255. a week. At the present time it possible means of economy for the purpose of decreasing did represent that, but they were only starting the the burdens of the public. In conclusion Mr. May said school with 100 scholars, and hoped in time to have he would not say that the salaries were excessive, but 1,000. Then the amount would be equivalent to a penny he thought they should not be passed on a resolution of on the rates. He hoped the committeeasa whole would a sub-committee. The whole committee should have support the School Management Committee, and keep time to consider the salaries. The sum of £l,OOO was the fee at 9d. suggested for providing education for 100 scholars when Mr. PLINSTON said he did not think any of the corn- an education could already be obtained by those who i mittee could charge him with inconsistency. He main- were able and willing to pay for it. , tamed that 6d. was too much to be paid in elementary Alderman BENNETT said that Mr. May was right from schools, and the day would come when he would pro- one point of 'view but wrong from another point. It pose something with reference to that matter. should be remembered that there were over 60,000 The CHAIRMAN said Mr. Plinston was not in order people in Warrington, and a large proportion were not as he had already spoken. able to educate their children as Mr. May suggested. , Councillor PIERPOINT: He proposed the amendment He would not express an opinion as to whether the and he can reply. salaries were high. They seemed to be very high at the i Mr. Jor,Lxv: We have no standing orders. outset, but it was their absolute duty to the town and to the state to provide the best education that was Mr. PLINSTON: Others have been allowed to do so, and I maintain that I ought to be allowed the same possible for the very poorest lad in the State at what- priveleae. ever cost. That was necessary if they wished to do 1 The NAIRMAN their duty to the rising generation. They wished to . twice. provide an education for those who could not afford to 1 Mr. PLINSTON: They reply. If I am not to be attend the Grammar School. ' allowed I will not ask the priyelege. Other people have _ ___</p>
        <p>They are not allowed to speak</p>
        <p>been allowed but I will resume my seat. - - The Rev. J. YONGE : I think we might hear Mr. Plinston. Mr. PLINSTON : If it is against the rules lam not going to do it. The amendment was than put to the meeting and the voting was as follows FOR THE AMENDMENT. Alderman Bennett Councillor Brophy Brough Upson Mr. T. May Mr. G. Walker Mr. E. Plinston-7.</p>
        <p>FOR THE RESOLUTION. I I The Chairman The Rev. E. J. Willcocin The Rev. John Yonge The Rev. Father Wilson Alderman Smethurst Councillor Pierpoint Hulse Colonel J. D. Buckton Mr. A. J. Jolley Mr. Fulton Smith Mr. R. T. Fairclough-11. The amendment was therefore lost. The Rev. JOHN YONGE referred to the bonuses to teachers' and said he did not understand why the assistant teacher should have a bigger bonus than the head teacher. (Hear, hear.) Mr. JOLLEY said the assistan t teacher only got the bonus on his class, bat the he ad teacher got it on the whole school. Mr. JOLLEY, in answer to Councillor Pierpoint ex- plained the system ,of the evening school teachers' salaries, and said the amount per evening varied accord- ing to the numter of classes taken. They would be pleased to see a larger number of evening schools instituted, but it was a matter on which they must proceed with caution. Previous evening schools in the town had met with varying degrees oft. success, but it was open to the committee to increase the number next year if thought advisable. The committee were also fully alive to the necessity for cookery and sewing, &amp;c.' and would give the matter their attention. The School Management Committee had been the heaviest taxed of all the committees, and the Director had given a great deal of time and thought to the advancement of the arrangements as far as they had gone. The Rev. J. YONGE called attention to the minutes with reference to the closing of schools for bazaars and tea parties, and tho tight that the 14 days' notice was too mush. Occasions might arise xvlibre it woulti be difficult to give 14 days' notice. He moved that it be altered to seven days. Mr. Mmt" seconded. Mr. JOLLEY said the department repired sev en days' notice, and how could the Director give seven days notice if he only received seven himself? Eventually Mr. Jolley agreed to a proposal that the tice should be ten dap.</p>
