      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>FATALITY AT STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>FATALITY AT STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>A Dangerous Road.</p>
        <p>A shocking fatality occurred at Stockton Heath on Monday evening. A potato cart in charge of Thomas Lathom' of Garwood, was being driven in the direc- tion of Warrington when the horse took fright at a traction-engine and plunged on to the footpath. Two boys named Fred Dudley, seven years of age, 21, Gaskell- street, and Walter Maddock, six years of age, happened to be standing there and Dudley was knocked down and sustained severe injuries. He was removed to the residence of Dr. Parker but expired shortly afterwards. Maddock escaped with an injured foot. . .</p>
        <p>• Mr. J.OH. Fisher, deputy coroner, conducted the inquest at the Mulberry Tree Inn, on Wednesday. After evidence of identification had been given by the father of the deceased,</p>
        <p>James Powel, 22 and 24. James-street, Warrington, said he was walking over the bridge on Monday after- noon, about half-past five, when he saw a traction engine with three empty lurries coming towards Warrington. Thedriver of the engine had "slowed up" for a horse and cart to go across the road. Before te engine had Tot up its speed witness saw a potato cart drawn by a cob coming from Stockton Heath. When it got near the engine the cob suddenly jumped to the right. Three little boys were on the crossing,and one was knocked down by the near fore foot of the horse, and the wheel passed over his body. There was no time for the driver of the engine to be signalled, and the driver of the cart was only going at a walking pace. After the wheel had passed over him the little boy got up and ran about four yards, and then dropped. Thomas Lathom, Garswood, greengrocer, the driver of the cart, said he had no time to pull up when his horse sprang aside.</p>
        <p>Joseph Holiday, the driver of the engine, having given evidence, Mr. Whitley, foreman of the jury, said this was the sixth or seventh fatal accident he knew of from London Bridge to Twenty-step Bridge, and he attributed this to the narrowness of the road. A verdict of" Accidental death " was returned, and a vote of condolence was passed with the parents.</p>
        <p>ARNOLD AND LEWIS, 7 ST. ANN'S SQUARR, HANOI:1113T1% HALLMARKED SILVER GOODS.</p>
        <p>Established 1830.</p>
