      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>Fire.</head>
        <p>Fire.</p>
        <p>Information was received at the Fire Station at about half-past four on Monday afternoon that a fire had broken out at the wood works of Mr. Eli James, situated on some open ground behind Cumberland street,. Knutsford-road. On arrival, however, the brigade, which turned out with the steam engine, under the command of Superintendent Turner, found that their services were not required. Some boys had been playing at the back of the premises and had ignited some wood. The flames, however, were speedily extinguished by a few neighbours, and very little damage was done.</p>
        <p>IT becomes increasingly THREE SORTS OF clear that much of the PROTECTIONISTS, support that Mr. CHAR-</p>
        <p>BERLAIN will be able to obtain for his proposed fiscal changes will be given for reasons which are almost, if not quite, unconnected with the grounds on which his pro- posals are based. Mr. GEORGE RENWICK, one of the members for Newcastle-on-Tyne, is supposed to be responsible for the Colonial Secretary's visit to that northern city. He is a Protectionist of many years' standing, and he sees Mr. CHAMBER- LAIN through the mist of his own views, and, con- equently, with some indistinctne3s. He sap the Colonial Secretary " wishes, and I think very properly so, to place (say,by means of import duties upon manufactured goods from those countries which tax our goods) a weapon in the hands of our Government by which they can negotiate more favourable terms for our merchants in such countries; and, further, I think he very properly wishes to give preferential treatment to our Colonies." But, really, this is not what Mr. CHAMBERLAIN wishes. Mr. RENWICK'S statement is partly a concealment and partly a 'distortion of the Colonial Secretary's proposals, on which the constituencies may be called upon to pass a verdict before they have been fairly explained, and whilst they are, as in Mr. RENWICK'S case evidently mis- understood.</p>
        <p>What is it, then, in respect to which Mr. CHAMBERLAIN asks for a mandate? He professes to link the Colonies more closely to us by giving them certain trading preferences. The idea is that they will then do much more trade with us than they do at present,and that, broadly speaking,they will take all that we have to sell, whilst we pur- chase from them all that we need. To bring about this condition of things Mr. CHAMBERLAIN would put a tax on certain articles that we receive both from the Colonies and from foreign countries, such as wheat, and beef and mutton. Having put this tax on the foreigner and the Colonies alike, he would, on condition of a lowering of Colonial tariffs as against ourselves, remit the tax on Colonial produce and only exact it on goods coming from foreign coun- tries. A distinct system of preference would thus be established. Our imports consist of food, raw materials, and manufactured goods. Toe Colonies do not send us manufactured goods to an extent that is worth mentioning. To tax manufactured imports would not help the Colonies in any way. Mr. CHAMBERLAIN, therefore, does not propose to shut outmanufactures by taxation. Nor does he propose to tax raw materials, without free admis- sion of which we could barely continue to exist as am industrial people. His own words are: "If you are to give a preference to the Colonies . . . you must put a tax on food." He contends that such a tax, with a preference given to us by the Colonies would raise wages, and he also represents that the money obtained by the taxation of food will ' be set aside for the provision of old-age pensions. , What the Colonial SECRETARY has said he has I said ; but the fact is no impediment to his saying something quite different later on, and so we have the further statement that the money which is to be set aside for old age pensions will be applied to the cheapening of tobacco :So beneficent a system is to be inaugurated, that is to say, that the British working man is to eat his cake, in the form of cheap tobacco, and to have it, later on, in the form of a pension for old age. But this is not what Mr. RENWICK understands or what is understood by, apparently, the bulk of those who have rallied to the support of Mr. CHAMBERLAIN. It is not even what is understood by Lord LANSDOWNE, the Foreign Minister, who wishes, as he says, to be armed with a "big revolver" in order that he may coerce foreign countries. All these seize upon Mr. CHAMBER- LAIN'S plan either as if it were merely retaliatory, or as if it were intended to exclude manufactured goods sent to us from abroad,a course which would result in the total evanishment alike of the pros- pect of old-age pensions and of cheapened tobacco. The peculiar economic school from which the Colonial Secretary is deriving most of his support has been contending for years that this country is being ruined because the foreigner "dumps down" his naanufacturea goods in our ports. That the country is not being ruined, but is more pros- perous than it has ever been before, is a fact that is not permitted to intrude into the discussion. If it is pinted out that only 18 per cent.of our imports are manufactures, and that our exports consist of manufactures to the extent of 85 per cent., we are referred to individual experience of certain small industries which may have suffered from foreign competition, but which are just as likely to have suffered through want of enterprise, or want of capital, on the part of those who are engaged in them. By means of certain clamorous publica- tions intended to alarm the English people, an impression has been widely disseminated to the effect that Germany, in particular, is flooding our home markets with manufactured goods, the truth being that our exports to Germany and our imports theteftom as nearly as possible balance each other.</p>
        <p>Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S principal supporters, then so far as can be perceived, are those who wish to keep our home markets for ourselves—the out-and-out Protectionists—and those who wish to deal a retaliatory blow at the foreigner because he does not let in our goods duty free. But when persons of this kind support Mr. CHAMBERLAIN it is a personal support which they ive him, perhaps in the hope that he will come ound to their views, or perhaps because they do</p>
        <p>not understand his own. It is of the</p>
        <p>highest importance that a clear distinc- tion should be made between these quite different and sometimes antagonistic sets of opinions. The retaliationists would want to retaliate on our Colonies if they sent us goods that we manufacture at home, for the Colonial tariffs are mainly and admittedly maintained against ourselves. The Colonial Premiers said with groat distinctness that any proposal to establish Free Trade with England would be opposed by the whole strength of the Colonial Protec- tionist and manufacturing interests. But this opens out another question which there will be further opportunities of discussing. What we have desired to make clear for the present is that the Chamberlainite party is made up of men who are involved in a welter of contending views, and that support given to Mr CHAMBERLAIN on purely Protectionist or Retalia- tory lines would be given under a misapprehension as to the nature of his policy, and for a purpose which his present proposals—whatever might be their ultimate effects—would not directly realise,</p>
        <p>The long fluctuating struggle POPE LEO XIII. which has been going on at</p>
        <p>Rome between death- and a very aged man came to its inevitable end on Monday. With LEO XIII. there passes away one of the long roll of Popes. A man of more gentle, irreproachable life never sat in St. Peter's Chair. At his elevation to the Popedom, Cardinal PECCI was little known outside Italy, though he held the office of Cardinal CAmEßLErrao,and though he had been Nuncio at Brussels long before,and had even paid a visit to our own country. When the Cardinals were lacked up in conclave and specula- tion was running rife as to who would be chosen successor to Pius IX. there was outside Roma hardly mention of him on whom the choice of the Cardinals ultimately fell. And yet it afterwards appeared that his selection had never really been in doubt, as it was not the qualities that were conspicuous in Pius IX. that were looked for in the new POPE. PIO NONO was a man made to pass through stormy times, aril to play a large if not a wholly successful part in the direction of the affairs of the world. There was a commanding aggressiveness in his character. Yet he ended by becoming, in his own phrase, "a prisoner in the Vatican." Toe temporal power • slipped away from him. BISMARCK rose up in revolt against his authority, and in consequence of this, and of the accompanying Kplyikarnpli movement,the Papacy lost much of its influence in Germany. And there was another direction in which the aggressiveness of POPE PIUS tended to bring the Popedom into peril. He proclaimed the dogma of infallibility, and there arose a storm of resistance to the new pretensions, one of its :incidents being the controversy to which Mr. GLADSTONE con- tributed his famous Vatican pamphlets- It ap- peared at the time as if a fighting POPE would be considered to be best suited to the then existing situation, whereas the Conclave chose as the successor of Pio Nona a man of singular gentleness who loved his Horace, wrote Latin verse, and had a perpetual kind smile for everybody.</p>
        <p>The Cardinals nevertheless were wisely guided. The POPE, who had just died could look back over a long period of power during which almost every difficult question had disappeared, the power and the influence of the Papacy meanwhile increasing in every land. - proposal was seriously made only a few week!' since to the effect that the POPE and the CZAR Of RUSSIA should be induced to issue a joint declara- tion against the ancient superstition against the Jews, which is said to have led to the Kischeneir massacre. A suggestion like this would not have been possible in the time of the preceding popz. The feeling throughout Eufepe was that Pius DC. interfered too much, not that he required to be stirred to exertion in the interests of humanity:rho power of LEO XIII. grew because he seemed SO little to insist upon it, and also because it seemed to be exarted from motives of pure benevolence. But under the gentleness of his demeanour there</p>
        <p>was real strength of character. He was a man of great intellectual activity, thoroughly acquainted with the main currents of his period, and with a mind exceedingly clear as to his duties and res- ponsibilities, and to the relationships between the Church of Rome and the other Churches of Christendom. To this the celebrated "Papal Bull ou Anglican Orders" bears sufficient testi- mony. The one respect in which he resembled his predecessor was his resolute adherence to the "Prisoner in the Vatican" theory, which has stood an impenetrable barrier in the way of all real reconcilement with Italy The lesson of the earner of LEO XIII. as POPE is that the Church of Rome increases in authority much in proportion as it abates its temporal pretensions ; but it was not a lesson that was learned by the POPE himself, and so the fund with the Italian Kingdom has been kept with the effect of estranging a large proportion of the Italian people from the Church. This question now remains for a new POPE to consider, and to resolve who the new Pox shall be it would be vain to surmise. He can hardly be a better man as to all good human qualities than the POPH who has just died. LEO XIII has greatly adorned a lofty station.</p>
        <p>There has been a revival in local Liberalism thi week,which is as pleasing as unexpected. Through- out the country, the forces of Liberalism have been for some iime at a low ebb, and the same condi- tion of things, in perhaps an enhanced degree, has obtained at Warrington. Recently, however, there has been a marked quickening of Liberal send- , meat all over the land, due unmistakably to the Protectionist machinations of the Chamberlain party. Coincidently there has been a Liberal rally in Warrington, but we should hesitate in ascribing it to the attack on Free Trade, particu- larly as "The Examiner" has taken a prominent part in helping to lead public opinion on this great question, and we have still some remnants of modesty left, though it is not looked upon as a characteristic of the Press gener- ally. Be the cause what it may, there is no doubt that Warrington Liberals are at present fully alive to the responsibilities and obligations attached to a profession of Liberal principles, and the great majority are, we believe, ready and eager for the fight whizih seems to be impending. On Monday evening there was one of the best meetings of the Liberal General Council which has been held for many years, complete unanimity characterising the proceedings from first to last. Mr. Charles E. Parker was again elected Presi- dent of the Association, an office which he has held and is likely to hold for some time, with consider- able distinction. There is nothing half-hearted about Mr. Parker's Liberalism. Unlike Mr. Balfour he has his "settled convictions," particu- larly in regard to Free Trade, Vie benefits of which he is week by week expounding lucidly in our columns. The bon, secretary and hon. treasurer, Messrs. C. Jackson Holmes and W. Furness, were also re-elected, and each is recognised as a worthY officer of the Association. Such stalwarts ae. Councillor Pierpoint, Councillor Tinnion, Coup-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>gl'bare.</head>
