      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <p>• eillor James Evans, were also en evidence, and alto- Tether the meeting was full of encouragement for 'the future.</p>
        <p>On Thursday, the mem. tiers of the Women's Liberal Association had their day. There is no more active or earnest political organisation in the town than this branch of Liberalism, and their services to the party have been frequently of the greatest possible value. Thursday was the day for the annual picnic, which was attended very largely and proved one of the most successful in the history of the Association. The locale, Data- raere,could not have been bettered,and the excur- sion into tbe heart of pretty, pastoral Cheshire Was exceedingly enjoyable. Tea is, of course, the Primary function on this annual occasion, and we can vouch for the fact that the Liberal ladies ap- preciated to the full the cheering cup at Eddisbury Lodge. Their gentlemen friends, too, were not one whit behindhand in this respect, and these afterwards nobly did their duty by making the customary brief,discursive,and chatty speeches on the political situation which are necessary for the proper combination, on this annual occasion, of businests with pleasure. There Wili be a heavy call on the members of the Association very shortly for it was announced that the great Liberal Bazaar which has several times been mooted is going to be held, it will depend very hagely for its success on the exertions of the Liberal ladies who, fortunately, can always be re- lied upon. We fully expect that the Bazaar will equal if not surpass its memorable predecessor of Borne twelve years ago.</p>
        <p>The principal business of the Education Coin- Mittee on Monday evening was the consideration of the new Secondary Day School. A desultory discussion followed on the proposition of the tninutes of the School Management Committee,but there was no real opposition to the recommenda- tions, though they included the appointment of a new staff at an aggregate salary of £9BO. We 'bad expected to hear more about so Important and costly an advance in the •educational facilities of the town, but the members f the Committee must either be better informed In regard toj details, or else be more easily con- vinced than we are of the necessity for such an 'elaborate teaching staff in the new school. Appar- ently the proposal of the School Manage- ment Committee is in the nature of an " ornni- hue bill," by which a number of deficiencies lo other departments of education are to be conveniently made up. Wit are told . now that the seven new teachers are not all re- luired for the one hundred prospective scholars 'of the future secondary school. Some of them are required for the training of the pupil teachers and others for the instruction of the purely technical classes. This fact might very reasonably have been made clear before. When one reads in the agenda, under the specific description of arrangements for the Secondary Day School "that advertisements be issued for the following staff etc," it is only natural to suppose that "the following staff" is for the teaching of that school solely and simply. Things are not always what they seem, however. We have no ,clesire to charge the committee with extravagance or to hinder in any way the raising of education in NYarrington to a higher level, far from it, but we like important public business to be brought for- ward in a more explicit fashion than this has been. Even with the explanation vouchsafed at the meeting on Monday it will hardly be clear tO the ordinary ratepayer that he is going to get Au adequate return for his money in the expendi- ture of this £9BO in salaries. He has to take a Pod deal on trust, and it is hardly wise to ask Ina to do so, especially when the matter is one that touches his pocket.</p>
        <p>The arrangement whereby one of the meetings I3f shareholders of Parr's Bank Limited, is held .*toli year in Warrington affords local shareholders 44 opportunity of attending the meeting and Zoraing in touch with the leaders of this great `4oaking institution. It is also a pleasant re- c7itai der of the fact that Warrington was the cradle this bank which has developed to such an tent as to become one of the mightiest VI, the banking world. The statement by chairman at these meetings is always Ititeresting and that delivered on Thursday proved exceptionally so. Mr. Cecil F. Parr has just come back from a holiday in south Africa, and the excel- lent report he had to present led him to face- tiously observe that the bank got on quite as well, if not better, witholt him. Bankers generally have had I very profitable half-year,the Bank of England rate being maintained for the greater part of that laeriod at per cent. In alluding to the industrial Bitnation, Mr. Parr spoke of the necessity of our heconaing less reliant on America for our raw eotton and encouraging Colonial production. Raving just visited Natal he was able to assure the meeting that experiments were being made there as to the possibilities of cotton growing.lt was Perhaps, only natural that Mr. Parr should make a Passing reference to Mr. Chamberlain's fiscal Propositions. He advised all, whether faie traders or free traders, to examine for themselves the Tiestion. This is sound advice but when he came to speak of workingmen being willing to make l'acrifices from their scanty store for the goorl of the Empire, he was on less solid ground. They Would, we imagine, prefer a scheme which does ilot involve any more sacrifices.</p>
        <p>The election in St. John's Ward is unique in °lle respect. It is the first municipal contest for 12,?any years which has taken place in a division of ?tie town without either political party nominat- Ing a candidate or officially supporting one. Both Political parties have held aloof in the Present contest and the two candidates who "ve entered the field are fighting independently of political colour or organisation. Curiously enough both claim to be non-traders, but Mr. Thornton has the pull over his opponent in this 48pect for his candidature is backed by the Non- Trading Committee, with which he as been con- Noted since its inauguration and has been a loyal Pporter from the first. Mr. Maginn has shown t4lroself a robust campaigner and has addressed L°llr open-air meetings during the past week. On 7ednesday evening he coolly took credit for intro- 'ucing a subject which, he said, had not 3en touched upon hitherto in Warrington. had reference to the assessment of llcensed houses in Warrington. Readers of "The Examiner" will be aware of the strong Iltand which we took on this subject some years ago and which ultimately led to a revision of the essessments of public-houses in the town. As the testilt of that agitation licensed houses are much lbore equitably assessed at the present moment !ban they were 8 few years ago, but he time has arrived when the assessments should aghin be revised. Many inequalities still exist, Mr. Maginn pointed out, but we can hardly see 1.1,:1vw he hopes to remedy them by his being re- Orned to the Town Council. The Town Council 48 nothing whatever to do with the fixing Of assebsments. This work is in the hands of the ;aBoessreent Committee which is appointed by the oard of Guardians. It is more a question for a --joard of Guardians election, but no harm will til,ave been done by again calling the attention of 'le public to the subject which is of immense im- lortance. With rates steadily increasing, it is the "31 of the responsible authorities to see that all terests are equitably assessed, and an advantage tkl(luld certainly not be given to such a wealthy tade as that connected with the sale of intoxicants</p>
        <p>The sad fatality to a boy at Stockton Heath "0 Monday evening emphasizes in some degree the essity for proceeding with Light Railway No. 1 t-I•c'• the extension of the Warrington Tramways :Victoria Square, Stookton Heath. • Mr. nitley, the foreman of the jury who assisted the ci(3.l.oner in his inqtairy into the cause of the fatality, orectly attributed the accident to the narrowness to the road over this section, and we are inclined to azTee with him. From the Swing-brid ze Victoria Square the road resembles the of a bottle and is at no point 43:91ing like wide enough for the large amount oght and heavy, slow and fast traffic, which 1:118eR over it, while the approach from the ehdon Bridge side adds to the general public Itbger by its steepness. Monday's accident is the</p>
      </div>
