      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <p>seventh of a fatal character on this road within Mr. Whitley's recollection. Traffic is naturally increasing, and recent events show that accidents are becoming more and more common. It is not much more than a month since a drayman was killed a little higher up the road,and there is hardly a week passes but there are several casualties of a minor character. Cyclists suffer frequently from the congestion which usually exists on the road, and the widening,' which is part of the Light Railway scheme has become`imperatively urgent. This part of;Stocktonilleath is:a veritableldeath-trap,and the sooner the Corporation proceed with the extension of the tramway, for which they have now got full powers, the better it will be for the public safety.</p>
        <p>The tragic death of the Rev. F. R. Pearson, who less than a year ago came to Warrington to Wm charge of St. James's parish, created a profound sensation in the town on Monday. It is a long time since an event so saddening occurred in our midst and one could not conceive a more pathetic end to a clergyman's career. Lovingly devoted to his wife, the rev, gentleman was so grief-stricken at her death that he took away his own life. Those townspeople who knew him were deeply pained at the terrible end of one who al- ways looked the picture of good health and genial- ity,and the congregation of St. James's are to be sympathised with in the double loss which has befallen the parish.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>LOCAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Personal. Mr. James Houghton, chief clerk in the Borough Treasurer's department, has been admitted an associate of .the Institute of Municipal Treasurers and Accountants (Incorporated).</p>
        <p>You CANNOT DO BETTER.! Buy your boots and shoes at ALF. TAYLoR's. Cheapest and best town. The Election in Howley Ward. _ _ _ .</p>
        <p>Mr. Sankey has made a -declaration as to his ex- penses in the recent election in Howley Ward. From this it appears that his expenses were A:2O Os. sd.</p>
        <p>BEST BOOTS FOR ALL.—UNDER the CLOCK.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Musical Successes. The following successes have recently been won by pupils of the Misses:Williams„`The Priory School" :—• Trinity College, London, junior pianoforte playing, Mary Dorothy Hall. The Incorporated Society of Musicians, Grade IL, pianoforte playing, Dorothy Truslove and Clarice ewall. H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC is the KING OF TONICS.-63, SANKKY-STREET, Warrington.—Advt. Accidents to Children. Two accidents to children occurred yesrerday after- noon, May Warburton, aged two years, livino. in Golborne-street was admitted to the infirmary suffer- ing from severe burns about the body. A boy named John Gough, (6), Liverpool-road,is lying in the iustitu tion suffering from injuries to the face and heap sup- posed to have been caused by being run over. The Crnsfield Conservatory.</p>
        <p>Mrs. John Crosfield will visit Warrington on Tuesday next and open" the Crosfield Conservatory" which she has presented to the town.. The conserva- tory has been erected on the site of the old vinery in the Park and is a pleasing addition to the attractions of this public resort. The camellias which Mrs. Crosfield also presented to the town have been housed in the conservatory.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BENBFIT, USDER. the CLOCK I3BiT for</p>
        <p>BOOTS in LANCAHIRE.—Advt. Street Improvement _ near Bank Quay Station.</p>
        <p>A desirable street improvem9nt has been made near Bank Quay railway station by the removal of the buildings and wall adjoining the Patten Arms Hotel and the new Police Buildings. This has enabled the Corporation to remove a sharp corner and widen the road. The inadequate lighting of the road has also been remedied by the erection of three large lamps.</p>
        <p>H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC is the RING OF TONICS.-63, SANKEY-STREET, Warrington.—Advt. A GOOD RESOLUTEON.—Wear ALP TAYLOR'S Wonderful Boots.—Advt. Music in the Park. crosfield's Works Prize Band will occupy the stand on Sunday and will play the following programme of music : —March, "The Powerful," Carter; selection, "Gems of evergreen melodv,"Round ; recit,and chorus "Comfort ye, my people," „ "And the Glory of the Lord," Handel ; selection, " Cing Mars." Gounod ; air vane, " Rousseau's dream, Round ; hymn, "Evening," W. Halliwell ; "God save the King." Conductor, Mr, W. Halliwell, band-master Ist V.8.A1.R. Wigan Rifles. The band will also play selections in the Park on Wednesday next,commencing at 7.30 p.m.</p>
        <p>ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MLNF,RA. WATERS.—Advt. Wild Flowers in the Museum. The following plants, in flower, are included in the current exhibit, on the West staircase :—Celery-leaved buttercup, field poppy, shepherd's purse, hedge mustard, dyer's mignonette, ragged robin, St. John's-wort, corn spurry, meadowcranes-bill, holly, furze, tufted vetch, meadow pea, blackberry, silver weed,meadow sweet,lady's mantle,fr enchwillow purple loosestrife,water parsnip, gueldex rose feverfew, chamomile,stink mayweed, sneezewort yarrow yarrow, mugwort, wormwood,burdock, knapweed; cornflower, mouse-ear hawkweed, yellow goat's-beard, tansey, groundsel, ragwort, lia,rebell, moneywort, poor man's weather-glass, forget-me-not, figwort, foxglove, brooklime, ground-ivy, skull-cap, self-heal, betony, woundwort, white dead-nettle, red dead-nettle, wood- sage, bugle, ribwort plantain, la :ge-flowered hemp nettle, knotgrass. snakweed, water plantain.</p>
        <p>ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S lIIGH-CLASS ATINERAL WATERS.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>Another Attraction at the Park.</head>
        <p>Another Attraction at the Park.</p>
        <p>A very interesting addition has been made to the Town Hall gardens during the past week, through the generosity of MI . Henry Thornton. The addition takes the form of a chicken incubator with foster mother and run attached, and it has been housed between the aviary and the aquarium. It has been so constructed that the public can see the eggs inside the incubator, and when hatched the chickens will be seen in the accompanying foster mother and run. At the present moment there are 26 Buff Orpington eggs in the incubator, and it is anticipated that these will be hatched in the course of 21 days. A temperature of 104 degrees is main- tained by a lamp in which Kerosene oil is burnt. Immediately the chicks are hatched they will be placed into a dry box provided at the top of the incubator and afterwards placed in the run with its sanded floor and nicely-graded temperature. The idea has been got from a similar exhibition in a window in Regent-street, London. UNDER the CLOCK or GOOD BOOTS.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
