      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>Examiner Saturday, July, 25, 1903.</head>
        <p>The Examiner Saturday, July, 25, 1903.</p>
        <p>Warrington and District Agricultural Society. Intending exhibitors at the forthcoming show in connection with the Warrington and District Agricul- tural Society are reminded that the list of entries closes on Wednesday next.</p>
        <p>The Dead Pope. A solemn requiem mass will be sung at St. Mary's Church, Battermarket-street, on Tuesday morning for the repose of the soul of his Holiness, Pope Leo XIII. FIRE GRATES of every description.—.T. S. GAR- LAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street,Warringion.—Advt. • Licensing. At the Borough Police Court on Saturday, David Walsh was granted temporaay authority to sell at the Bewse2,,- Inn, Pitt-street, in lieu of John Worrall, who, it was stated, had suddenly left the town.</p>
        <p>BASSINETTES AND MAIT, CARTS in latest art shades.—J. S. G :ARLAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>The Late Councillor Baird's Will.</p>
        <p>The will of the late Councillor John Baird, brewer, who died at his residence, 661, Knutsford-road, on the 25th ult., has been proved. He has left property which has been valued by- the widow Mrs. Margaret Baird, at .4L2,590 12s. 101 gross and 32,512 16s. 10d. net.</p>
        <p>GIVE TEIEM x TREA.E.—ALP.T.tyLoR's wonder- ful boots for everybody. Co-operative Employees' Picnic.</p>
        <p>The employees of the Warrington Co-operative Society, together with their wives and friends, had their annual picnic on Tuesday, when Blackpool was visited. The party, numbering about 100, left Bank Quay Station at 8.35 and arrived at Blackpool at 10.50. For two hours- the weather was fine, but a heavy downpour of rain then commenced and continued almost incessantly for the whole of the day. The various entertainments were well patronized and the party reached home soon after eleven o'clock.</p>
        <p>Warrington P.S.A. There was a good attendance at the meeting of this Society held on Sunday in the Co-operative Hall, Sankev-street, under the presidency of Mr. Scragg. Mr. dreenhough sang " Comfort ye," and " The Sailor's Grave " very creditably. Mr. A. Mounfield gave an instructive address, basing his remarks on " Gymnastics " and comparing the presemt with the generations of the past. The quarterly prize distribu- tion took place at the close of the service, when upwards of 200 books were distributed to the mem- bers for regular attendance. FOR CLASS AND CRINA. of every description try JAS. GARLAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. Railway Servants Church Parade. In aid of the orphan fund of the Warrington Branch of the Amalgamated Society of Railway Servants special services were held on Sunday, in the afternoon at St. Paul's Church and in the evening in Friars' green Chapel, Cairo-street. The sermon at St. Paul's Church was preached by the Rev. W. Bracecamp, the vicar. A procession was formed at one o'clock in front of the Town Hall, and at 1.30, headed by the Borough Prize Brass Band and the branch banner, it proceeded through several streets to the church. The Vicar spoke about various matters of interest to rail- way men, and earnestly appealed for a large offertory in aid of the orphan fund. The response was a con- tribution of :F.,2 2s. sd.</p>
        <p>Poor Children's Summer Camp. Yesterday m )rning the first batch of boys from Warrington who have spent a week under canvas at Moreton returned home, and in the afternoon another contingent left to take their places. On the whole the weather during the week has not been very favour- able to camp life, but ,the lads have managed to extract plenty of enjoyment from the holiday. There have been organized walks nearly every day of the week.On Tuesday the boys visited Bidstowe:observatory and lighthouse,and as the afternoon was wet recreation was indulged in in the room provided for the purpose. During the night the rain felt so heavily that some of the tents had to be abandoned and the occupants slept in the recreation room. On Wednesday the youngsters walked to Leasome Castle and Wallasey where shells were collected and afterwards classified according to an instructive chart hanging iu the recreation room.</p>
        <p>The principal outing of the week took place on Thurs- day when the walk was to Hoylake, West Kirby and Ilibre Island. Two thunderstorms detracted some- what from the pleasure of the ramblers. Yesterday morning the boys rose early in order to clean up the camp for the new-corners. They arrived at Warring- ton about eleven o'clock and marched to the Drill Hall where prizes were distributed t ) the boys who have behaved the best in camp. In the afternoon a detach- ment of 83 poor boys departed for the camp. Like he first section they will be in charge of Mr. F. J. Ellison and a band of willing assistants. Mr. B. Grimishaw, of Warrington, has sent two sacks of potatoes for con- sumption in the camp next week.</p>
        <p>LADIES BEST GLACE BOOTS and SHOES, UNDER the CLOCK, BRID GE-STREET.—AdYt, Choir Trip. A very successful and enjoyable outing was that participated in by the members of St. Paul's choir and friends on Saturday. The party numbered about 40, and included the Vicar (Rev. W. Bracecamp), Mrs. Bracecamp, the Rev. T. W. Barber, and Mrs. Barber. Bowness-on Windermere was the destination, and the trippers arrived at Windermere shortly after eight lo'clock and walked to Bowness. Here the Stag's Head Hotel was made the headquarters for the day. About ten o'clock three char-a-bancs conveyed the party round by Strawberry Bank, a distance of about TO miles, stoppages being made here and there to allow of the gathering of wild strawberries and rasp- berries, which 'grew in great profusion. The hotel was reached again about one o'clock, and, an excellent hot dinner was partaken of. The afternoon was devo- ted to various forms of enjoyment, some boating, others fishing, whilst the major portion of the com- pany sailed up the lake as far as Ambleside on the s.s. Swift. Here a few paid a hurried visit to the famous Stock Glivll Falls, others visited the beautiful church, &amp;c., the return to Bow- ness being a,ccomplished in time for five o'clock tea. After tea .the members of the choir andl friends presented the Vicar and Mrs. Bracecamp each with a memento of the day. The former presentation took the shape of a bound volume, by Canon Rawnsley, and dealing with the Lake district, and was presented by Mr. E. Wilkes (the organist) in a neat speech. Miss Bate also presented Mrs. BraCecamp with ati afternoon tea cloth. The Vicar thanked all for their kindness on behalf of himself and wife. The return journey was commenced at 0.40, Warrington being reached shortly before ten o'clock. The whole of the arrangements were carried out by Mr. Charles Gelderd, who was heartily thanked for his services.</p>
        <p>was served at fresco,and was heartily enjoyed. After tea several speeches were delivered. Miss Bessie Dutton read the following report pre- pared by Mrs. O'Brien, who with Mrs. Clay represented the Association at the annual meeting of the Federation at Halifax :—Mrs. Clay and myself attended the annual meeting of the Federation at Halifax from Monday May 11th to Thursday May 14th inclusive and at the outset have to testify to the kindliness of the hospital-, ity we received. On Monday evening a reception was held in the Victoria Hall, when tie delegates were received by Mrs. Whitley, president and Mrs. Smith secretary of the Halifax Branch. On Tuesday the pro- ceedings proper commenced presided over by Lady Aberdeen, when the Mayor of Halifax was introduced, and delivered a brief speech of welcome to the meeting. Lady Aberdeen then delivered her presidential address dealing with the objects of the Federa- tion : To promote the principles of peace and justice, the breaking down of exclusive privileges and the development of all legislation that tends to uplift the masses and produce a purer patriot- ism and love of country. After the preliminary busi- ness of appointing sessional officers, receiving reports, Scc.,was completed. and London decided upon as next place of meeting, the principle of the right of Woman Suffrage, Home Rule, Welsh Disestablishment and the Reform of the House of Lords, also its hostility to the State Regulation of Vice, also the desirability in the Law of Divorce to place women on an equal footing to men were discussed. On Tuesday evening a reception was held when the guests were received by Lady Trevelyan. On Wednesday the first question taken was Clause 13, when Mrs. Sadd Brown moved a resolution condemning the Government's London Education Bill. Therelwas no discussion. All the following speeches were to the support of the resolution which was carried as were also resolutions in favour of Sunday Closing, Direct Veto and the local control by the people of the Liquor Traffic. The desirablility of giving better oppor- tunities for Women on Local dovernment Boards, and taking measures for the better Housing of the poor, Questions affecting and in favour of the Irish Land Bill, laundry legislation, overcrowding and its effects on the morals of the people, also the South African question of forced labour, and sugar conventions were exhaustively dealt with by various speak- ers. On Wednesday evening the annual public meeting was held in the Victoria Hall, and in the absence of. Lady Aberdeen who had been called to Holy- rood by command of the King, the chair was taken by Mrs. George Smith, of Halifax, addresses being de- livered by Earl Beauchamp, Mr. C. P. Trevelyan, M.P., Mr. J. H. Whitley, M.P., Mrs. Balmford Slack and others. Thursday, the concluding day of the Confer- ence, was used for considering questions of compulsory arbitration, the employment of children,the betting and ;gambling evils, also, which was probably one of the Imost interesting items, how to organize and utilize Women's Liberal Associations. In all the questions ' that arose your representatives voted on the same side ' and always on that side which would have commended itself to the Warrington Women's Liberal Association. Mr. C. E. PARKER said he was sure they all recog- nized what an excellent report of a very excellent congress they bad had read to them. After hearing that report and all the subjects which were discussed and settled at the meetings he was afraid the poor men would soon feel they were getting very much behind. (Laughter.) He was sure that speaking for the gentle- men present he could say that they were very much obliged to the ladies for their invitation to that gather- ing. They were very pleased to be present and they hoped and expected that in-all things men aid women would work together for the common wealth and the common weal. Some of them were present at a meet- ing of the Warrington Liberal Association the other evening and what impressed him about that meeting was its unanimity, cordiality, and enthusiasm. It almost seemed that after a time of cloud and dark- ness, and to some extent division, at last the Liberal party in Warrington was having a resurrection, and they trusted that now they had, more than perhaps they had had for a long time, great national issues to fight and great national interests to-safeguard, both the Liberal Association and the Women's Lthatral,Association would unite in making a strenuous effort, So far as Warring- ton was concerned,to puSh the principles which had been the foundation of Liberal policy for so many years to the front, and carry them to a successful issue. Mr. Parker went on to allude to the proposed Liberal bazaar of which Mrs. Armitage had kindly, along with himself, accepted the presidency. This bazaar had hung fire for many months but he did not think it had been through any lack of zeal or heartiness ' on the part of the ladies. At the meeting of the Liberal Association it was felt that they now ought to take some definite steps to forward this good work. If they were going to fight for Liberal principles and for the sending of a Liberal candidate to Parliament to represent Warrington, it was absolutely necessary that they should have some funds to fight with, and it was thoughtthat a bazaar in which all could co-operate and help to the extent of their ability was the best method of raising the money. Thereavere some who could not give much money, but they could give work and help to a bazaar. It was hoped that in the autumn of next .year a Lazaar could be held. They were dependent on the ladies in this matter. The gentlemen would do all they could, but after all, the arranging of 'it baziar was only athing that ladies could do. Mr. OsbOrne had the secretary- ship of the bazaar and had been very active in dis- charging the duties, and he (the speaker) wanted all of those present to do what they could to help forward that very useful and very necessary work. (Applause). Councillor PIERPOINT, who was introduced by the Chairman as the father of the Liberal party in .Warring- ton, said he was bound to admit that he had had a long and at the same time a very pleasant and intimate con- nection with the Liberal party in Warrington. He recollected it at the than when its funds were more than they well knew how to spend, and even at the time when he had to some extent the management of the party, he recollected it when it was in the depths of poverty. Then they appealed, as Mr. Parker had done, to the ladies, and they never appealed in vain, because at all times the ladies came forward with such a spirit of generosity that after asking them to help once, the officials asked them again many times. He was rather pleased that Mr. Parker had intimated the desirability of holding a bazaar. He did not look upon a bazaar en- tirely as a means of raising money but as a means of bringing the ladies of the party togpther. However, a political party if itiwas to be useful must have funds in hand not only to meet immediate requirements but to make provision for a rainy day. He hoped the ladies of Warrington would put their heads together—in fact he thought they would and if they would not he should be ashamed of them. (Laughter). Even in that little • gathering there. was a nucleus of an association which might be formed—and he felt sure would be formed—for the promotion of the bazaar. They had an excellent president in their good friend Mrs. Armitage—(Applause)—who would be supported to the very best of his ability by Mr. Parker—he would rather say by Mrs. Parker because after all the under- taking depended upon the ladies. He hoped sufficient money would be raised to relieve the minds of the workers of the party in Warrington and make them go strong to fight that great battle not only for the people of Warrington but for the great population of the country. (Applause.) It was many:years since he had the pleasure of accepting the hospitality of the War- rington Liberal ladies on the occasion of the annual picnic. He did not know that he had ever spent a pleasanter day, and he hoped they would afford him a similar opportunity on the occasion of the next picnic. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>Councillor TINNION expressed his gratification at the report which had been read and said the ladies had set a very good exampleto the male members of the party in preparing such an excellent thorough-going, and exhaustive report. In regard to the bazaar, Mrs. Holmes would be very glad to receive the names of any- one desirous of helping. Mr. Osborne sent out five hundred circulars some time ago and received a fair number of replies but he (the ppeaker) believed many ladies who did not reply would be willing to assist and Mrs. Holmes would be pleased if they would communicate with her. Mr. Osborne would also be pleased to receive offers of assistance. It was an absolute necessity that in fighting political battles in Warrington they should have some money. Liberalism Lad suffered in Warring- ton for want of funds for some ' time. They were getting in a little better feather now, but Liberals in their national policy did not believe in getting into debt and he was sure that in their local policy they did not believe in keeping in debt. He was sure the ladies would do all they could to raise funds by means of the bazaar, not only for election purposes but to enable the party to take that part which he regretted it had not already taken in the political education of the people of Warrington. If they would only work together t L the party worked together some years ago they would have a very successful bazaar. (Applause.) In conclu- slortl the speaker expressed the pleasure which itafforded him to be present and said they were much indebted to Mrs. Holmes, Mrs. Clay, Mrs. Walker and Mr. Holmes for organizing the picnic. (Applause.) Mrs. ARMITA GE expressed the hope that the annual meeting in October or November would be largely attended. She was sure it would be an earnest for the success of the bazaar if they had a good annual meet- ing, and the ladies wbo attended would also hear the reports and be enabled to take more interest in the work of the Association. The annual meeting was the time when they all ought to rally together even more than at a picnic. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>Mr. JACKSON HOLMES addressed the gathering, and said he had attended the annual picnics ever since their inception. It was justas much pleasure for hint to come that day as on the first occasion, and it was a great pleasure to Mrs. Holmes to do her best to organize that successful gathering. (Applause). Mr. OsuonvE expressed the hope that the ladies would do their best for the success of the bazaar, and that sewing parties would shortly be arranged. The visitors were then at liberty to spend an hour in rambling about the woods and it goes without saying that all thoroughly enjoyed themselves amid such beautiful surroundings. Shortly before seven o'clock the return drive was commenced, the route being through Overton and Frodsham. Warrington jwas reached with- out the slightest mishap at half past ten after an outing v;rhich everyone voted to have been an unqualified success.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>WARRINGTON WOMEN'S LIBERAL ASSOCIATION. _ Picnic to Delamere Forest.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON WOMEN'S LIBERAL ASSOCIATION. _ Picnic to Delamere Forest.</p>
        <p>Right in the heart, of picturesque Cheshire the members of the Warringtonyomen's Liberal Associationjourneyed for their armual picnic on Thursday afternoon. There are not many prettier places than Delamere Forest within easy access of Warrington and on all hands the selection of such a charming venue for the outing gave unbounded satisfaction. The picnic was successful in a measure' which must have been exceedingly gratifying and en- couraging to officials of the Association—indeed it is questionable whether there has ever been a more enjoy- able picnic since the annual outings were inaugurated There was only one cause for regret and that was that Mrs Jackson Holmes,the energetic seeretary,who had rendered the most valuable service in making the arrangements,was prevented by indisposition from attending. The party, which left the Liberal Club in conveyances supplied by Mr. It F. Greenough, shortly after noon, numbered about seventy. It included Mrs. Armitage (President of the Association), Mrs. Garnett, Mrs. Parker, Mrs. W. Pierpoint, and Miss Dutton (vice-presidents), ! Mrs. Walker (treasurer), Mrs. Clay, Mrs. P. Garnett ! and Mrs. Hill. There was a fair muster of mem- bers of the sterner sex, but as usual they were com- pletely outnumbered by the ladies. Those who attended included Mr. C. E. Parker (President of the Liberal , Association), Councillors Pierpoint and Tinnion, Mr. C. Jackson Holmes, and Mr. E. Osborne. When the start was made, the weather was everything that could be desired, the sun shining brilliantly from a blue and almost unclouded sky. During the afternoon, however, dark clouds gathered omin- ously and it was feareti that heavy rain might mar the enjoyment of the pasty. Happily, one slight shower at Delamere was the only downpour experienced and the drive each way was made under most favourable • conditions. The beauties' of the country could be and were fully appreciated. There was always some charming scene for the passengers to feast their eyes upon. Verdant fields backed by richly wooded slopes, lanes almost arched with spreading trees, hedge-rows bright with wild flowers—all lent beauty to the charming country in which one could. linger for hours and admire the works of nature. The outward journey was made via Stretton and Weaverham. Delamere Forest,wa.s reached in good time, amd a move was at once made in the direction cf Eddisbnry Lodge, where tea soon awaited the visitors. The meal</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>A RUNCORN ROBBERY.</p>
        <p>At Chester Assizes on Monday John Antrobus (51), labourer, and his wife, Elizabeth Autrobus, were found guilty of stealing £l5, the money of Annie Okell, at Runcorn on the 27th June. The male prisoner was sent to prison for six months and the female for three months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>LIVELY SCENE AT THE LOCKS.</head>
        <p>LIVELY SCENE AT THE LOCKS.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn Police Court on Thursday, David Ashley, a boat man, was charged with assaulting Henry Moores, a Ship Canal constable. Moores alleged that prisoner met him on the locks of the Bridgwater Canal, on Tues- day night at 11 o'clock, and struck him over the eye with a boat-hook, used disgusting language, and then kicked him on the leg, bringing him to the ground.— Prisoner was sentenced to a months' hard labour, with- out the option of a fine.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>RUNCORN FREE LrBRARY.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN FREE LrBRARY.</p>
        <p>The Librarian's report for the month is as follows:— Number of days open, 15; number of issues, 1,396; average dail3 issue, 93; details of issues—theology, 16; philosophy, two; science, 51; history, &amp;c.. 74; poetry, twelve; fiction, 1,077; miscellaneous, 101; French literature, four; music, seven; total, 1,347 •' increase on corresponding month of last year, 960. Reference department, 49; decrease, 49. Gross total, 1,396; increase, 47. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>WIDNES SUCCESS.</head>
        <p>A WIDNES SUCCESS.</p>
        <p>We understand that Miss Ethel Lea, a student at the Widnes Pupil Teachers' Centre, and pupil teacher at Warrington-road School, has been successful in passing the London matriculation examination before the com- pletion of her apprenticeship. Miss Lea has had a most successful career. She was bracketed with the top students in the recent King's scholarship list, and has gained certificates in science and art. In each year of her apprenticeship she has received a "Sutton-Timmis " prize for distinction in Scripture. She enters Univer- sity College, Aberystwyth, next term to conti.tue her professional studies. MERSEY MISSION TO SEAMEN, RUNCORN. In connection with the Runcorn Branch of the Mersey Mission to Seamen,the 28th anniversary was celebrated on Sunday. The Rev. E. S. Olive preached at the morning service. In the afternoon a children's service was held. The preacher was Captain Tattersall, of the Church Army, St. Mary's, Widnes. At the evening service the preacher was Mr. Thomas Williams, lay missionary, Garston. Mr. E. Robinson Roberts sang "When I survey the wondrous Cross." Special hymns and anthems were sung by the choir and children assisted at the evening service by Mr. C. Tonks' band.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>UNFILIAL SONS AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>UNFILIAL SONS AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>William Hughes and John Hughes,- brothers, living off Heath-road, Runcorn; were summoned at the Run- corn sessions on Monday to show Cause why they should not contribute towards the maintenance of their mother who was chargeable to the funds of the union. Mr. Frodsham, an officer of the union, said the woman received 3s. 6d. out relief. John Hughes was £l. 10s. in arrears and . William £2. They were chemical labourers and earned ,Cl. is. 6d. weekly. The first defendant pleaded that he had been put to considerable expense by his wife's illness. The second defendant said he earned fivepence an hour. He said it was no business of their's to pay anything. What did they pay poor rates for? An order for the payment of is. weekly and the costs, 10s. 6d., was made in each case. ANTI-VACCINATOR AND MAGISTRATE.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn Petty Sessions on Monday, Charles Hankers. 22, Albert-street, Runcorn, applied to Messrs. A. Thomas and J. Pritchard for a certificate exempting his son Charles from vaccination. He said heconsidered that vaccination would be injurious to the health of the child.—The Chairman (Mr. Thomas): Have you any reason to show why? Applicant hesitated and the Chairman said that if he had no reason he would not grant the certificate.—Applicant said he had hadanother child vaccinated, and it had been injurious to the child's health. It was revaccinated three times.—The Chair- man: Is the child unhealthy now ?—Applicant: It is not healthy and it is not unhealthy. It is betwixt and between.—The Chairman: Have you a doctor's certifi- cate?—Applicant: No.—The Chairman: Then I shall not grant the application.—Applicant: What's the good of the law then? This is a conscientious objec- tion. According to you I mustbe a doctor and give you all the technicalities of the child's condition.—The Chairman: You can do it through a doctor.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS At these sessions on Monday, before Messrs. A. Thomas (in the chair) and J. Pritchard, Dominic Sherlock, farm labourer, Sutton, was fined ss. for having been drunk and disorderly in Chester-road, Sutton, on July 12th. Sergeant Bennett proved the case.—John Sephton Ball, 4, Cross-street, Runcorn, against whom there were several previous convictions was fined lOs for a similar offence in Regent-street on the 11th. John Stunner was fined ss. for having been drunk and disorderly in Wilson's- row on the 12th. These cases were proved by P.C.'s Davies and Preston.—Thomas Smiddy, 47, Wellington- street, was fined ss. on the evidence of P.C. Brayford for wallowing his dog to be at large on the 11th without muzzle or collar.—Samuel Millington, 22, Jackson's-lane, was fined ss. for riding a bicycle without a lighted lamp between one and two o'clock on the morning of the 11th. He said that daylight was break- ing and he did not know the extinguishing time. P.C. Oakes said defendant had neither lamp nor bracket. Extinguishing time was three o'clock.—John Feeney, 28 Cooper-street was fined ss. for allowing the chimney of his dwelling-house to be on fire on the 11th. A police officer said there was very little fire in the grate, but the chimney was a mass of burning soot.—John Joseph Basnett, an old offender, was committed to gaol for a month for begging in Bridge-street on Saturday.</p>
        <p>ASSAULT ON A GIRL AT WIDNES. At Widnes Sessions, on Thursday, Alfred Higginson,a married man, was charged with assaulting Cecilia Dowling.—Prisoner pleaded guilty to a commonassault. —Cecilia Dowling said she was seventeen years of age, and lived at 4, Midwood-street. About eleven o'clock on Saturday night she was going on an errand along Frederick-street, and on nearing the corner of Dean- street a man accosted her from the back. He tried to assault her, but she screamed. She fell on the ground and screamed again, whereupon he ran away.—Police- Constable Davidson, who resides in the vicinity,hearing the screams, saW he prisoner running away. He gave chase and caught t him in an entry in Elliot-street. He was brought to the police station, but released on account of insufficient evidence. He was apprehended on a warrant on Wednesday evening.—Defendant stated, in defence, that he had made a mistake in'the girl, and he was sorry for what he had done.—The Chairman said he was almost inclined to send him to gaol, but, owing to his previous good character, he would impose a fine of 40s. and costs, or a month in default. WIDNES POLICE INSPECTOR SEVERELY HANDLED. . .</p>
        <p>At the Widnes Police Court, on Monday, before The Mayor (Alderman W. W. Gossage) and Mr. T. Smith, Patrick Murphy, Sutton's-lane, was charged with being drunk and refusing to quit the Norton Arms, and with assaulting Police-Inspector Strickland. His brother John was also charged with assaulting the same officer. —lnspector Strickland stated that at 7.45 p.m. on Saturday night he was in company with P.S. Blockhall inAnn-street when he heard a disturbance going on in the Norton Arms. Witness went in the back way and found the landlord endeavouring to eject Patrick Murphy, whose mouth was bleeding as there had been some disturbance. The prisoner was asked to leave quietly, but on refusing he had to be removed by force John Murphy, seeing his brother being ejected joined in the struggle which lasted ten minutes. The inspector was severely handled and some of his clothing was to rn After a while Patrick was taken to the lockup, John Murphy being apprehended later in the evening.— Sergeant Blockhall corrohorated.—Both prisoners asked to be leniently dealt with and stated that they had got elated over a "walk" in connection with a works and had over indulged.—Patrick Murphy was fined 2s. 6d. and costs for drunkenness and Cl and costs r one month's hard labour for the assault, and John o urphy was fined El and costs or one month for his Mare in the assault. w ill</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>ENGINEERS AT WIDNES.</head>
        <p>ENGINEERS AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>There was a gathering of engineers at Widnes and Runcorn on Saturday, for the Lancashire and Cheshire district meeting of the Incorporated Association of Municipal and County Engineers.—The Mayor of Widnes welcomed in the Council Chamber a very large number of members, not only from the counties named, but from Nbrth Wales, and even London. Mr. I. Carr, M. mat, C.E. dealt with "The Gas and Water Undertakings of Widnes." His paper has attracted much attention. The total number of gas con- sumers, he said, was 5,822, and of these 3,745 were con- sumers by "penny-in-the-slot meters." The selling price of gas within the borough was is. 4d. and is 2d., and for motive power purposes is. per 1,000 cubic feet. Consumers by "penny-in-the-slot" received 40 cubic feet for a penny. The illuminating power of the gas was 18 candles. The prices charged were lower than those of any other gas authority in the world. The gas for motive power purposes had a calorific value of 670 B.T.U:, and at is. per 1,000 feet was 25 per cent, cheaper than Mond gas at 3d. per 1.000. s Elec- tricity for power purposes to be comparable with the gas at the Widnes price must be sold at id. per unit. In Widnes public lighting is free. A paper on the Transporter Bridge contained many in- teresting facts. When completed the bridge would be the only one of its kind in England, and would have the longest span of any bridge in the United Kingdom designed for carrying road traffic, the clear space over the Mersey and the Ship Canal being I,oooft.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>ST HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST HELENS.</p>
        <p>KICKING AFFRAY AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>John Wynne. of 2 Vernon street, was charged on warrant at St Helens on Thu rserly with assaul ti ng Patrick Harvey on May 16th. Wynne pleaded guilty and said he struck Harvey because he assaulted his mother. Harvey said Wynne kicked him'severely.—Fined lOs or seven days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>VACANCY IN ST. JOHN'S WARD.</head>
        <p>THE VACANCY IN ST. JOHN'S WARD.</p>
        <p>Mr. Maginn's Candidature.</p>
        <p>The issue in St. John's Ward lies between Mr. Henry Thornton, the °trial A Non-Trading candidate, and Mr. John Maginn, who comes out as an independent. The electionwill take place on Thursdaynext and the polling station will be in the Old Club Room, John-street. It was at onetime thought there would be a three-cornered contest, but at a meeting of the Conservative Ward Committee on Monday evening, it was decided not to bring forwarda candidate. On Wednesday, nominations were handed in on behalf of Mr. Henry Thornton and Mr. John Maginn.</p>
        <p>Henry Thornton, gentleman, the Laurels Fearnhead, is proposed by Thomas Ellison, seconded by James A. Leslie, and supported by James Ratcliffe, Johu Delooze, Jonas Collins, John Millman, Thomas Millbank Pollard Roberts, William Massey, Joseph Unsworth, and William Amos. In a second paper Mr. Thornton is proposed by Thomas Pawney, seconded by John Acton, and supported by Thomas Murphy, George G. Plumpton, James Hill, John H. McMahon, George • Hargreaves, William Jolley, James Heacock, and James Cottam. in a third paper Mr. Thornton is proposed by John NV. Wilding, seconded by James McGuinnis, and supported by Richard A. Howard, Joseph Taylor, Peter Taylor, Thomas Clare, Charles Stewart, Daniel Savage, Ralph Poole, and Henry Cheeney. John Maginn, furniture manufacturer, 85, Bridge- street, is proposed by Thomas Mather, seconded by Alfred Hardy, and supported by Samuel Ball, James Bolton, Albert E. Smith, Thomas O'Day, William Dag- null, Samuel Ryan, George Newton; Frederick E. Dean. In a second paper Mr. Maginn is proposed by Hannah Vernon, seconded by Margaret Rhodes, and supported by Niary Ellen Oakes, Bridget Cunningham, Mary O'Donnell, Martha Ellen Highani. Maria Dugan, Mary Kenny, Alice Kelly, Catherine Walsh. Mr. Maginn has addressed several open-air meetings in support of his candidature during the week. On Tuesday he spoke on a piece of ground, off Birchen- street, on Wednesday in Sharp-street, and on Thursday at Cockhedge, near the Borax works. He addressed another open-air meeting last night. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>TUESDAtS MEETING.</head>
        <p>TUESDAtS MEETING.</p>
        <p>At the meeting on Tuesday, Mr. PETER, DELANEY, who presided, spoke of the services rendered to the town by the late Councillor Baird, and also of Mr. Afaginn's 25 years' career in the town. Mr. Maginn, he observed must have been a man of sound common sense and keen judgment to have got together the business he had in Warrington. He was not coming out as a nominee of any political party, neither was he a member of either o, them. Nor was he the nominee of the Non- trading party, although they had no better or sounder man in their ranks than he would he if he was returned at the poll.</p>
        <p>Mr. SIAGINN said he had thought it necessary to offer himself as their candidate to study their interests, and not the interests of some political clique. Mr. Thornton had been nominated by the Non-trading party. He (Mr. Maginn) was a Non-trader and had to fight the Warring- ton Corporation single-handed before the present crowd of Non-traders thought of • the matter. He objected to outside interferences with their own affairs, whether it was from Manchester or Dublin. (Hear,hear.) If they were not able to manage the affairs of the town it was time they lost their charter and were disfranchised. (Hear, hear.) He (Mr. Maginn) wished to avoid personal spleen and dispute between his opponent and himself. Referring to the wretched state of dwellings in the ward, there were some, he said, which were not tit to live in. The ratable valuation of St. John's Ward WM £27,204, and they paid annually in rates £9,974 Is. 2d. What r;turn did they get for that sum? What had they spent upon that ward during the last seven years? They knew that the answer was "Nothing." Again, they had spent on the tramway system £149,294, and up to the present they had not had one penny return in the shape of profit. They, in St. John's Ward, had appealed for a system of trams down Orford-lane and the district, but there was not yet the least attention paid to their claim. Surely they had had enough of cliques and parties going into their local parliament and having their hands tied. Practically all that he (Mr. Maginn) and his brothers possessed was at stake in Warrington, and, therefore, what was in the interests of the ratepayers was in his (Mr. Maginnts) interest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>MEETING ON WEDNESDAY EVENING. THE ASSESSNIENT OF PUBLIC HOUSE PROPERTY.</head>
        <p>MEETING ON WEDNESDAY EVENING. THE ASSESSNIENT OF PUBLIC HOUSE PROPERTY.</p>
        <p>Mr. MAGI'SN addressed a meeting of burgesses on Wednesday evening on a piece of ground near Sharp- street. It was originally intended to hold the meeting in the Mission Room, but this was found to be too small and an adjournment was made to the vacant piece of land near by. Mr. John Thompson presided, and in introducing Mr. Maginn, referred to him as a Non- Trader, and a working man's friend. Mr. JOHN MAGINN contended that the bulk of the ratepayers were being robbed daily by an unfair system of taxation.</p>
        <p>Mr. RODDY who interrupted the speaker, said he (Mr. Roddy) was a friend to Ireland and Mr. Magian was not.</p>
        <p>A VOICE : You are playing into his hands. Mr. MAOINN : I don't think you areal] black-guards (Hear, hear and Laughter.) Mr. Maginn proceeded to refer to the increase of rates which had taken place within recent years. Notwithstanding a steady increase in the rateable value of the town from £131,941 in 1884 to £243,455 in 1903, the rates had continued to go up. In 1884 the total rates were 4s. 10d. in the .2, whilst to-day they were 7s. 4d. in the £. Within the last two years the rates had been increased by is. Gd. in the 2 and that had been the period in which they had witnessed such disgraceful scenes in the Town Council. There had been two set parties in the Town Council like two dogs on two chains bow-wowing at each other. (Laughter.) If the burgesses were satisfied with the Town Council he was not. (Hear, hear.) He was now going to introduce a subject which had not been mentioned from a public platform in Warrington before. He referred to the assess- ment of licensed houses in Warrington. The number of fully-licensed houses in Warrington was 125 andhe contended that unjustifiable favouritism was shown in the assessment of some of these houses. Mr. Maginn proceeded to give particulars, and referred to houses individually. He pointed out that one licensed house in Bridge-street had a less rateable value than a bakery on the other side of the street, whereas the licensed house was taking six times more money. Mr. I Magi= proceeded to contrast the rateable value of, public-houses which were in the hands of the brewers with those in the possession of the Corporation, and showed that where the tenancy was open to free com- petitions, the rentals and consequently the assess- ments were higher than those of tied houses Mr. Maginn contended that if the licensed houses od Warrington were assessed at their proper value, a reducd ition in rates to the extent of threepence in the £ couln be made. Proceeding, Mr. Maginn said real harm lute been done to the town by the bad name which had been given to it by an outsider. This' was evidenced by thn difficulty which was now experienced by the Corporatioa in negotiating loans. Seven years ago, when the town had a decent name, the Corporation advertised for a loan of £186,000 and in three days it was subscribed ten times over. This year the Corporation wanted a loan of £70,000, but behold the differ- ence. It was advertised for a month, but only one application was received. That was for £lO,OOO and a commission had to be paid to an agent to get that. He did not accuse the Non-traders of this but he blamed the Corporation for spending the puoney when they knew the condition of affairs. Mr. Maginn criticised some of the expensive buildings which the Corporation had erected and said there were men in the Town Council who had not enough business aptitude to manage a small shop. (Laughter.) He was accused oi being a bad Irishman by one section of his countrymen and another section of ratepayers accused him of the heinous crime of being an Irishman. (Laughter.) He was between the devil and the deep sea. (Laughter.) He trusted to the commonsense of the ratepayers on Thursday next. (Applause.) In answer to a question as to whether he was a Non- trader, Mr. MAGINN said he had always been a non- trader. He had to fight the Corporation single-handed before the Non-traders came into existence in Warrine- ton. Whether elected to the Council or not he would support the party that opposed illicit trading as long as he had breath to do it. (Applause.) Mr. G. VERNON proposed 0. resolution of confidence in Mr. Maginn and 'alluded to Mr. Maginn'sstatement on the unjust taxation of business premises. Mr. HUGHES seconded and the vote was carried un- animously. MEETING ON THURSDAY EVENING.</p>
        <p>Mr. MA G INN addressAd another meeting near the Borax works on Thursday evening. Mr. S. RYAN occupied the chair. _ _ .</p>
        <p>The following question was put to Mr. Maginn "Would you, if elected, oppose the sale of intoxicating liquor onany premises in possessionof the corporation? Mr. MAGINN replied: No, certainly not. He had travelled the continent and found that in places where the publichouses were under the control of the corpora- tion there was less drunkenness. In his opinion all licensed houses should be under the control of the Corporation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>POACHER DROWNED AT RIXTON.</head>
        <p>A POACHER DROWNED AT RIXTON.</p>
        <p>Exciting Chase. Shortly after midnight on Monday last P.C. Clarke stationed at Rixton, received information that there were a number of poacher § on the Rixton estate. He proceeded to the place along with three gamekeepers in search of the men, and whnn they came across them one man named Wilson hit the constable on the hand with a stick entirely disabling the limb. The Man then made off and the constable followed. • Wilson forgetting the net he luui set, tripped over it and rolled down an embankment of the Mersey. H picked himself up .and made a dash into a little wood the constable in the meantime following close behind The man quickly turned round and leapt into the river, and being an expert swimmer made for the other side. The constable noticed him swimming and heard a noise in the water, but it suddenly ceased. • Inquiries were made for Wilson at Salford, where be resided, and it was then elicited that the man had not been home that night. On Thursday afternoon the body ' was pulled out of the river by some flatmen. The other men who took part in the affray escaped.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>WARRINGTON VICAR'S</head>
        <p>A WARRINGTON VICAR'S</p>
        <p>SUICIDE.</p>
        <p>HEARTBROKEN AT HIS WIFE'S</p>
        <p>DEATH.</p>
        <p>A Pathetic Message.</p>
        <p>St. James's Vicarage, Latchford, was on Monday the scene of one of the most painful and distressing tragedies which have .happened in this district for some time. Shortly after nine o'clock the parishioners, and in fact the townspeople generally, were shocked to learn that the Vicar, the Rev. Fred It. Pearson, M.A.,, had committed suicide by shooting himself with a revolver. Details of the sad story soon leaked out. In the early hours of Sunday the deceased clergyman's wife, to whom he was tenderly devoted, passed away after a lingering illness. It is only about ten months since Mr. Pearson took charge of St. James's parish. He came to this town from the pretty seaside village of Sewerby, near Bridlington, having exchangethbenefices with the Rev.F. E. Powell,M.A.,in the hope that the change of air would be beneficial to his wife. Unfortunately Mrs. Pearson derived no benefit from the ehange, and gradually becoming worse, expired on Sunday. To her husband, her death came .as a terrible blow and during the day he was much depressed. Before he retired to rest at night, his brother, Dr. R. S. Pearson, of London, who had come ta Warrington that day, gave him a tonic and on Monday morning he appeared to be in better spirits. It can readily be imagined with what alarm Mrs. .Ann Harrison (Mrs. Pearson's sister), who had also been summoned on the death of Mrs. Pearson, heard the report of a firearm in the house at about nine o'clock. She rushed upstairs and on entering the Vicar's bed- room was horrifiel to find that Mr. Pearson had shot himself. He was lying on the bed, bleeding from.. • wound in the right temple, and in his hand It, ~,rasped a revolver. Almost iminediately after the discovery, Dr. Pearson, who had been to Bank Quay Station to make arrangements for the funeral of Mrs. Pearson at Fleetwood, entered the Vicarage. He did all he could for his brother who, however, was beyond all hope of recovery, and breathed his last very shortly afterwards... The sad news soon gained currency in the town and created a painful sensation. The deceased clergyman was of robust build and cheery disposition and had made many friends. His tragic end caused surprise and grief especially in St.James's parish where he was such a familiar figure.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>INQUEST. PATHETIC LAST MESSAGE.</head>
        <p>THE INQUEST. PATHETIC LAST MESSAGE.</p>
        <p>The inquest was conducted at the Police Station on Monday evening by Mr. F. A. Jones, deputy coroner. The only witness called was</p>
        <p>Dr. Reginald S. Pearson who told the jury that he came to Warrington to make arrangements for the funeral of his brother's wife who died on. Sunday. He saw the deceased on Sunday even- ing and he seemed very much depressed. Before he retired to rest witness gave him a tonic. He seemed in good spirits—and in his usual health on rising at six o'clock the next morning. Shortly after eight o'clock witness had ocbasion to go to the railway station and he returned to • the Vicarage about nine. He was met by Mrs. Harrison and in consequence of whit she told him he went to his brother's bedroom. He found deceased lying on the bed with a wound in his right temple and grasping a revolver in his right hand. He expired almost immediately and witness sent for the police.</p>
        <p>In reply to the deputy-coroner, witness said his brother was for some time at Southport and he believed he knew Mr. Brighouse, the Coroner, personally. Inspector Taylor, who had charge of the case, pro- duced the revolver.</p>
        <p>The Deputy-Coroner read the following pathetic message which was written by the deceased gentleman ' on the back of a card announcing services at St. James's Church and was found on the bed:—" I cannot live without my darling. I have tried for 24 hours. I miss her loving ways too much. This is not the act of a. coward, but of a broken heart to whom life is a blank. It was Piggie and Lamb. With her I could live; for her I could work all day and all night. Those who know us most will be least surprised at this, for one of us could not live without the other.- Please lay me beside her, and find from her mother and others how- intense was her love fo r me. She loved me more than all the things in thd world. This was her expression, and those who know us know how we did love and will pray that God will forgive me one sin, for it is a sin, andallow me to meet her. Even if He does not, I can- not live in the world without her. Mr. Graham or his mother or anyone who knows us will give corroboration of this."</p>
        <p>The first portion of the message was written in ink and the latter portion which was not 80 distinctly written, in black lend. _ _</p>
        <p>Without hearing further evidence the jury returned a verdict of "Suicide whilst temporarily insane" and expressed their deepest sympathy with the bereaved relatives.</p>
        <p>THE DECEASED CLERGYMAN'S CAREER. The deceased clergyman who was 47 years of-age, was the son of the late Rev. James Pearson, M.A.,F.R.A.5., at one time vicar of Fleetwood. He was educated at Rossall, and Merton College, Oxford, and was ordained in 1880. His first curacy was at the Church of the Ascension, Broughton, Manchester. Subsequently ho was appointed to the 'curacy of St. Prideswide's, Ox- ford, and then he went to Holy Trinity, Southport. In 1884 he was appointed vicar of Fleetwood, and was afterwards for ten years Rector of St. Al bans,Manchester Prior to coming to Warrington he was for four years Vicar of Sewerby, near Bridlington and his departure for this town was much regretted by the parishioners. Mr. Pearson was a high churchman and maintained the advanced ritual which has been in use at St. James's for many years. He was a prominent Freemason, and was for a long time a member of the travellers' Lodge in Manchester. He was Past Provincial Chaplain for East Lancashire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>FUNERAL.</head>
        <p>THE FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>Amid manifestations of profound regret the remains of the late Rev. F. R. Pearson and Mrs. Pearson were laid to rest in the Cemetery at Fleetwood on Wednes- day. On Tuesday evening the bodies wereremoved from the Vicarage to St. James's church and remained in the chancel over night. At eight o'clock on Wednesday morning there was a celebration of the Holy filomm— union which was attended by a large number of parish- ioners who were anxious to pay a last tribut e of respect. The Rev. A. N. Taylor, of Chester, who until recently was curate of the Parish, was the celebrant. The cortege did not leave the church until shortly before ten o'clock and by this time many people had assembled in Wilderspool-road. There were two hearses, the remains of the late Vicar being conveyed in the first and those of his wife in the second. Blinds were lowered in the vicinity of the church as a rr_ark of respect to the deceased clergyman and his wife. On arrival at Bank Quay Station the coffins were removed to the train which was soon on its way to Fleetwood. A large concourse of sympathetic spectators witnessed the interment at Fleetwood emetery. The cortege was met at the railway station by the churchwardens and sidesmen of the Parish Church, and as the procession passed through the streets blinds were drawn everywhere. At the Parish Church a large congregation assembled. Here the first portion of the burial service was gone through by the Rev. E. J. Reeve, Vicar of Fleetwood, who was assisted by the Rev. J. Gogging, of Buxton. Afterwards the cortege wended its way to the cemetery, where the last. rises were performed by the Rev. J. Gogging. The principal mourners were Dr. Pearson, Miss Pearson, Mrs. Jones, and Dr. Collins, of Warrington. Several Masonic representatives were present. It had originally been intended to send two representatives of the Church Council of St. James's; Latchford. to the funeral, but. as the parishioners were afforded an opportunity of attending the special celebration of the Holy Com- munion, it WM not deemed necessary for church officers to be present at the obsequies. A beautiful floral cross Nvas forwarded by the members of the Church Cowden, in addition to many tokens from relations and friends.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON POLICE V. SAL FORD PoucE.—Played on the ground of the former off Thursday. Score:—</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON. SALFORD. Hollingsworth run out 8 Dransfield not out ...... 1G Bigginbottont b Ronan 0 Ronan c&amp; b Hayes ... 2 Whitby b Ronan 8 Dainteth run out Phillips h Farrer Hayes b Barnfield Newey b Dainteth 8 Moore run out Lester c&amp; b Farrel. Houghton not out l6 Sharpies b Hayes Evans b Dainteth Dennett c&amp; b Ronan 0 Hadfield b Houghton 0 Sankey b Ronan 0 Wright b Houghton ... 2 Extras 8 Extras</p>
        <p>Total Warrington Won tiwir match by exceptionally smart fielding.</p>
        <p>chapman's Public-House. The Monument public-house, Union-street, Borough, S.E., which attained an unenviable notoriety in conse- quence of a fire occurring there under suspicious cir- cumstances whilst occupied by Chapman, who was subsequently hanged for the murder of Maud Marsh, has now been demolished by the London County Coun- cil for the purpose of making improvements. It was; at this house that Chapman first engaged the unfor— tunate girl as barmaid.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
