      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>BENEFITS OF SCIENCE.</head>
        <p>THE BENEFITS OF SCIENCE.</p>
        <p>It is seldom that any discovery of science is such as will benefit all classes of people. Some of the greatest discoveries of the age, while they have proved directly beneficial to thousands, have also, at least for a time, injured other thousands. Even some of the best medi- cinal preparations, however much they may do to remove one clags of disease, or suit some constitutions, at the same time either deepen the hold of another disease, or leave it entirely untouched, or if they benefit one class of patients, they may injure another. Now the great need of the age is some scientifically arranged preparation which will cope effectually with • the prevalent diseases of this country, which will be certain to do good when fairly tried, which will be equally adapted to the needs of tke mer- chant and the workman, the professional man, and he who wins his bread by the sweat of his brow, the student, the clerk, the factory hand, the miner, and the roadside labourer. It should be, too, such a preparation a t contains no injurious-ingredients, and which may be tlkken with impunity by the weakly child, or the delicate lady, as well as by the stronger constituted man. Such a discovery would deserve to be called The Perfection of Medicinal Preparations, and would indeed be an Invaluable Boon to Suffering Humanity. Now this much needed boon has been found, has been tried, has been proved, and wherever proved, it has been found satisfactory. We refer to Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, the Vegetable Topic, 'which when once tried, has been always recommended, and has proved successful when all other medicine has failed to give rerief, and we may say further, that it has proved permanently beneficial, when other pre- parations at best only gave temporary relief. It is strongly recommended as The Best Remedy of the Age for Indigestion in its different forms, such as Sick. Headache, Pains in the Side, Giddiness, Loss of Appe- tite, also for Nervousness and Nervous Disorders, Sleep- lessness, Neuralgii, Low Spirits and all kinds of Weakness. It has often proved very beneficial to persons suffering from great Weakness either after an illness, long eonfinement to ill-ventilated rooms or any other cause. It strikes at the source of the Disease, removing the cause of the illness, and strengthens those parts as the system which have been weakeaed by it, and therefore most liable to colds and other ailments. Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters being purely vegetable, it isa Natural Tonic, and suited to all ages at all seasons of the year. It is everywhere acknowledged to be a powerful aid in restoring the body to a healthy state when deranged by disease. If you suspect that your health is beginning to fail, brace up your nerves, and fortify your constitution by taking now a course of this excellent Tonic, which is sold verywhere in bottles at 2s. 9d. and 4s. 6d. each, but would any difficulty be experienced in procuring it, the proprietors will forward same for the above prices. carriage free. Avoid Imitations. Whenpurchasing,see the name, "Gwilyre Evans," on Label,Stamp,and Bottle Sole Proprietors : Quinine Bitters Manufacturing Com- pany, Limited, Llanelly, South Wales.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>Minor Cases. Warrington Borough Police C9urt.</p>
        <p>MoNDlY.—Before Mr. Monks (presiding), Alderman Sutton and Smethurst, and Messrs. Birchall, Wright,</p>
        <p>and Thornton.</p>
        <p>WoMAN ASSAULTS A SERGBANT.—Catherine Doherty, of no fixed residence, was charged with havin,, been drunk and disorderly in Dolman's-lane on Saturday night, and with assaulting Sergeant Smith.—Sergeant Smith and P.C. Clarke saw the prisoner helplessly drunk. She was making use of obscene language, and they took her into custody. They had to obtain a cab to take her to the Police Station. When she was being put into the conveyance, she struck the sergeant a severe blow in the mouth.—Superintendent Monaghan said prisoner had b3enbefore the Court 40 times prey- iously.—She was sent to gaol for a month. THEFT FROM A SHOP.—A boy named Thomas Collins, of 22,. Ship-yard, was charged with steeling from the shop of Richard Roberts and Sons, Bridge-street, a pair of lady's boots of the value of us. 6d.—He pleaded guilty.—Superintendent Monaghan stated that the boots were placed on the shop counter at one o'clock on Friday and an hour later the prisoner and another i boycanr, n and asked for some empty boxes. The lady in the shop gave them some, and went into another room to get some more. During her absence the prisoner took the boots and left the premises. The matter was reported to the police and he (Superintendent Monaghan) and Sergeant Cheney visited Ship-yard on Sunday evening. They saw the prisoner's sister with pair of boots on which looked "too good for Ship-yard." They took her into the houte and found that they were the stolen boots.—Prisoner's mother said the lad brought the boots home.—The Chairman: Was it not a most injudicious thing for your assistant to leave the shop,Mr.Roberts?—Cotmcillor William Roberts said they often had boys and girls coming into the shop for empty boxes which they were glad to give away because there was a great danger of them taking fire. In their 50 years of business that was the first tim3 anything of that kind had occurred.—Mr. Monks: Still it would be a sudden temptation to the lad which he yielded to.— Collins was ordered to receive six strokes of the birch- rod. DISORDERLY BEHAVIOUR.—For making loud and nnsee-nly noises. Catherine Newton. 18, Old-road. and Jane Hogan, 4, Cambria-place, were each fined 2s. 6d. MAINTENANCE.—George Peplow, 45, Tanners-lane was summoned for being in arrears to the amount of £2 10s. for the mainteaance of his step-son James Burns, at North Lancashire Industrial School, and also to the extent of £2 15s. in respect of Walter Burns. He was ordered to pay the money or go to gaol for two months. —John Barnes, Cambria-place, who was summoned for arrears in respect of his son at the Liverpool Refugs, to the extent of £6 55., was ordered to pay Is. a week and 6d. a week towards the arrears.</p>
        <p>TUESDLY.—Before Aldermen Sutton (in the chair) and Hutchinson, Mr.. J. R. young and Dr. For.</p>
        <p>Frownvo.—For fighting in Thirsemarket-street on July 4th, Sarah Ann Baguley, Naylor-street, was bound over in the sum of .£5 to keep the peace for six months. A COWARDLY SON.—Michael Rabbit, Albert-street, was charged with assaulting his aged father, Peter Rabbit, on July 18th.—Prisoner was making a noise downstairs' and when requested to stop, went upstairs and struck his father violently in the face.—Alderman Sutton said the assault vasl cowardly one and prisoner would have to go to gaol fof a month. A NEGLIGENT Foram—Frederick Davies. 18, Victoria-street, was sentsnced to a month's imprison- menkfor neglecting liis wife and three children, whereby they'bec.ame chargeable to the common funds of the Union.—Mr. Shearer, inquiry officer to the Guardians, said the three children were all very young, the eldest being.only three years old. .They were admitted to the Workhouse in March, since which time prisoner had done nothing to support them. He took drink and did little work. The Guardians had thirty or forty cases of the stung kind in the Workhouse.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.—Before Mr. Birchall (presiding) and Alderman Hutchinson. MAGISTRATES AND YOUTHFUL SMOKERS.—A small 'boy named Thomas Bibby, who resides in Scott-street, was charged with stealing from the shop of Charles Booth, 5, Wilderspool-road, a quantity of cigarettes of the value of 'Thd. Pris-mer took the cigarettes from behind the counter.—The boy's father promised to thrash him, and he was thereupon dismissed.—The Chairman said the Bench thought it was desirable that there should be some legislation to prevent young children from buying and smoking cigarettes. It was a crying evil and the sooner it was dealt with the better.</p>
        <p>FRlDAY.—Before Mr. H. Roberts (presiding),Councillor</p>
        <p>John Roberts, Messrs. Thornton and Young. ASSAULT.—Mary lllKenny, 2, Bakehouse-yard, was summoned for assaulting Ann Noon. 2, Litherland's-yard, on Monday last.—Defendant stated that she struck complainant in self-defence and produced a piece of hair which she alleged complainaxt pulled from her head. —Complainant denied this.—Erefendant was ftned in the costs (11s.) ALLEGED WIFE DESERTION.—WiIIitem Elliott Hynd- ham. wal summoned for deserting his wife Ethel Hvndham, 20 St. Peter's olace.—Complainant said defendant left her on Saturday morning and did not return until Monday afternoon. He gave her no money when leaving.—Defendant said he left town to seek work.—The case was dismissed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>" WHA DAUR MEDDLE WI ME.</head>
        <p>" WHA DAUR MEDDLE WI ME.</p>
        <p>BY SIR WILLIAM ALLAN, M.P Whit dattr meddle wi' me ?—A Scot ! fecht onv dizzen an' send them—to pot. Ma kintra, ye ken, Ts famous for men Wha aye ga'e mair blows than they got—l wot, Wha dear meddle wi me ?—a chiel Wha never was fleyt for a man—or a doll? For fechtin' rm fain, An' hand aye my am. Wi' a heart an' a han' o' steer,—just feel. ,</p>
        <p>Whs laur meddle wi' me ?—tak' tent, A Sco'Lsman's prood spirit can never be bent. Tie's thrawn an' he's dour, An' croons he will door, Tie nesor for a coward was meant,—that's Kent. Wha daur meddle Nvi' me ?—Come a' ! Our faithers frae foemen wad ne'er rin awa' We think on their deeds, An' baud up our heids, • For Scots hae nae equals ava--Hurrah</p>
        <p>" Pall Mall."</p>
        <p>Two brothers named, John William Eavey. were out shooting at Great Maplestead, near Holstead, when the trigger of John's gun came in contact with a twig, and one of the barrels was discharged. The shot entered William's neck andhe fell back dead.</p>
      </div>
