      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>FORESHADOWINGS OF DANGER.</head>
        <p>FORESHADOWINGS OF DANGER.</p>
        <p>It is said that the late General Wrauchope had a premonition that he would never return from the Boer war.</p>
        <p>An American physician was told one night by a friend while at dinner that he was sure President Lincoln had been murdered. Within an hour the news of the assassin- ation was flashed into the town.</p>
        <p>George Henry Fletcher, a Great Bridge (Stafford- 'shire) boilerman, had 'a constant fear that he had over- looked something which would result in a boiler explosion. This presentiment was, however, due to liver complaint and constipation Charles Fordo's bile beans, have now restored him to complete health, and the other day ho told a "Birmingham Echo" re- porter some particulars of his case. "Until about three years ago," said he," I enjoyed tolerably good health. I then bogan to suffer from liver disorder, which brought on acute constipation. So bad was the latter, that when the bowels did act it was so painful that I was thrown into a violent sweat, and quite exhassted for some time afterwards. The weakness caused by all this,soon began to affect my nerves, and at all sorts of times, whether sitting in the house or walk- ing in the street, I became subject to sudden dizziness which bore me down until I felt as though I were dying. To the nervousness on this account was added a. constant dread that I had foritL" something or overlooked somethino. in connection with the boilers, and that some terrible dis- aster might happen. I tried scores of medicines but all in vain.</p>
        <p>I was in a very low state indeed, when, a few months ago, I picked up a London paper and read a statement about the qualities of bile beans. I made up my mind tb see if they would do me any good, and before I had used one box, the effect astonished me! T foundl could eat a good hearty meal again and enjoy it. My confid- ence increased as I persevered with the treatment, audl was soon able to smile at the nervous fears and terrible presentiments that had troubled me. The beans seemed to correct the liver trouble imme- diately, and the other evils which really arose from that then disappeared. I an now quite cured, am in splendid health, and look upon my former sufferings and fears as one looks at something in the far distant past." Bile Beans are obtainable from all medicine vendors, at one-and-three halfpence or two-and-nine per box, but you are warned not to accept worthless substitutes, whieh may even prove harmful.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, July 25, 1903.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, July 25, 1903.</p>
        <p>Dr. Fox seconded the vote of thanks. At these meet- ings,he said,they always got some information that they (lid not possess before. The lucid explanations that the Chairman gavelof the various accounts and the general state of affairs,&amp;c.,were certainly sufficient inducements, at any rate for those residing in the immediate vicinity, to come to Warrington andhear them. (Hear, hear.) The vote was carried unanimously. in acknowledging the compliment,the °HAMM A.N said it was always a great pleasure to come to Warrington and he felt more than he could express, the warmth of the appreciation which had been expressed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>PARR'S BANK, LIMITED.</head>
        <p>PARR'S BANK, LIMITED.</p>
        <p>MEETING AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Mr. Parr on the Industrial Situatiod.</p>
        <p>MR. CHAMBERLAIN'S TARIFF PROPOSALS.</p>
        <p>At noon on Thursday, the half-yearly meeting of Parr's Bank, Limited, was held at the bank premises, Winwick-street, Warrington. There was a repre- sentative attendance of directors and shareholders. The proceedings were presided over by Mr. Cecil F. Parr (chairman of directors).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>CHAIRMAN'S STATEMENT,</head>
        <p>THE CHAIRMAN'S STATEMENT,</p>
        <p>The CHAIR-VAN moved that the report and balance sheet be 'adopted and that a dividend at the rate of 19 per cent. per annum be declared for the half-yeay ended 30th June, 1903, payable free of income tax, on and after Ist August. He observed that after a somewhat uneventful six months as regards the financial and industrial world, they were met once more in that well-known room to declare their usual dividend. Some of them might have noticed that the name of Mr. Nix, the deputy ehairman,appeared at the foot of the report instead of his (the Chairman's.) He must confess he had been takingrather a long holiday in South Africa and he only returned on Saturday 'last. As they would see, the report was perhaps stronger than usual and it only showed that the bank got on quite as well, if not better without him. (Laugh- ter and "No, no.") He did not come back proposing to open any, branches in South Africa, though from all he heard there was plenty of money to be borrowed and one bank was almost offered to him if they liked to acquire it straight away. He did not, however, propose to say anything further on that point to-day. The conditions which had prevailed this half-year had been most favourable for bankers. The Bank of England rate was maintained for the greater part of the half-year at 4 per cent., the whole average being £3 17s. 2d., which, of course, was a rate which bankers considered conducive to good profits. They got these profits pro- vided the dmount of bad debts was small and he was glad to toll them that once again their bad debts, con- sidering the business done, were very small in amount.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>INDUSTRIAL SITUATION.</head>
        <p>THE INDUSTRIAL SITUATION.</p>
        <p>The Industrial situation in this country continued to be in a measureanomalous. Trade could not certainly be called brisk for neither colleries nor mill g were working full time and some coal masters were com- plaining that they could hardly earn a living profit. Still, if they were to judge from statistics, trade gener- ally was increasing. The report of the traffic returns on our railways showed that they were greater than they were last year awl the statistics published by the Board of Trade showed us that our exports for this half year had been greater by over £10,000.000 than they were in the corresponding half of last year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>COTTON TRADE.</head>
        <p>THE COTTON TRADE.</p>
        <p>In the cotton trade the high price of raw material which in some measure was due to the manipulations of New York speculators and of course in measure also due to the shortness of the crop, had injuriously affected the consumption over here in our mills and therefore had injuriously affected the profits as well. The scarcity or the abundance of the raw material hitherto had been mainly a question of a large or small crop in America. Most of it,whatever the amolmt,used to find its way to the Lancashire mills, but of late years Americahad been working up her own cotton in her own mills more and more in ever increasing quan- tities till to-day she was actually using more of her cottonat home than she was sending to us. This was a serious question and it became more and more incumbent upon us to find, if possible, new fields for the growing of the raw material. If we could find these new fields in our colonies so much the better. (Hear, hear.) He was glad to say influential men were not only discuss- ing but trying experiments in some of our colonies already in3cotton growing and if they were successful he hoped their success might ultimately render them to a greater extent less dependent upon the American supply. Amongst others he might mention that experiments were being tried under the encourage meat and the sanction of Sir Alfred Jones, a gentleman known he daresay to some of thosepresent, and well-known as already having done so much to pro- mote the trade interests of West Africa and the West Indies. He might say also, having just come from Natal, that he knew experiments were being made there as to the possibilities of cotton growing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>SITUATION IN AMERICA.</head>
        <p>THE SITUATION IN AMERICA.</p>
        <p>Perhaps morelanomalous than our own position was the situation at present existing in America, There it would appear the banks had lent so much money to speculators and " bosses " that they had not enough now for the legitimate trade requirements of the cora- munity,and they had the curious spectacle of banks call- ing in their commitments at the same time that trade was expanding„and of railways actually earning increased dividends at the same time that the shares of those railways were falling heavily in value. MR. CHAMBERLAIN'S :FISCAL PROPOSITIONS. It was impossible for even bankers, impartial as they, are and ought to be, to hold themselves aloof and dis- interested from the controversy which was now beginning to divide the nation on the subject of Mr. Chamberlain's fiscal propositions. He did not intend to discuss those proposals there, but he could not say he admired the attitude of those who resolutely shut their ears to any discussion on the subject. It seemed to him to argue a somewhat timid faith in their own convictions, but altogether apart from the main question as to whether thocost of living would be raised or not, he should like to remind his countrymen that after all man does not live by bread alone. There was something higher than a love of bread And that was a love of country. He believed that the workingman, if he cheerfully paid and submitted to pay two shillings extra on corn for the good of the Empire during the late war, he would be quite ready to continue to pay it and possibly more if—and that was a very large if—it was made quite clear to him that the exigencies of the united Empire required it. Whether they were fair traders or free traders he would say, examine for yourself the question. If it be wrong in principle then a rigid examination would bring it to naught but if it be sound then let them beware of ignorantly opposing it, lest haply they founq themselves fighting against the well-being of the country. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>POSITION OF THE BANK.</head>
        <p>THE POSITION OF THE BANK.</p>
        <p>The balance sheet of the bank showed even more than its customary strength. Compared with the figures of a year ago, the money lodged showed the sub- stantial satisfactory increase of over a million, the figures being £27,300,000 as against £26,200,000. Then if they took the cash on hand and money at call and short notice, altogether they came to over £10,000,000 as against £8.000,000 in the corresponding period of last year, and if to these they added the investments it made a total of £13,000,000 as against £11,000,000. That of course was a very strong position. (Hear, hear.) On the other hand the loans and advances, includ- ing bills discounted to the extent of £2,200,000 were fully a million less than last year, being £17,000,000 as against £18„000,000. Whilst. they were really stonger than ever he had almost said unnecessarily stronger—it was a fault on the right side if it was a fault at all. They were placing as would be seen from the balance sheet £20,000 to their property account and they were also asking the shareholders to place a further sum of £5,000 to the officers' pension and provident fund. This was a new fund which they started last December. Most of the large banks had their own officers' fund and he believed it was quite right that they should have theirown fund. (Hear, hear) Amalga- mations, as they would see from the papers, continued to be the order of the dal, amongst banks. At present they were resting on then oars but should any sound business be suggested to them they were ready, as always to favourably entertain any proposal that might be made to them for amalgamation on fair and reason- able terms. (Applause). Sir DAVID GAMBLE seconded the adoption, of the re- port which was unanimously agreed to. THE DIRECTORS AND OFFICIALS THANKED. Dr. WHITE moved a vote of thanks to the directors for their services. He was sure the directors had the confidence of all the shareholders and the latter were very much obliged to them for their services. Mr. HENRY GREENALL seconded the proposition and said that the report' spoke for itself. They were al/ indebted to the directors for the way they had carried on the business of that large banking institution. The vote was carried unanimously and the Chairman briefly acknowledged the compliment. _ _ _</p>
        <p>- Mr: A. CREWDSON proposed a vote of thanks to the general manager, the assistant general manager,the man- ger's secretary,andithe other officers of the bank for their services during the past half year. Anyone who had read the report and seen the steady progress of the bank, he said, could not fail to see that the management must have been very good. As one who came in daily touch with the correspondence of the head office he could say that the very greatest care and attention was bestowed upon every detail of the bank. Captain WITHINGTON seconded the vote of thanks and it was carried unanimously. Mr. WHALLEY, in responding on behalf of the staff, spoke of the excellent assistance he received from every member of it. In regard to the branch managers they were able and experienced bankers. Speaking of the staff as a whole he might say they were an efficient and loyal staff. (Hear, hear.) THEICHAIRMAN'S SERVICES.</p>
        <p>Mr. JOHN P.A.KEMAN proposed a vote of thanks to the Chairman. He observed that they were all thankful to see him safe back home. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Parr had had a pleasant duty to discharge that morning and he had discharged it in an exceedingly pleasant manner. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>FATALITY AT STOCKTON HEATH. A Dangerous Road.</head>
        <p>FATALITY AT STOCKTON HEATH. A Dangerous Road.</p>
        <p>A shocking fatality occurred at Stockton Heath on Monday evening. A potato cart in charge of Thomas Lathom, of Garswood, was being driven in the three- Lion of Warrington, when the horse took fright at a traction-engine and plunged on to the footpath. Two boys named Fred Dudley, seven year of age, 21, Gaskell- street, and Walter Maddock, six years of age, happened to be standing there and Dudley was knocked down and sustained severe injuries. He was removed to the residence of Dr. Parker but expired shortly afterwards. Maddock escaped with an injured foot. Mr. J. H. Fisher, deputy coroner, conducted the inquest at the Mulberry Tree Inn, on Wednesday. After evidence of identification had been given by the father of the deceased, James Powel, 22 and 24. James-street, Warrington, said he was walking over the bridge on Monday after- noon, about half-past five, when he saw a traction engine with three empty lurries coining towards Warrington. The driver of the engine had slowed up " for a horse and cart to go across the road. Before the engine had Tot up its speed witness saw a potato cart drawn by a cob coming from Stockton Heath. When it got near the engine the cob suddenly jumped to the right. Three little boys were on the crossing,and one was knocked down by the near fore foot of the horse, and the wheel passed over his body. There was no time for the driver of the engine to be signalled, and I the driver of the cart was only going at a walking pace. After the wheel had passed over him the little boy got up and ran about four yards, and then dropped. Thomas Lathom, Garswood, greengrocer, the driver of the cart, said he had no time to pull up when his horse sprang aside. Joseph Holiday, the driver of the engine, having given evidence Mr. Whitley, of the jury, said this was the sixth or seventh fatal accident he knew of from London Bridge to Twenty-step Bridge, and he attributed this to the narrowness of the road.</p>
        <p>A verdict of" Accidental death" was returned, and a vote of condolence was passed with the parents.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Lymm Congregational Church. The Sunday school anniversary at the above church was keld on Sunday last, when the Rev. Bertram Smith of Leeds preached special sermons. In the afternoon a flower Service was held the flowers being afterwards conveyed to the Warrington Infirmary.Goodcongregat ions attended each service and the collections amounted to over £3O. \.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>Wycliffe Church.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe Church.</p>
        <p>The sermon of the Rev. John Yonge at Wycliffe Con- gregational Churah on Sunday evening was specially directed to young men, of whom there were many pre- sent. His subject was God's call to attention, and he based his discourse on the text "Son of man stand upon thy feet, and I will speak unto thee." He drew many pointed lessons from God's call to Ezekiel as applicable to the young men of to-day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>The meeting of this Society on Sunday was an open one, the members being allowed to bring their wives and lady friends. There was a !aro attendance. Mrs. Simpson presided and in the course -of a brief address she said they must be on the side of Christ if they were to be happy.—Mrs. Moores also delivered an address based on the text "Seek ye first the kingdom of God and His righteousness and all these things shall be added unto you." She referred to the many ways in which men sought to obtain what they called "life," but pointed out that there was no satisfaction to be got out of earthly things.—The Firth Male Voice Choir very efficiently sang two pieces entitled "Perfect in Christ " and" In Absence,"—The President (Rev. John Yonge) made a presentation of a fountain pen to Mr. Burton, the hon. secretary of the Savings Club, on his leaving Warrington.—Mr. Burton suitably acknowledged the gift.—A collection was taken on behalf of tlie Society's funds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>LOCAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Licensing. At the Borough Police Court on Saturday, David Walsh was granted temporary authority to sell at the Bewsey Inn, Pitt-street, in lieu of John Worrall, who, it was stateol, had suddenly left the town.</p>
        <p>Railway Servants Church Parade.' In aid of the orphan fund of the Warrington Branch of the Amalgamated Society of Railway Servants special services were held on Sunday, in the afternoon at St. Paul's Church and in the evening in Friars' green Chapel, Cairo-street. The sermon at St. Paul's Church was preached by the Rev. W. Bracecamp, the vicar. A procession was formed at one o'clock in front of the Town Hall, and at 1.30, headed by the Borough Prize Brass Band and the branch .banner, it proceeded through several streets to the church. The Vicar spoke about various matters of interest to rail- way men, and earnestly appealed for a large offertory, in aid of the orphan fund. The response was a con- tribution of V 2s. sd. The Late Councillor Baird's Will.</p>
        <p>The will of the late Councillor John Baird, brewer, who died at his residence, 664, Knutsford-road, on the 25th tilt., has been proved. He has left property which has been valued by the widow Mrs. Margaret Baird, at £2,590 12s. 101 gross and £2,512'1.65. 10d. net.</p>
        <p>Warrington P.S.A. There was a good attendance at the meeting of this Society held on Sunday in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street, under the presidency of Mr. Scragg. Mr. Greenhough Sang " Comfort ye," and "The Sailor's Grave ' very creditably. Mr. A. Mounfield gave an instructive address, basing his remarks on "Gymnastics" and comparing the presest with the generations of the past. The quarterly prize distribu- tion took place at the close of the service, when upwards of 200 books were distributed to the mem- bers for regular attendance.</p>
        <p>Fire. Information was received at the Fire Station at about half-past four on Monday afternoon that a fire had broken out at the wood works of Mr. Eli James, , situated on some open ground behind Cumberland- street, Knutsford-road. On arrival, however, the brigade, which turned out with the steam engine, ! under the command of Superintendent Turner, found that their services were not required. Some boys had been playing at the back of the premises and had , ignited some wood. The flames, however, were ' speedily extinguished by a few neighbours, and very little' damage was done. Choir Trip. A very successful and enjoyable outing was that participated in by the members of St. Paul's choir and friends on Saturday. The party numbered about 40, and included the Vicar (Rev. W. Bracecamp), Mrs. I Bracecamp, the Rev. T. W. Barber, and Mrs. Barber. Bowness-on Windermere was the destination, and the ' trippers arrived at Windermere shortly after eight I o'clock and walked to Bowness. Here the Stag's IHead Hotel was made the headquarters for the day. About ten o'clock three char-a-bancs conveyed the i party round by Strawberry Bank, a distance of about 116 miles, stoppages being made here and there to allow of the gathering iof wild strawberries and rasp- berries, which 'grew n great profusion. The hotel : was reached again about one o'clock, and, an excellent hot dinner was partaken of. The afternoon was devo- ted to various forms of enjoyment, some boating, others fishing, whilst the major portion of the corn- ! pany sailed' up the lake as far as Ambleside .on the s.S. Swift: Here a few paid a hurried i visit to the famous Stock Ghyll Falls, others I visited the beautiful church, &amp;C., the return to Bow- ness being accomplished in time for five o'clock tea: lAfter tea the members of the choir and; friends presented the Vicar and Mrs. Bracecamp each with a m,pmento of the day. The former presentation to )k the slaape of a bound volume, by Canon Rawnsley, and dealing with the Lake district, and was presented by Mr. E. Wilkes (the organist) in a neat speech. I • Miss Bate also presented -Mrs. Bracecamp with an afternoon tea cloth.* The Vicar thanked all for their kindness on behalf of himself and wife. The return' journey was commenced at 6.40, Warrington being reached shortly before ten o'clock. The whole of the arrangements were carried out by Mr. Charles Gelderd, who was heartily thanked for his services.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>LIFE 1743T11NZ1:.: EXIRAORDINARY.</head>
        <p>LIFE 1743T11NZ1:.: EXIRAORDINARY.</p>
        <p>Twenty-five Policies on one Man.</p>
        <p>Pierpoint Morgan is said to be insured for a million sterling. A busy Welsh commission agent's Life Insurance attracted attention from another reason. He had been insured by variaus people, to whom his life was important, in no less than twenty-five different policies, and the Companies began to grow uneasy, for it looked as if they would have to pay up. Mr. Evans found time to tell the story at his home, Tanygraig, Morriston, Glamorganshire. "I used," said he, "to stiffer terribly from an en- larged liver and general debility, and owe my present excellentlealth to Dr. Williams' pink pills for pale people. At one time I was so ill that the doctors were expecting my death daily. The illness was very severe and of course I had to give up all work. In twelve months my weight was reduced from fifteen stone to nine stone. When I read in a paper of the remarkable cure effected by Dr. Williams' pink pills for pale people in one who had suffered as I was suffering, I decided to give the pills a trial. _ _</p>
        <p>"The effect they had on me was wonderful. I began to pick up immediately. After the third dose I felt considerably braced up and by the time I had finished the third box I had put on so much flesh that I almost looked my former self. Dr. Williams' pink pills un- doubtedly saved my life." There have been few more interestinr, cures in all the cases of anemia consumption, fits, interesting diseases, paralysis, women's ailmmts, and other diseases which Dr. Williams' pink pills have cured. Those who have experienced these benefits generally show willingness to give fellow sufferers the benefit of their experience. Mr. Evans said: "Oat of sheer gratitude I give per- mission for the facts of my case to be published. Some who read the story may be in need. I hope they'll follow my example, and take whit proved of suchin- calculable benefit to me."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>-MESSRS. GROSFIELD'S FIRE BRIGADE.</head>
        <p>-MESSRS. GROSFIELD'S FIRE BRIGADE.</p>
        <p>Interesting Competition.</p>
        <p>The works of Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons Limited were the scene on Saturday of an interesting competition in connection with the works' fire brigade Few Warrington firms have meade such surprising progress or shown a greater desire to keep pace with modern requirements than Messrs. Crosfield's, at Bank Quay. The most casual observer cannot fail to have noticed the great extensions which have taken place within recent years. Buildings are still in course of erection and at the present rate of progress, Messrs. Crosfield's will ere long have the largest works u the town. The success of tlae firm has contributed largely to the commercial prosperity of Warrington and all well-wishers of the town will hope for a continuance of the firm's prosperity. A recent outcome of Messrs. Crosfield's enterprise has been the organization on modern lines of an eEficient fire brigade, and the competition which was held on Saturday was proof of the careful training of the brigade and the adaptibility of the workmen in manipu- lating the steam fire engines and fire-extinguishing apparatus. The proceedings were held at the back of the genera/ offices and the new warehouse, and there was a large attendance of the employ4sand their friends and a number of javited guests. Representatives were present of the Warrington Fire Brigade and the VVilders- pool Fire Brigade, and also of the following works : .Messrs. Armitage and Rigby, Monks, Hall and Com- pany, Whitecross Company, St. Helens Cable Coin. any, Richmond and Company, Fletcher, Russell and Company and several tanning finals. Amongst others present were Dr. Markel (managing director), Captain Crosfield, Major Roome, Dr. Adams, Alderman Smethurst (Chairman of the Fire Engine Council), Councillor Peacock (deputy chairman), and Mr. Thomas, Longdin (boro engineer), Mr. Drinkwater, and many of theheads of departments at Messrs Crosfield's works The fire brigade consists of the Captain (Mr. Gallon), the sub-captain (Mr. Warburton) two lieutenants, a station officer, two policemen, a night watchman a night patrol man and 31 firemen. There are now two steam fire engines on the premises, and one of them was used for the first time on Saturday. This latest one must rank as one of the finest in the district. It is provided with three pumps and is capable of pumping 750 gallons per minute. The training of the men is of the severest kind and thereisa smartness abdut the brigade which would even surprise acute American. During one of the drills a number of girls also showed their usefulness by running out the hose in a smart manner. The hose couplings, it may be stated, are of the latest pattern and the coupling can be safely and quickly accomplished with the slightest difficulty. Judging by Saturday's competition, the directors of the firm need have no fear of their premises being burnt down, for the fire brigade would be playing on the fire before it had time to gain a firm hold. There were six drills undertaken, and the competition judges were Mr. Elliot (Messrs. Shand, Mason' and Co., London), Mr. Frank Turner (superintendent of the Warrington Fire Brigade, who was accompanied by the deputy superintendent, Mr. Frank Turner, jun.), and Mr. Gallon (captain of Messrs. Crosfield's fire brigade, who had with him his sub-captain' Mr. Warburton). In the first drill five men had to answer the alarm, run to the station, dress, take out the hose cart, and get to work with 180 feet of hose, stand pipe and branch.' This took up a considerable time owing to the large number of squads which entered the competition. The best times were as follows:-1, Luin. llsec.•' 2, lmin.l 15sec.; and 3, lade, 15 2-ssec. The leaders of the win- ning squads were Messrs. Flood Whitley and Hershell. In the second drill eight men had to answer the alarm and branch a pipe to go to a position 15 feet from the ground. The men had to be in uniform, run out 180 feet of hose, set up three scaling ladders, and take a branch pipe to third rung from the top of the ladders. The results were :-1, Imin. 3sec.; 2, lmin. 7 2-ssec.; and 3, lmin. 10 4-ssec. Leaders of winning squads,' Messrs. Webb, Hatton and Webster. The third drill was to illustrate how rescue work is carried out with life lines and Pompier belts. The top part of the large new printing works which is 45ft. in height was used. :The person to be rescued took the form of a dummy of a man, and the "rescue" work was accomplished in a manner which aroused the enthusiasm of the spectators. The next item was a competition between six men from the Liverpool works of Messrs. Orosfield, and six from their works at Warrington, and the result was in favour of the local squad. Both teams acquitted them- selves remarkably well and the respective leaders, Messrs. Henshall and Robinson, are to be congratulated on their work. The men had to answer the alarm, but not in uniform (one man acting as superintendent), to stand near the hydrant, run out 180 feet of hose with branch pipe, get water and strike the target. The remaining items were perhaps the most interest- ing of the afternoon's prograstme. The two fire engines were brought into use and the brigade demonstrated their smartness in turning out to an imaginary fire. In the first place, eleven men had to answer the call for the steamer outside the works, run to the station and dress, take" steamer "to the position chosen, set up the (lam, rim out two lengths of parallel hose, ooanect these into one 60 feet hose and set up branch with holder; while in the next drill eleven men had to answer the call for the steamer inside the works. They had to be in uniform, run steamer to, underground tank, and get to work at the targets with two branch pipes, time to be taken when the jets went through the hoops which were suspended from one of the telegraph wires. The leaders of the winning squads were Messrs. Warburton, Flood and Webb. The concluding event wasa full turn out of the brigadel illustrating first and,,gecond call. Mrs. Roome broke the glass near the loadinci, stage, and gave the alarm, and "first aid "was rendered by two men getting to work with a single line of hose from a hydrant. Then the smaller steamer was drawn out by a section of the brigade, after which Captain Gallon, assuming that the' imaginary fire was too big for one engine, to enable him to cope with, sent for the larger one, which was brought out by the deputy captain and drawn by a couple of' horses. In a few moments no fewer than eight jets were at work, the supply of water being obtained from the underground tank. The spectators showed their approval of the brigade's smartness by hearty applause At the close of the competition money prizes were I distributed to the successful competitors by Dr. MaiticEi„ who said he was sure that such proficiency I and progress could not have been obtained without hard work and self-denial on the part of the firemen. They had given Op a great deal of time to the work, and he thanked them very heartily, not only in his own name, but in the name of all who had had the pleasure of watching the success of their work. In handing the prize to the Warrington team in drill D he said that, though the Liverpool team had not won, they had not been disgraced. (Hear, hear, and applause),</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>TWO INTERESTING LETTERS.</head>
        <p>TWO INTERESTING LETTERS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Archibald MacMillan, of Crowhill. Innerwick, East Lothian, Scotland, has written to a London firm two very significant letters. The first, is dated February 22nd, 1902, and in it Mr. MacMillem says :—" I saw in a Glasgow weekly paper an account of how a Mr. Syder was cured of pains in the back and limbs by Dodd's Kidney Pills. I must confess that I oftetr feel just about the same symptoms as Mr. Syder describe, and would thank you to send by post one box of Dodd's Kidney Pills for which I enclose 2s. 9d." The second is written after Mr. MacMillan had com- pleted a treatment of Dodd's Kidney Pills. It says:— "As I wrote you I was for a long time troubled with sore limbs and a very severe backache. I tried many different medicines but only Dodd's Kidney Pills helped me. These Pills have made a new man of me. am stronger and better than I have been for years. "I wish it were in my power to tell all men who are. suffering as I did of what Dodd's Kidney Pills have done for .me." The testimonial of Mr. Syder led Mr. MacMillan to try Dodd's Kidney Pills. His second letter tells the successful result of the trial.</p>
        <p>Those who suffer from similar pains should follow Mr. MacMillan's:experience as he followed Mr. Syder's.</p>
