      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>PASSIVE RESISTANCE.</head>
        <p>PASSIVE RESISTANCE.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON LIBERAL ASSOCIATION, ANNUAL MEETING OF THE GENERAL COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>..,lo MINISTERS STILL RESISTING. 4The Rev. C. E.lng,ram, a Baptist minister, of Wimble- t,'"iy was summoned on Wednesday for non-payment of •ma rates. „Ye said he regarded the Education Act as insulting, Just, and oppressive. b_rit, e Magistrate: This is no place for strangers to ..ate speeohes, or to discuss the question. If you have There was an encouraging attendance at the annual .. `Lo legal objection the order must be made out, meeting of the General Council of the Warrington I'. Ingrain; 1 acknowledge that you represent the , Liberal Association, held at the Committee Room of we represent justice. (Applause.) 1 the Liberal Club, Bawsey-street, on Monday evening. „,,lhe order for payment was made out. 3 -111,„.,e Rev. R. W. Farquhar, a Congregational minister, I Mr. C. E. Parker (president) occupied the chair, and Wimbledon, was similarly summoned. He said he was f there were also present :—Cowacillor William Pier- ,Ainerican citizenpand objected to pay the rate. pint, Councillor Tinnion, Councillor James Evans, „s4J?.e Magistrate made out a similar order. Councillor Upson, Councillor Barton, Councillor There were in all fifteen wther defendants, but they Peacock, Messrs. G.. Wallington, G-. Clay, J, A. J. ' Were disposed of in almost as many minutes. Morton, E. Osborne, J. F, Dixon, J. A. Ewart;George The Court was crowded, and the ministeis were loudly 'Thompson, C. Haley, W. Clegg, James Clegg, A cheered. Downes, •E. G-riffiths, J. Willett, G-. Colley, J. PUBLIC MEETING AT EARLESTOWN. Garner, Nicholson, Dutton, James Heywood, J. Elwell, REV. J. HIRST HOLLOWELL AND THE WESLEYANS. ,J. Bissell, W. Drewett, J. E. Worrall, J. Houghton, •A• largely attended meeting Was held in the Earlestown IH. Few, Atkinson, T. Bibby, J. Lelland, P: Connelly, 4.°Nvn Hall on Wednesday evening, under the auspices F. Dean, G. Shaw, Winstailey, Hughes, A. Morris, 7f the local branch of the Passive Resistance Citizens' ' with F. Farness (hon. treasurer), and C. Jackson Z eague. Mr. Welford, of Lymm presided, and was sup- I Holmes (hon. secretary.) -"led b the Rev. G. Cook, Primitive Methodist I kin: y • - ELECTION OF PRESIDENT. k.., later (who is at the head of the agitation against the Mr. GEORGE WALLINGTON proposed the re-election "cation rate),the Rev.J.Elirst Hollowell,and others. The Ch - of Mr. C. E. Parker as president. In doing so, Mr. I), airman said that he regarded the Act as an invasion on Wallington referred to the impending struggle against IZ6,.rights of the people, and he hoped they would not free trade, and expressed himself as a confirmed believer until they had equal rights and have a complete in the policy of free trade as being the best for this Ilontrol over the management of the schools and the ex- Dem- country. He paid a tribute to the services of Mr. R dare of the ratepayers money (hear, hear). Mr. Parker to the Liberal party in Warrington. it• J. George (president of the league) moved the follow- I Mr. DIXON seconded. ~..; resolution :—" That this meeting asserts its deter- Councillor TENSION supported the proposition and Lurked objection to the Education Act of 1902, . said they had in Mr. Parker the right man in the right , Zrilillse it violates the principles of civil and place. Mr. Parker had endeavoured to become , Igious liberty, seeks to propagate sectarianism at the acquainted withevery member of the Association, and Zrlic expense, and imposes religious tests on teachers he (Councillor Tinnion) thought they should pledge of whom are now civil servants; this meeting also' themselves to do everything they could to further the 2..cl3resses its sympathy with all who on conscient-ous interests of Liberalism in Warrington. in politics, Mr. Kounds refuse to pay the sectarian portion of the Parker's views were always worth knowing (Hear, lcation Rate." The Rev. Hirst Hollowell, who hear.) They were indebted to him for expressing his ,14conded, said that there were eight schools in that views in the public press and for the lead he had given „strict all of which were sectarian, and they as Free them. (Hear, hear.) I 2airchmen were called upon to support them by rates. I le,Mr. MORTON said he understood that the next sloe- ft.,-4e country was up in arms against the Act, and the, tion would be fought on the question of free trade and vernment dared not fight a general election upon the protection. Aocorcling to last Saturday's " Examiner " .'4ltication Act. The Act was a political outrage, ha understood Mr. Parker was a protectionist. (Laugh- Political immorality, and under such an Act he would ter.) hot willingly pay, nor receive on behalf of the schools ? ac IV f his own denomination, the rate. Whilst he objected ~e.r. U. i, ALLINGTON said he was afraid Mr. Morton had not seen the irony of the letter. (Laughter) He t° Pay for the maintenance of Roman Catholic schools, I was sure the meeting understood it. (Hear, hear.) ; lb would refuse with ten times more indignation to 1 The proposition was carried unanimously. r 37 the rate for Wesleyan schools, whose managers The CHAIRMAN, in acknowledging his election as "tgght to know better tlian receive it (hear, hear). The president, said he was afraid that all the kind things %elution was unanimously passed. said about him would make him what his good friend . Mr. Bindloss said he was "so conceited," that there I would be no holding him. (Laughter). In regard to I the question raised by Mr. Wallington, free trade was a . matter about which he (Mr. Parker) had got thoroughly settled convictions. Its was because he had settled con- victions that he had no doubt at all of the soundness of the ground on which they stood. (Hear, hear.) It.was I quite possible in the letters which he had written that i some might be inclined to think he was ratker taking a . dictatorial position. He did not wish to do that in any , way. He did not want to press his own individuality , into the letters or into anything he might eay or I do. It was not because of his own strength but because of the strength of the position he was trying to uphold. (Hear, hear.) Referring to his contribution to the "Examiner "which had beeo Ireferred to by Mr. Morton, the President said it was meant for a joke and he thought everybody could, see , it. (Laughter.) The subject which would come before the electorate at the next general election effected the well being of every man, woman and child in the country. It behoved everyone of them, as he hoped to do, to take off his coat to this subject. (Hear, hear.) He wanted the Liberal executive and the offiders to be in as close touch as possible with the members. He Ishould esteem it a favour if any of the members would write to him on any matter they thought ought to be , brought forward for the good of Liberalism. There was no doubt there had been certain disturbing influences at work which had weakened the cohesion of the Liberal party in the town. It was very necessary, considering the great need there was for a determined Liberal policy, for all of them who professed Liberal principles to work together in perfect cordiality. (Hear, hear.) 1 THE VICE-PRESIDENTS.</p>
        <p>COUNCILLOR PIERPOINT AND HIS POSITION. Councillor TINNION moved that Messrs. Frank A. Roberts, A. Crosfield, Ziba Armitage, EV. Guest, Thomas Birchall, and Co uncillor William Pierpoint be the vice-presidents. Councillor JAMES EVANS seconded. Councillor WILLIAM PIERPOINT observed that if he were judge of what had happened recently he did not think he was quite the kind of person to occupy such a , prominent position in the Liberal party as that they ! proposed to place him in. ("No, no.") He could assure them that if they did not place him in that position, they would not lessen his interest in the Liberal party of Warrington or in the Liberal principles which would have to be enunciated at the next general election. But he thought every leading member of the organisation ought to be acceptable to every part of the body politic. Certain events had happened of late that had shown him very plainly that he was not acceptable to the party as a whole. He would therefore be glad if they.would withdraw his name from the list of vice-presidents. He could assure them it would make no difference as to the effect of his work.</p>
        <p>A discussion ensued, and Councillor Pierpoint was preva.iled upon to allow his name to stand as a vice- president. The names as submitted were then approved of unanimously. HON. TREASURER AND HON. SECRETARY.</p>
        <p>Mr. William Furness was unanimously re-elected. hon. treasurer. In accepting the office, Mr. FURNESS said the Assoeia- tion was in a better financial position to-day than it had been in for the past four years. (Hear. hear.) • Mr. WlLLETT.proposed the 're-election of Mr. C. J. Holmes as hon. secretary and the proposition was agreed to unanimously. Mr: HOLMES said he anticipated a heavier year's work than in the past year and he would be pleased to do his best. CO-OPTATIVE MEMBERS OF THE EXECUTIVE COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The following were added to the Executive Committee, which consists of the Chairmen, deputy-Chairmen, Secretaries, and ono-member of each ward :—Rev. John Yonge, Mr. J. Shaw Green, J.P., Mr. Joseph Bailey, Mr. Jame,s A. Ewart, and Mr. Edmund Osborne. THE GENERAL COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The following were elected co-optative members of the General Council :—Messrs. John Harrison, John J. Crostield, George Thompson, Dr. H. E. Markel, John Critchley, Rev. John Yonge, Francis Brown, Benjamin yew, David Hill, Samuel G. Davey, S. B. Stringer, James A. Evvart, Dr. J. B. Naden, Dr. J. H. Kisby, Dr. ,J. Dore Liston, G. Holford, John NV. Tanner, R. H. Pearson, Captain G. R. Crosfield, James Povey, William Bibby, Captain H. G. Roberts, Rev. John Davies, James Strange, Thomas Birtles, P. B. Garnett, E. Osborne, Dr. R. Smith, Rev. Herbert Dnnnico. THE FORTHCOMING BAZAAR.</p>
        <p>Mr. E.. OSBORNE reported on the initiatory steps which had been taken towards instituting a bazaar to be held next year. A discussion ensued and a large number in the room promised support. It was suggested that the ladies who had promised support should be called together and a weekly sewing meeting arranged. . .</p>
        <p>A resolution was tinanimrinslv passed expressing opinion that the bazaar should be proceeded with. A meeting of the Liberal executive was afterwards held.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>CURRENT POLITICS.</head>
        <p>CURRENT POLITICS.</p>
        <p>The new Education Act and Mr. Chamberlain's fiscal proposals were considered, and a special committee was formed, consiSting of Vessrs. Parker, 4Tinnion, James Evans, Haley, Osborne and Elwell, for the purpose of arranging with Mr. W. P. Rylands, Mr. Bindloss and others to deliver addresses at a special meeting to be held at the club some time in September on these ques- tions. It was also decided t 6 arrange to ask a Cobden Club lecturer to attend at about the same time for a similar purpose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>PEDESTRIANISM.</head>
        <p>PEDESTRIANISM.</p>
        <p>St. I.lcns Co-operative Society Employees.</p>
        <p>The employs of the St. Helens Co-operative Society held a walking match on Tuesday afternoon. The start took place at three o'clock, the distance being over a course of fourteen miles. There were over twenty entrants, and valuable prizes were offered. Fourteen competitors were started by Mr. G. H. Winterbottom, the manager of the society. ' They all qualified for medals by covering the distance under 2hrs. 45mins. H. Ashall was first, going through in 2hrs. 34rn1ns. The five immediately following Acliall were H. James, J. H. Elliott, E. Williamson, W. Doan, and W. Mather.</p>
      </div>
