      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>ORDER OF RECHABITES. the Forthcoming Conference at</head>
        <p>ORDER OF RECHABITES. the Forthcoming Conference at</p>
        <p>Swansea.</p>
        <p>Ole annnal movable conference of the Independent Vler of Rechabites will be held on the first week in 111Ilgust at Swansea. The 'organisation is the largest l'broperance order, and the third largest friendly society 14 the United Kingdom. The Board's report to the Conference indicates that tleat progress has been made during the two years ended L'tember, 1902. No fewer than 35,000 benefit members i7:111 been added, bringing the number up, all told, to 4),000. The funds amount to £1,400,000. Doring the Years 173 new adult tents and, 185 juvenile tents e been added to the list of branches in the United 1„11gd• onli te"ith regard to old age pensions, the committee ixtPrort that attention has been given to the subject, and tieularly to the scherpe propounded by insttuctions 11„1,t,rep1esentatives' (but not a membership) vote at the Conference of Friendly Societies. vv.s think" (the • report states) "that 0t,7453, societies would be more usefully p_lll3ied in arranging for pensions for their Loriembers at 60,0 r 65 years of age on the fines ern- ktylPti in our general rules some years ago, and of pe7", ng it compulsory on all new entrants, after a given t.,..„10d, to pay for sickness benefits terminating at the euinted age, and supenannuation benefits beyond th,,t age, and towards this end we recommend the incoming board be empowered to rilunicate with the National Conference of tcily Societies and its constituent orders and socie- -14:1 Mlgg„esting that all such orders or societies assur- yo° l'enefits such as our order does, bind themselves to 4:114 this course of action to operate as and from 44(114trY Ist, 1910, or earlier by arrangement. State aid att 'voluntary thrift are apart and distinct, and we tioo,_llgly advise our membership to keep the two ques- t° " separate and from a friendly society point of view 1) %trench the grand principles of self-help and mutual „ 4iutTierhood and as citizens to require the State to do 'ce to the aged deserving poor.'</p>
        <p>There's nothing better than Starch</p>
        <p>for starching. . You can't add anything to it that will really improve it. If it's good starch, pure starch, all the chemicals in the world won't make it better. Clothes last longer, retain their stiff- ness better, keep a whiter colour, when pure starch</p>
        <p>is used. And Colman's Starch is an absolutely pure starch.</p>
