      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday July 25, 1903.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday July 25, 1903.</p>
        <p>THE FREE CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>THE HOLIDAYS.</p>
        <p>44,$aW'</p>
        <p>August is the great Ministerial vacation month, and in many of our places of worship familiar faces will be absent not only from the pew, but from the pulpit also. Among the prominent Free Churchmen going away, the Rev. James Travis, the President of the National Free Church Council, is leaving town almost immediately, and will be absent the whole of August, returning in September for a most vigorous campaign up and down the country. Dr. W. J. Townsend is going to have a holiday at home—an example which many Free Church leaders might follow, and one peculiarly acceptable to Dr. Townsend, as his home is by the sea in a lovely North Wales village. He has been spending so much time campaigning in the country that this form of holiday will be a decided change. The Rev. Silas K. Hocking, the popular preacher-novelist, is dividing his vacation time -between Margate and Scarborough. The Rev. F. B. Meyer, 8.A., the President-elect of the National Free Church Council, than whom perhaps there is no harder worker in the country, will leave town on thb 4th August. The Rev. J. H. Shakespeare, the genial Secretary of the Baptist Union, ha's fixed Broadstairs for the scene of his holiday. The Rev. J. H. Jowett, M.A., who has not yet quite recovered from the effects of his illness, is already in Switzerland. The Rev. Thomas Law, the Secretary of the National Free Church Council, has decided to remain in England in order to be near London in view of the Educa- tion Campaign. His holiday will be divided between the South Coast, Scarborough, and Scotland.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>GOSPEL IN THE OPEN AIR.</head>
        <p>THE GOSPEL IN THE OPEN AIR.</p>
        <p>While the majority of people regard the summer as the time for rest and recuperation, there are many forms of activity, religious included, which are particularly in evidence now. At our seaside resorts preparations for a great open-air campaign have been made by Free Church Councils, and the services by the sea-shore will attract numbers who never enter a place of worship. The National Free Church Council's Mission Van has been at work in the rural parts of Cambridgeshire and West Norfolk. At King's Lynn on a recent Sunday fine meetings were held in the evening, not less than 2000 people crowding round the van. Some of the results are especially gratify- ing, and there can be no doubt aFi to the need for this kind of work in our villages. The van goes on to Watlinaton Gayton, Pentney, Castleacre, Dereham, (kc. The missioner in charge is the Rev. Jesse Mayo, who has recently returned from America to take up the work.</p>
        <p>MR. LLOYD-GEORGE ON AN EDUCATION SETTLEMENT.</p>
        <p>Mr. D. Lloyd-George, M.P., has been giving to a writer in the Free Church Chronicle his view on the probable settlement of the Educa- tion Question. He thinks that a repeal of last year's Act is out of the question. "When a Liberal Government gets into power," says Mr. George, "it will have to abolish the sectarian schools absolutely. Every school maintained by the State must be under popular control, and the parson should have no more right es-officio to intervene in the management of the schools than the publican." Mr. Lloyd-George does not think that the country should buy up the denominational schools. "The majority of them are not worth buying. Antiquated, ill-ventilated, and badly lit as they are, I should doubt whether it is desirable for the country to go to a heavy capital expenditure to buy them out. But I think a rent might be paid for them. I would object to that rent being a rack rent, inasmuch as the trust upon which these schools have been erected is of a two-fold character. One element in their trust is undoubtedly their sectarian complexion, but the other is educa- tional; and although some men have no doubt contributed heavily, having regard to the sec- tarian objects, there are others who have sub- scribed to the building funds or have given sites because they regard them as village schools for the education of the children of the parish in secular subjects. It wohld, therefore, be an injustice to compel ratepayers tq' pay a rack rent to a sect for the school that only partly belongs to them."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>L.C.C. AS RATE-REFUSER.</head>
        <p>L.C.C. AS RATE-REFUSER.</p>
        <p>Mr. Lloyd-George thinks that the London County Council should adopt the policy of the Welsh Councils when the London Education Bill becomes an Act, and refuse to make any contribution out of the rates towards schools' which imposed a sectarian test and denied popular control. He thinks they would succeed. If the London County Council refused to aid denominational schools do you think this Government is strong enough to coerce, let us say, a dozen County Councils in Wales, plus the County Council of the largest city in the world The idea is too ludicrous. They would rather go out than face the situation. I think pressure ought to be brought to bear on London County Councillors to pledge them to this position, and no support should be given to any candidate who declines to pledge himself to that course. It is by far the most effective way of fighting the Act."</p>
        <p>THE GOVERNMENT AND ALBERT HALL DEMONSTRATION.</p>
        <p>People are still talking about the great de. monstration which the Metropolitan Free Church Federation organised at the Royal Albert Hall, London. Whether it will affect the Government is an open question; one can never say how the present Government will act towards any question. They may ignore the demonstration; or they may climb down, but even they must at last see that Free Churchmen have resolved neither to surrender nor to com- promise, and that we are only at the beginning of a struggle, the end of which no man can fore- tell.</p>
        <p>DR. AKED'S ILLNESS.</p>
        <p>Much sympathy is expressed with Dr. Aked, who is lying ill at. a sanatorium in Switzerland. The latest news indicates , that he is distinctly improving. The letters received from him are of a far brighter tone. - The doctors definitely give him the hope of being back at Liverpool by next summer. That means till then lying where he is practically all the time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>SAINTS IN SLUMS.</head>
        <p>SAINTS IN SLUMS.</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. Tolefree Parr, one of the Mie• sioners of the National Free Church Council, and who ,has had a wide experience of life in poverty-stricken London, writes in this month's Free Churchman on "Saints in Slums." Hu says: "We usually associate sinners railer than saints with slums, and, alas! it is only too true that the vast majority of their inhabitants are utterly godless. But it is not all the truth. There are striking and beautiful exceptions. Many a gem of purest piety is hidden away in the moral and spiritual darkness of Slumland. Many a flower of grace pours forth its fragrance in the arid desert of its dismal courts and alleys." Pathetic instances of these he gives, and the article is illustrated by pictures of the Salvation Army Sister, Wesleyan Sister of the People, nhildren in the slums, &amp;c.</p>
        <p>TRY ALLEN'S FOOT EASE a powder to be shaken into the shoes. Your feet feel swollen, nervous, and damp, and Ret tired easily. If 'you have aching feet, try Allen's Foot-Rase. It rests the feet and makes new or eight boots easy. Cares hot, swollen, sweating feet, blisters and callous spots. Re- lieves corns and bunions of all pain, and gives rest and comfort Try it to-day. Of all Chemists and Stores. Price is lid. Accept no srtsstitute, Sent post free for is. 10. Trial packagePß4ll.—Aldress ALLEN Solmsted, 14, Silver-street, Bloomsbury Square, London.</p>
