      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>CLERGY INSTITUTIONS AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>CLERGY INSTITUTIONS AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>THE ANNIVERSARY PROCEEDI.O3. Extensions of the Clergy Daughters' School Required.</p>
        <p>The anniversary of the Clergy Widows' and Orphans' Society and the Clergy Daughters' School, chnnected with the three dioceses of Chester, Manehester and Liverpool, was kept at Warrington on Wednesday. The meetin* of subscribers was presided over by Chancellor Phillip Vernon Smith (Chancellor of the Jiocese of Manchester) in the absence of Mr. W. W. B. Hulton (president). -Amongst others present were the Bishop of Liverpool, the Dean of Manchester, Arch- deacon Gore, Revs, Canons Penrhynand Willis, Rev. E. C. E. Carleton and Mr. H. Greenall.</p>
        <p>The Rev. CANON PENRHYN (hon. secretary of the Clergy Widows and Orphans Pension Fund) presented the annual report. The committee regretted to have to announce that since the last anniversary three of their number had been taken away by death.—Canon H. 131ackburne, Captain Willis of Halsmead, elder brother of the Rector of Warrington and Dr. Watkins, of Newton-le-Willows, who had been on the committee for very many years. The latter was a regular attendant, and was hardly ever absent. Another vacancy was caused by the resignation of Canon Armour, who was giving up his position as headmaster of Merchant Tailors School, Crosby, and leaving the neighbourhood. In their places the committee recommended Mr. W. E. Willink, Mr. Douglas Buckton, Rev. G. B. Thurston, Rector of Lymm, and the Rev. Percy Stewart, Rector of West Derby. They had also to deplore the loss of Mr. G. B. Gilmour, who was president in 1893. No other names having been sent in to the secretaries the retiring members are re-elected subject to the confirms- lion of the annual meeting. Of those who received I grants last year none had died, but one widow and one orphan from Manchester had not sent in applications. On the other hand there were new applications from two widows and three orphans from Manchester, one widow from Chester and one widow and one orphan from Liverpool. The meeting was asked to sanction pensions and donations to the amount of £1865 and if the recommendation of the Committee was approved there would be on the list of recipients 18 widows and 16 orpl-an3 from Chester 30 widows and 20 orphans from Manchester and 15 widows and 11 orphans from Liverpool. The income for the year-ended December 1902,was £2,379 Os. Id. It was pointed out that a large increase in donations had taken place and this was chiefly due to the special effort which in the diocese of Chesteramounted to £l9O. The Bishop of Liverpool had paid in £l5O, andthe amount from the diocese of Manchester had not yet been re- ceived. Theymust not, however, relax their efforts, as they needed to have at least £2,000 a year, and they must look to the likelihood of the calls upon them in- creasing rather than diminishing. The Rev. Canon WILLIS (hon. secretary of the Clergy Daughters' School) presented the report of the Com- mittee of Management for the year ending July 22nd. He stated that the results of the Cambridge higher local examinations and of the Oxfordlocal examinations in 1902 were all that could be deeired. Perhaps the most satisfactory feature was that every girl in the school to , the end of the upper third was presented for examina- tion, and that only two failed either to pass in some group of the higher local examination or to obtain an Oxford certificate, neither being a serious failare, the one passing in two out of three sections into which Group A (English literature) was divided, and the other passing the Oxford Junior in religious knowledge, dictation, English, Latin and French, but failing in arithmetic, which was a compulsory, subject. The June and July examinations of this year had just been held. Thelreport for music was excellent, 26 out of 28 having passed in knowledge of the piano, four obtaining dis- tinctions. The Oxford Examiner, Dr. Randell, 8.D., Con., and D.D. Dublin, said the general results of the examination must be pronounced very good, and Mr. Ablett, art director of the Royal Drawing Society said in his summary—" a good result.' For the violin one passed and one failed. Great praise was due to Mr. Darby and the assistant music mistresses the Misses W. Harry and H. Thatcher. The health and conduct of the girls throughout the year had been very good, as there had been only two serious cases of illness. The actual number in residence was 80, and for these there was now ample sleeping accommodation, another dormitory fpr some time used by the college having been restoreU to the school. The chief want in the way of extension was a large recreation' room, the import- ance of which the committee fully appreciated. The recognition of the school by the Board of Education as a centre for the training of student teachers was a matter of pressing moment to all students belonging to it who wished to make teaching their profession and were obliged to study economy. The requisite stops for effecting that recognition were now being taken The committee had the greatest pleasure in announcing that one of their student mistresses, Miss Ruth Freer, had 'obtained an open scholarship of £4O a year, tenable. for three years at Lady Margaret's Hall, Oxford. To her, as to the head mistress, they had expressed their ' warmest congratulations, and to the latter their deep sense of the value of her successful preparation. (Applause.) The CHAIRMAN, in moving the adoption of the reports, thought they presented satisfactory features. It was necessary, however, that the sNcial effort which had been made should become an annual one if all the liabilities were to be met. He must admit with a cer- tain amount of shame that Manchester, whilst receiving the largest number of benefits, contributed the smallest amount.</p>
        <p>The DEAN of MANCHESTER: I am not prepared to admit that for a moment.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN trusted that greater efforts would be made in the archdeaconries of Manchester to increase the contributions to the institutions.</p>
        <p>Archdeacon GORE seconded. The DEAN of MANCHESTER stated that, as a result of a special effort, Manchester, had raised .£l5O, but the money had not yet been paid into the funds of the institution. During the past few years Manchester had also increased their annual subscriptions, and that was a far healthier sign than any special donation. He was not yet satisfied with the subscriptions, and he thought the rural deaneries of Manchester might do more. They had reached a new departure now in the Clergy Daughters' school. The Education Committee met on Tuesday to consider certain recommendations at the request of the head mistress (Miss Kennedy). They desirous of obtaining full recognition of these in- stitutions for;the training of students and teachers and to do that they must increase the size of the building by hook or crook. There was a resolution, which had not been read expressing the keen regret most of them felt that they had not been able to come to such terms with the Training Collegeas to enable them to go there al- together,and for them (the Clergy Daughters) to go elsewhere. They were obliged to find further accommo- dation, and if they did not they would fall out of the rank of institutions of this kind. He felt that the time hadDnore than arrived when they should have the school so equipped that it should be second to none in all its appointments, and as years went on to satisfF . more fully the desires and aims of Miss Kennedy. It was the intention of the committee to consider, first of all, the interests of the three dioceses for which the institu- tions were founded. They would have to ask those who took an interest in the institutions to assist them in the undertaking. The reports were adopted unanimously. In moving a vote of thanks to the chairman, the Bishop of Liverpool said he believed that when the school was originally founded, the Rector of Warrington meant that the girls should pass into the TraininkCollege, and become mistresses of their national schools. It seemed o him that they were reverting to the old system inr tan improved way for he could conceive of no highe vocation for the daughters of the clergy than being teachers in their secondary schools. .Mr. JOYNSON (Bowdon) seconded. He was informed by Miss Kennedy, he said, that 60 per cent of the pupils took up the teaching profession. If they adopted the new system a still larger proportion would avail them- selves of the teaching profession. Tho vote of thanks was unanimously carried. THE ANNIVERSARY SERMON.</p>
        <p>The Right Rev. BISHOP THORNTON (vicar of Black- burn) preached at the anniversary service. Basing his discourse on the text " Follow after charity,' (I. Corinthians, 14c., lv.), he said he said he knew of no text which suggested more exactly tho spirit in which such an institution, as that which they celebrated that day, shouldlbe carried on and the motive which should induce church people at large to contribute to it. There were many of their clergy who could not possibly with their present incomes and calls upon them, make special provision for their widows and young children, in view of the contingency of their premature death. Thiseought not to be so in the National Church; but so it was, and it had been brought about in part by depreciation of church properties which could not be foreseen. In such circumstances it was a Christian duty to provide for the widows and children of the clergy. He made bold te say that as a wholo the clergy amply deserved this, and not less than this of the Church they served.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>LUNCHEON.</head>
        <p>THE LUNCHEON.</p>
        <p>After the service luncheon was served at the Training College, the catering being excellently carried out by the Winmarleigh Cafe. Company Chancellor P. V. Smith presided. The toasts of "Church and King," and "The Queen and the restiof the Royal Family" were honoured on the proposition of the CHAIRMAN. Mr. BARBER, of Bolesworth Castle, proposed the health of the Bishops of Liverpool, Manchester, and Chester, and expressed regret that the Bishop of Chester was unable to attend. There was no class of men more bard-worked, more liberal with their pockets, and more unselfish than the Bishops :who held rule over 'the dioceses of England. Let the clergy do their best to uphold the Bishops, let the laity do their bcst to uphold the clergy and the Bishops and by so doing let them hope that the day of the disAta. blishrnent of the Church would never come. If England retained her position as a religious country they need never fear for the decadence of England or that the sun of England's glory would set. In responding, the. BislioP of LIVERPOOL said he</p>
        <p>could only !re-echo the wish of Mr. Barber that the clergy and laity might work loyally together. They ought , never to forget that the clergy did not formthe Church. I They were only the representatives and delegates of the I laity and unless the clergy and the laity worked hand in hand the Church of England could never hold he position that she now occupied as the National Church of this country. He believed that in no pArt of Englun I were the clergy doing a nobler and more devoted than in the three dioceses represented that aftermon. There were amongst them men of the very highest• ability and the very greatest self-sacrifice. Here in Lancashire they had to deal witka class of men who were delightfully outspoken and honest but excessively criti- cal and as he moved about the diocese of Liverpool— which he was sure WiLS only an example of what took place in other dioceses—he found that the laity were more and more gradually rallying to the help of the clergy—(hear, hear,)—and that an increas- ing spirit of union anil appreciation was growing up amongst them. In conclusion the speaker made a feeling reference to the impending retirement of the Bishop of Manchester, who he said, was one of their great bulwarks and whose place it would be hard indeed to fill. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. JOYNSON proposed "The diocesan societies" and said it very often struck him as strange that the institutions were not better supported than they were. He did not know whether to put this down to thqk selfishness of the laity or to their indifference or ignor- ance. It might be a little of one or the other or a mixture 'of all three.</p>
        <p>The ARCHDEACON of Blackburn responded and said these institutions would not be necessary at all if it were not for the poverty of the clergy which made men unable to provide for their widows and orphans. He had always thought it was a great disgrace to the Church of so wealthy a country as England that the clergy shwa(' ,not be paid in a proper manner for the services theylrendered. He was hardly prepared to go with Bishop Thornton in the proposal as to the redistribu- tion of incomes because it appeared to him that, so far ashis knowledge went, none of the clergy had anything to spare. What the church really wanted was not a. new division of what the clergy had but a new endow- ment from top to bottom. The toast of Bishop Thornton was heartily honoured on the proposition of Mr. Walter Kinch, and in reply the Bishop paid atribute to the worth of the retiring Bishop of Manchester. Referring to the income of the clergy his lordship said that what he proposed was a redistribution of posts of duty. They should mass five or six country parishes together andappointitwo clergymen to go round and serve them. By pooling the revenues they would get more than sufficient income for the country clergy and release men for service in the crowded parishes. The toasts of the Chairman and Miss Kennedy having been honoured thelproceedings were brought to a close. Subsequently a concert was held and the prizes gained by the students were distributed.</p>
      </div>
