        <p>A FAMOUS COMPOSER. THE DIARY OF A CELEBRITY occasion- ally throws many curious and interesting side- lights on the circumstances attending his rise into public favour. A Well Known Composer, whose exquisite melodies have contributed to the happiness or numberless homes in every corner of the globe, makes a strikingly significant statement regard- ing his most Popular Successes. It is well understood that the Musical Temper- ament, highly strung and sensitive, is peculiarly liable, especially under a physical affliction, to worry its possessor into a form of mental activity which speedily exhausts all the available nerve</p>
        <p>forces, -and becomes a serious menace to . Efficiency. Events combined to reduce the .:ompos.. co the ...Aing lion described above, but (as he relates in the impressive communication which follows) he was fortunately ordered by his doctors to take Phosferine, which he found so beneficial that the public have been further enriched by the delightful songs which his wonderfully restored health allowed him to compose. Mr. EDWIN GREENE, Buckingham Villa, Cheltenham, one of the most famous Musical Composers of to-day, author of those beautiful songs" Springtide," "There is a Garden,"" I remember, I remember,' "Sing me to Sleep," songs that brought pleasure and consolation into thousands of homes throughom the world, writes;—" Three years ago I had to undergo a most serious surgical operation (Inguittat Colotomy), this saved my life, but left my nerves in a frightful state. I took Phosfenne on he adv.ce of my doctors, which kas so thoroughly restored my heath and vitality, that since then I have written my most successful songs. Altogether, since then, I have written 27, and of these published 5, three of theta have been more successful than I could possibly have anticipated. I always say that it is a great mystery to me that there should be a person living who has never used or heard of the wonderful effects of Phosferine. So many friends of mine have expressed their wonder at my wondrous vitality after so severe an illness and operations; my answer is Phosferine. I belive in Phosferiue as I do in the s'-y being above me. You will recollect I wrote you a testimonial bet, re (in March, igoo) and now after a lapse of three years I send you this one to use as you like. I have recommended it to scores of f nen ts and hav,2 always found it exactly as you state. Your Phosferine has helped me so much that you cannot possibly understand all it has been to me."—March 7th. 1903.</p>
