      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>MR. CHAMBERLAIN'S TARIFF</head>
        <p>MR. CHAMBERLAIN'S TARIFF</p>
        <p>PROPOSALS.</p>
        <p>A QUESTION FOR MR. HUGHES.</p>
        <p>A correspondent, signing himself "LABOURER; desires to ask Mr. H. P. Huahes "whether under one sided Free-Trade. or any other kind of Trade, the majority of workingmen have ever been able to afford more than one daft trip in the year by the seaside, or whether they rel ever likely to do so?"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>MR. CHAMBERLAIN'S "DOTAGE"</head>
        <p>MR. CHAMBERLAIN'S "DOTAGE"</p>
        <p>All " COCeASTON&amp;L CORRESPONDENT" writes : I think Mr. Chamberlain's proposal to tax the food of the people must be attributed to his otd age or dotage, for no one can reasokably expect a .policy that failed in the last =century to be a success in this century. I con- sider this proposal to obtain money by an old method proves that there is a dearth of ability in the present Cabinet, in fact their spendthrift trait of squandering -seems to form their chief art of governing. It is alleged that Mr,tChamberlain's policy caused many to fall victims in War. It is almost certain if Mr. Chamberlain succeeds in his designs that many of the or will diel est famine in the near future. Hunger will very likely prevenp them attaining old age and old age pensions. Again the promised old age -pensions may be nothing but crapty promises—siraply baits used by Joseph to land the people and make them endure ;another period of Tory misrule.</p>
        <p>R. W. P. RYLANDS' REPLY TO MR. I. H. CROSFIELD. Arguments for a Preferential Tariff. • taiiff 141 t. W. P. 'RYL&amp;Nos writes : ,One of'the most curious aspects,of this question lies in the fact that the Radical leaders have officially ranged themselves against any proposals having as their object'the modification of our .fiscal policy, thus making it a purely party question. There is no essential feature, asli matter of fact, in the proposals which have been put forward, that should excite more sympathy from a Conservative than from a Radical. On the contrary it I 'has Always been a feature of progressive Liberalism to' be ;prepared to, entertain any policy, however novel, if calculated to ,effect a desirable end, while to resent a departure from a policy of 50 years, is a sign by which above everything else, a Tory is known. There seems to beit regrettable endeavour to make this a class ques- tion,as in the old days the abolition of the Corn Laws -waslargely a class question between the landowners and the working classes. There appears to be .an idea thatwhile a chaugedn our fiscalpolicy might beto the, advantage of the rich, in some way or other itwould be a source of injury to the poor. Here, again ask your readers to reflect that the interests of manu- facturers, and of their employs, must be Identical. ,Given good trade, employer and Men reap the benefit. If trade isbad and businessunprofitable, men are thrown out .of -work. If the adoption of _Preferential Tariffs would be bad for the trade of the country ,it would I be the manufacturers who would suffer, and if the working, of Free Trade is the success that some of our theorists claim, it is the manufacturers who must be everieneing the benefit. lsio member of the conknunity will feelthe effect of the fiscal policy of a country as directly as the manufacturers who are in daily contact -with its operation in practice, and it is an old saying that no ,one knows how the shoe pinches so well as the wearer. Working men therefore should remember that their ,prosperity is bound up in the prosperity of their employer, and .instead of viewing proposals with pre- judice, as though it were a question setting classagainst class, the fact that manufacturers are strongly in favour of a change in our fiscal policy should excite their sympathy,rather than their apprehension. In the middle of last century our economists when considering fiscal policy had only to deal with the effect •of protective duties, and they realised that the country which produced most cheaply had the best chance in the open markets of the world, and that free imports afforded the best 'opportunity for developing the natural resources of a country. This proposition no one now disputes, but to-day we have to face the prob- lem complicated by a system of bounties in conjunction With heavy protective duties, and it is in the bounty system that the-whole of the-present difficulty of the 13ritish manufacturer lies, ,and which has rendered a departure from our former policy necessary, if we are to meet the changed condition of the world's trade to</p>
        <p>day. Mr. Crosfield in his letter in your last issue devotes a large portion of his space to setting up argumentewhich no one, so far-as lam aware, has ever put forward, and then scientifically demolishing them. This is a contro- versial method which has the advantage of simplicity but while interesting it can hardly be regarded as instructive. In the -small portion in which he makes a reply to my last letter he does not seriously controvert the two points which I put forward, and if a reply is needed,l would ask your readers to refer once more to my letter in your issue of the 4th instant. In relerring to the United States, however, Mr. Ores- field makes a somewhat serious misrepresentation of my argument. My contention was, as Mr. Crostield rightly says, that the case of the British Empire in territory awl population is analogous to the United States. I did not suggest that in every respect the two countries werealike,or that it would be practicable, even if expedient, to form an Imperiel Zollverein. This is quite another question, involving speculations in regard to which I do not propose to follow Mr. Cros- field. My only objet in referring to the United States was to lay stress upon the fact, which is patent to all, and cannot be controverted, that in the case of a large country with a large internal trade, it is possible to njoy remarkable prosperity, in spite, if not as a result, of the heaviest system of Protection the world has seen There is one fnrther statement:in Mr. Crosfield's letters to which I must take the strongest exception. Mr Crosfield states that I assumed a general advance of wages as a result of Protection. I never assume d any thing of the kind. I was referring to the increase in the earning power of the people in the aggregate, as the result of increased employment. While not involving an actual increase in wages either per hour or per piece this might involve an increase,and a substantial increase in the total wages taken home at the end of the week by any particular workman. A very differeneproposition. My contention is not that the taxation of food-stuffs would be in itself an advantage to the community but that the sacrifice involved would be far more than counter-balanced by the additional employment which would be given to the people through the increase of our export trade, resulting from Preferential Tariffs placed upon British manuipctures entering our Colonies. In correspondence in the papers, and in speeches, I fre- quently have noticed this false point made by the opponents to the new policy. They dwell upon the evil results flowing from the taxation of food, and argue as though it were the contention of its supporters that the benefits of the policy would arise from the taxation of food-stuffs. The contrary is,of course,the case.Thetaxation of food-stuffs is regarded as a sacrifice,and a disadvantage in itself,but a sacrifice worth making in order to obtain the concessions which our colonies are now offering to us. I ask your readers therefore to always bear this in mind, that in arguing:in support of Prefereiltial Tariffs Ido not sEty that the slightest benefit will be derived by this country from the taxation of any import what- ever, but I do say that if a larger share of the Colonial trade can be secured by British manufacturers, and by British working men, it is worth some sacrifice to secure '</p>
        <p>it. We may lay down the broad proposition that the cost of a commodity wholly manufactured in a country is made up of three factors, namely, wages, rent and profit, of which wages form by far the largest proportion. The money realised by every ton of material sold _abroad is distributed as income among members of the community in one of the three forms I have mentioned, namely, wages, rent or profit. The greater the amount of exports, the greAer J population a country can</p>
        <p>support. The amount of profit arising from a trans- action is quite immaterial, as the total amount realised, be it great or small, is distributed as income, and the absence of profit merely effects a limited portion of the community, and is only important so far as it en- courages capital to develop an enterprise or the reverse. It might therefore be to the interest of a country to grant a bounty upon the exportation of any commodity and although the selling country would, no doubt, be giving to the country to which such commodity is ex- ported the amount of the bounty, still the remainder of the accruing price would be wholly profit to the country which exports it. Let us take a concrete case and assume that a buyer in Australia requires 1,000 tons of iron, the cost Of which in Germany is 15s. per ton higher than in England Assume, however, that a bounty of 21 per ton is allowed •by the German Government upon the export of this iron, in consequence of which the German is enabled to secure the order in competition with the English manufacturer, which otherwise he would have lost. We will assume that the selling price of that iron is £9 per ton, the total sum therefore realised by the transaction would be £9,000. The £9,000 paid by the buyer being distributed amongst certain members of the German community as income in the form of rent, wages or profit, the result of the transaction would be that while on the one hand the community has subscribed; £l,OOO on the other hand, £9,000 is distributed amongst them, leaving a nett gain of £B,OOO or sufficient to keepabout 100 working men over 12 months. the result of a sale which under our assumption would not have been made at all but for the grant of that bounty. This is exactly what is happening to-day,-and is the principal difficulty with which the English Manufacturer has to contend and this is the consideration which makes the problem to-day entirely different from the problem with which our economists had to deal 50 years ago. Bounties take various forms, and may be direct or indirect. The direct bounties given by the German Government are principally confined to Preferential Freights, goods being carried to the seaport from the manufacturers' works by the Government railways, either for anominal sum, or even for nothing. By this means the cost of carriage from a works in Germany to some neutral market, say Australia, is invariably substantially lower than the cost of carriage from an English works to the same poit. Another, and perhaps more serious form of bounty adopted by the Germans is an indirect one, and is subscribed, not by the community through the medium of the Government, but by means of a pool to which all the members of the trade interested contribute. This is what is known as the "Cartel" system. Subscriptions to thispool are provided out of the great profits secured from the home trade with the assistance of the protec- tive tariff. A bounty or rebate is made from this pool of so much per ton in respect of every ton of material exported. The amount varies with the character of the commodity, and upon the quantity of work which has been put into it, ranging from 10s. to 20s. per ton. This taken in conjunction with systemized trade arrange- ments, selling pools, and other artificial devices, affords a weapon to the German exporter, with which it is almost impossible for the British manufacturer to con- tend.</p>
        <p>in America the system adopted is different, although the effect is the same. In America the Iron Trade, for instance,is now almost completely consolidated in the huge company which commonly goes by the name of the "Billion Dollar Trust." A large bulk of trade is, of course, done in the United States, and being con- trolled by the company, they are able with the assistance of their enormous tariffs to secure margins of profit beydnd the wildest dreams of any British manufacturer. It is a well-known fact, so great is th advantage of increased production, that if half a mann , lecturer's trade is done at a-large profit, it is profitable to sell the other half actually below cost. Conse- quently with the assistince of protected home markets, the Americans are able to continuously export manu- factures at a profit to all parts of the world, at prices a I which an Englishman, in spite of the advantages of Free Trade, would lose money. he Americans, realising that the strength of their position lies in the profitable prices which they are able to obtain at home, take pre- cautions that the British manufacturer shall if possible be prevented from, enjoying a similar advantage. Con- sequently theyhave established offices in this country, and while during the last year or two their exports of steel and steel manufactures to this country have no t been very large, owing to the extraordinary prosperity they have been enjoying in their own country, they are always in touch with the English market, and are con- tinually accepting orders at a very low level of prices regardless of cost, with the object of preventing English prices getting to a profitable level, which might be the case should the Americans witkdraw their competition. This is the most serious aspect of modern trading, and while,as I have suggested,it may be advantageous for the people to be able to buy things that they require cheap, it would be a matter of the most serious concern to this country if our manufacturers were crushed out by the operation of the system of bounties direct and indirect, which.are practised by our competitors. This country has taken cognizance of the bounty system upon sugar practiced on the Continent, and has taken steps to pre- vent its action crushing an industry in one portion of the British Empire. Does it seem unreasonable that the British Government should take cognizance of a bounty system which is crushing out the life of indus- tries not in out-lying parts of the British Empire but within its own borders? The difference between the direct bounty and the indirect bounty, which."' have endeavoured to describe is a difference only in operation, but not in effect, and it must strike anybody as reasonable that however cheap our costs of production, if a Foreign Government gives a sufficiently large, bounty to their manufacturers they could secure the whole of our trade. This then is the problem with which we have to deal, and the danger, is a real one. At present the pressure is most keenly felt by the manufacturers of iron and steel, but the same policy is spreading in America through other branches of trade. As I have said, during the last two years the home demand in the United States has been enormous. When, however, the home demand falls off as it most assuredly will in the near future, we will see American surplus manufactures dumped down in this country, and in other markets of the world at prices systematically 10s. or 20s per. ton below the English prices whatever those may be. There may be various ways of Meeting this difficulty, but assuredly the best cannot be to sit down, and rely upon the assistance of Providence to ward off the avalanche. Militant policy must be met by militant policy. It is no more reasonable to rely upon the natural workings of Free Trade to save the com- merce of this country than to disband our army in the belief that it is the law of nature that the fittest shall survive, and that therefore guns and ammunition can- not be of real avail. One remedy is to be found in the suggested change in our fiscal policy. Our Colonies offer the largest markets of the future, markets the di- mands of which are continually on the increase. The advantage of seizing these markots to ourselves must be obvious to any one, and to refuse the offer of sub- stantial preference for our manufactures over those of other countries now made to us by our Colonies is a 1 course which I trust and feel convinced this country will not adopt.</p>
        <p>One Colonies at the present time; however mulch Mr. Crosfield may sneer at the volume of their trade,import 250 millions sterling per annum, but little less than the total valudof our exports in any one year.Of these imports Great Britain supplies only 100 millions. Where Mr. Crosfield obtained the figures he gives in his letter I am at a loss to discover. I have before me the Annual Statement of the trade of the United Kingdon, and I see that the total exports of produce and manufactures of the United Kingdom to Australia in 1900 was 21i millions. The total imports into Australia from all</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday july 25, 1903.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday july 25, 1903.</p>
        <p>working men of this country would be immense, and it ' w2uld insure full time in many towns where at present short time is worked. The rate of piece work or time work might remain the same, but a working man is fully alive to the advantage of working six days per week. 1 It is a general rule in this world if you want a benefit you have to pay for it, and the question to consider is not whether you must pay anything, but whether the benefit is cheap at the price. I quite agree that tax- ation of food is to the disadvantage of the community, and I do not think that anyone has seriouslyput forward any contention to the contrary, although Mr. Crosfield appears to think that this is the case. The question we ' have to consider is whether the advantage obtained by preference in the Colonial markets is worth the cost in- volved. I do not pretend to say what tax it is con- templated should be imposed. I have little doubt, however, that 3s. to 4s. per quarter upon foreign corn would be ample, probably less. All Colonial corn would come in free. A small tax in favour of New Zealitnd Mutton might be adopted without doing much harm. It is aft open question whether moderate import duties of this kind would effect the price of food at all. The kmericans would continue to endeavour to compete with the Colonial producers, and in competition would reduce their prices, with the result that while the price of corn might possibly increase to the extent of about one half the duty, the remainder would be paid by the American, Russian, or other foreign importer. All manufacturers who have to send goods into a heavily protected market are quite familiar with the process of specially shaving the price, in order to get through the protective barrier of duties, and there is no doubt that where competition is keen the exporter to a protected market pays a large proportion, if not all, of the duty himself. In spite of the is. per quarter imposed last year upon corn, the price was lower than it had been .before. Immediately the duty of is. per quarter was taken off, the price of corn leapt up, and bakers actually advanced the price of bread. Fluctua- tions of is. per quarter are commonly experienced in the grain trade, and to speak of a moderate duty of this kind as a crushing load upon the poor people of this country is the language of extravagance. Exactly What the result of Preferential Tariffs would be cannot be defined. We have it as a fact that the Colonials import 150 millions sterling of goods each year from countries other than Great Britain. There is therefore a large and growing trade for the British manufacturer to exploit. The result of the preference given by Canada has already been most marked, and there can be but little doubt that preference of a similar character given us by other of our Colonies would bear immediate fruit.</p>
        <p>Referring again to the Annual Statement of the Trade of the United Kingdom, I find the exports of the pro- duce of the United Kingdom to Canada during the past five years has been as follows :</p>
        <p>1898 £5,838,000 1899 ... £6,969,535 1900 ... £7,605,257 1901 ... £7,785,472 1902 £10,345,256</p>
        <p>It may be interesting to compare these figures with the exports to Australia during the same period, which were as follows :</p>
        <p>1898 ... £17,085,051 1899 ... £17,957,156 1900 ... £21,575,828 1901 .............£21,356,491 1902 ... 219,530,118</p>
        <p>It will be observed that our exportsto Australia have been practically stagnant during these five years, while our trade with Canada, with the assistance of the Pre- ferential Tariff has almost doubled during the same period. I see an announcement that a Glasgow firm has ob- tainel a contract for 20 locomotives for the Canadian Pacific Railway Co., in competition with the Americans, and the Germans, and that it was by the assistance only of the Preferential Tariff that the British manufacturer was enabled to secure this order. The value of a loco- motive is approximately £4,000, the total value of this order is therefore £BO,OOO. The whole of that £BO,OOO will be distributed among the members of this community in the form of income, either as wages, rent, or profit, with the exception of the small portion of the material employed which may have to be imported from a foreign country. £BO,OOO is sufficient to keep nearly 1,000 workmen 12 months. Here is one concrete example, and there can be little doubt that unless we are pre- pared to do something for Canada in return for the con- cession which she is making us, this concession will be withdrawn, and the preference at pre_ sent given to us will no longer continue.</p>
        <p>question generally, and if it has any local application it is for thosewhose concern it is to apply it. Is not the somewhat unsatisfactory condition of the trades interested in the manufacture and further</p>
        <p>manipulation of steel, largely due to th want of appli. cation of the most improved methods, and the best machinery for the inanufacture of the different pro- ducts? Are there not many works in the country as well as machines requiring scrapping? I venture to think that if some of the energy now being employed crying out for a revolution in our fiscal policy were used to effect a revolution amongst mmterstmen, methods, and machinery, it mould add much to the general pros- perity. If Protection is allowed to creep into this country it will create a premium for inefficiency, it will perpetuate methods,ithat ought to be obsolete, and it will enrich the few, and impoverish the many. It will give power to combinations of capital to create Trusts , arid Cartels, which will be ableto corner and raise the I price of articles of general use. It will lessen the guarantees for peaceful co-operation between this coun- try, our Colonies, and all the countries with which we trade for mutual advantage, it will put back progress and plant the seeds of strife.</p>
        <p>I am sorry to see that Mr. Bindloss purposes retiring from this controversy. We have cleared away some preliminaries, and are just getting warmed to the work, and suddenly our friend gets weary and retires. Itis somewhat amusing in this connection to read the question of Mr. Bindloss "Why should Mr. Parker and his co-thinkers be in dread of an enquiry ?" I had an impression that at the invitation of the editor of this paper we were doing a little in a local way to prosecute this enquiry. Mr Chamberlain and other members of the Government have invited business men, the Press Chambers of Commerce,&amp;c., to take part in this enquiry. Mr. Bindloss with a commendable zeal enters into the task and assists the enquiry, and just as he is getting comfortably at the work, and before it is half finished, he withdraws from the correspondence. He tells me that I havingthe "weaker side to defend shall have the privilege of the last word, while at the same time he tells us that" we dread enquiry." We welcome enquiry, and regret that Mr. Bindloss should get weary and withdraw from taking part in it. One word more, if in this correspondence I have spoken with an emphasis born of settled convictions, ),Ir.Bindloss:will:please attribute it to the strength of the cause I advocate, and not as he does in his last, to an assumption of personal authority.</p>
        <p>I may have something further to say to some points I have not been able to touch in this letter, but I do not see how I caR get on much further without the helpful stimulus of letters from Mr. Bindloss. I shall feel something like those traders who under Protection get flabby and lazy, because the invigorating influence of competition and opposition is withdrawn.</p>
        <p>curtailment of wages, and the lessened spending- power of the people ; but -it is a subject so important that, with the Editor's permission, I will later on enter into it, in detail, to show how the present enormous purchase of foreign manufactured goods disturbs. British home indutries It has been gratifying to me to find that many business men, in. Warringtondistrict, are taking an interest in this series of letters': awl it will be my endeavour to make each lettr contain same fresh point of view. Indeed, the subject is so vast, that a volume could easily be written, without at all exhausting the topics that arise in its discu-ssion. With the Elitor's per; mission, I shall be pleased to contribute several more letters, to open out, or develop, further phrases of tills greatest of national questions. This morning I unexpectedly received an encouraging letter, from London, from one of the most keen and able of Warrington business men. He had taken my last week's installment to read in the train, and he kindly enclosed me a cutting from "The People," headed "'acts and Figures for' the Thoughtful." I am at his suggestion, reproducing some of the valuable material he has forwarded, as it will help lour Trade Union friends to see where their true interests are to be found. I addresslny remarks specially to Trade Unionists, because the settlement of our future national policy, for Protection or for Free- trade, will, to a very great extent be decided by the massed votes of Trade Union men, at the general elec- tion which may very suddenly come up. Here is a workingman's letter, reproduced from "The People":—</p>
        <p>"A fellow workman and I were in receipt of 30 shillings per week, each, some time ago. My pal decided that he. would abandon his country and the flag, and go to a country which would do better for him. So he want to the Unit6d States, and earned GO shillings per week, right away. He wants me to follow him, and as a proof of the benefits which he receives, in a country where labour is protected by Protective Tariffs,he sent me the following instruc- tive little table : In this Country his In U.S.A. his re- receipts were 30s. 301- ceipts are 60s. 60/- weekly weekly His weekly expendi- His weekly expendi- ture in this Country 27/- ture in U.S.A. is was</p>
        <p>Margin of Saving) Margin of Saving power in this 3/-power in U.S.A 24- Country</p>
        <p>or in other words, for every shilling he used to SAVO in this country,he can now sive eight shillings, in the U.S.A., besides having better educational facilities provided for his children." Free Importers will have a hard job to explain away these figures. They are facts which strike every British workman in the face, and tell him that, under the pre- sent system, "something is wrong." . Another story from "The People" :—We are told by the Free Importers thaC though wages may be higher under a system of Protection, the cost of living will be higher still. Have they heard of the Irishman who, , unable to get work in h:s ovira country, came over to England to look for it, and was lucky enough to find it. The first Saturday night, when ha got his wages,he want to buy a dozen eggs. He was charged a shilling for them. He was aghast. "A shilling," said he," Is it a shilling for a dozen of eggs? Why, sure, when I was in Ireland, I could buy a chzen of eggs for sixpence." "‘Then why," he NY IS asked, "didn't you stay there? ' Because, bedad, I hadn't the sixpence to bay the n This remark of the Irishman conveys to us, in a nutshell the dishonesty of the Free Imparters in laying stress upon the possible increase in the price of various necessaries of life,without at the same time fairly allow- ing for thecounterbalancing advantage of work and wages which will accrue to the Iworking man under the pro- Posed new order of things.</p>
        <p>"The People article 3 contains two other items, of special value, and particularly wallset-out (1) •' Every year, thanks to the present mis- chievous system. of Free Imports, we are losing thousands of the best workmen in this Country lost to the Empire, th3ir Country, and their flag. That this constant giving away of British stock to foreign countries constitutes a real danger to us, the fol- lowing figures will show. According to-the present rates of emigratioa,and increases of population,this will be our position in 17 years' time. British Empire (Whites) 62,000,009 United States 1.00,000,000 Germany The best and surest way to avert such a fate as this is to make this country a more paying place to live in, by abandoning the paralysing system of •Free Imports.", (2) "One of the stock arguments of the Free Importers is, that Mr. Chamberlain has admitted, as an axiom, that a tax on imports must raise the price of food to the consumer. So far as we are aware, he has never admitted anything of the kind; nor is it by any manna a rule of universal apylication— particularly in commodities of ?Mich the consumption is general, anti the supply, therefore, very large." Let us examine how the duty on wheat, in France, affected the price of that most important com- modity.</p>
        <p>1878-1882. 1899-1902. s. d. a. d. French Import Duty on Waeat, 1 01- 12 2 per quarter. Average Price of Wheat, per quarter. .1 49 10 37 in France. We fancy that these most significant figures —which by the way, were supplied a few days ago by the President of the Board of Trade, in an answer to a question in Parliament—will surprise a good many people who believe the Radical tale that a tax on foodstuffs. must, necessarily, mean a cor- responding rise in the price of them to the consumer in this country."</p>
        <p>I had intended incorporating or weaving-up some interesting material, drafted by Lord Masham, but my Warrington friend (in London) was wishful for his fellow-townsmen to see the telling facts he sent me from" The People," so I must needs leave Lord Alasham's instructive details for a further letter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>MR. C. E. PARKER REPLIES TO MR. E. W.</head>
        <p>MR. C. E. PARKER REPLIES TO MR. E. W.</p>
        <p>BINDLOSS.</p>
        <p>The Iron Trade and Retirement of Mr. Bindloss.</p>
        <p>MR. PARKER writes :</p>
        <p>I avow my ignorance in respect to the details of the trade with which Mr. Bindloss is so honourably associated. But as an outsider I notice the rapid fluctuation in the imports and exports. During the last five or six years there have been great increases of the imports of iron ore, puddled and pig iron, iron bar, bolts and rod, and imwrought steel generally in the form of billets &amp;c. The increase corresponds closely with the period covered by the coal boom. Am t not right in inferring, that British iron manufacturers scoured the world, and bought all they could of these articles from foreigners, because as a result of the rise in coal, the British product was much dearer, and indeed the required quantity was not produced? I doubt not that the Warrington manufacturers took advantage of their ability to buy in the cheapest market the goods they required for further manufacture, and were then able to'sell their products to other industries in which iron is used,goods at a much lower cost than if they had been entirely dependent on the British product, costly and scarce in consequence of the high price of coal. If tariff barriers had existed to keep out, orto increase, the price of iron goods in the early stage of their manufac- ture, the Warrington works would have been seriously handicapped. If Mr. Bindloss replies that he would not tax iron inthe early stage of its manufacture, I would ask him how he is -going to keep company with Mr. Chamberlain whose object is to help the Colonies which only produce food stuffs, or goods in the early stage of their manufacture? If he parts company with Mr. Chamberlain, and only taxes goods after the manufac- ture has considerably progressed, we would like him to indicate at what point he would begin his taxation. If only certain trades are to be protected, then how are the other trades unprotected, and the consumers generally, to be recouped for the disadvantage they are placed under from the inevitable increase in prices of the protected articles? Protection for selected trades would mean injustice and loss to the others. It would create an artificial activity in certain trades at the expense of others, and a consequent disturbance of labour. It would mean tbo gain of a fewand the loss of many, and in the national stocktaking the balance would come out at the wrong side. Mr. Bindloss makes a contrast between the exports of bar iron or steel in 1882 and 1902, which shows a great decrease. It would be interesting if Mr. Bindloss would show us how his Protection plan involving increased costs of production will help tbe export of this or any other article. He omits, however, to give the increased exports of iron goods under other heads, which in most cases indicate that the manufacture of iron has before exporting been carried some points further, all to the benefit of the country. Mr. Bindloss should have given us the figures dealing with iron and steel rails,telegraph and other wires, galvanized sheets, plates and sheets</p>
        <p>countries of the world in the same year amounted to ' engines and machinery, as well as of bar 70 millions. I have not before me the imports from the iron. May I venture to give one piece of advice rest of the world for 1902, but I may mention that the to Mr. Bindloss? In quoting figures, do not exports of British manufactures to Australia in that only sele,ct special items which support a case, but give year was 191 millions, or a decrease of two millior.s the whole which bear upon it, and further do not select from the figures of two years previously, while the particular abnormal boom years, but take five year total imports into the Continent of Australia would no periods. doubt show an increase. As I have said, Great Britain Ido not contend that the iron and steel trade is in a supplies her Colonies with about 100 millions of their prosperous a condition as we would all like. to see its requirements out of a total of about 250 millions. If It would be discourteous on my yart to make any we could secure 50 millions of the remaining 150 special local reference, but may Ibe allowed to put the millions the benefit derived by this country, and by t</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>ONE-SIDED FREE-TRADE v. PROTECTION.</head>
        <p>ONE-SIDED FREE-TRADE v. PROTECTION.</p>
        <p>Further Facts and Figures for Trade Unionists.</p>
        <p>Mr. H. P. HUGHES continueshis argument as follows When quoting some significant figures of 1902 im- ported, manufactured or partly-manufactured goods, in my last week's letter, I laid special stress upon the well-known fact that a very large portion of such im- ports represents wages totally lost to British workers The aggregate yearly total of these lost wages, will amount to many million pounds a year; and it occurs to me to ask how many of us, who so glibly speak or write of "millions," ever give a thought to grasp or comprehend what a " million " of money really is? Let me try to make a simple illustration, to help in bringing home to all of us some idea of the extent of the enormous loss of wage-earning power, caused by the present improvident, reckless importation of manufac- tured or partly-manufactured goods. To count a million sovereigns, would occupy the smartest of bank cashiers nearly seventeen days, counting (or weighing) a hundred sovereigns a minute. and working without cessation for ten hours each day.</p>
        <p>It would be rendering a valuable public service, if our Trade Union leaders would endeavour to ascertain, from the Government 1902 Blue Book, the total value of imported manufactured or partly-manufactured articles; and then tell their men what amount of this could fairly be estimated as lost wages to British work- ers. At a rough guess, I should say that these lost wages would represent at the very least twenty to thirty million pounds a year.lf my rough estimate is anything like correct, it would mean that—the leaders of British Trade Unions,-while fiercely contending with Capital ists in regard to the "gnat" of maybe a farthing or a half- penny an hour, incontinently allow the "camel)" of im- ported manufacturers to rob Trade Union labour to the tune of at least a hundred sovereigns of wages, during every minute of ten hours of every working day in the year. Surely this caps the world's record for incon- sistency! The wage-earner is the true backbone of the country; and we all know that if a back-bone is seriously injured, the result—sooner or later—may be hopeless paralysis. Applying this backbone metaphor to the Free-trade question, we should have to allow that the life of a nation is so much longer than the life of an individual that it takes, sometimes, two or three generations before a mistaken policy plainly manifests its inevitable symp- toms of trade or commercial paralysis. Thus, although the Navigation Laws (devised by Cromwell and the Long Parliament) were repealed, more than half a century ago, it was not until last year, when Mr. J. P. Morgan coolly appropriated over two hundred and eighty of our merchant snips, that the astounding folly of that long-forgotten Act of repeal was manifested so plainly that the-man-in-the-street became alarmed lest he should be too late to repair the national backbone. By the way, I may mention—in passing—that, in March last year, just while public opinion was warm upon the great question of this shipping purchase, I wrote an article in the Liverpool " Courier," sketching the history and object of the Cromwell and Long-Parlia. ment Navigation Laws. A reprint was sent to Mr. Balfour, Mr. Chamberlain, and, many leading Members of Parliament—including Lord Charles Beres- fora, and others Interested in the Navy League; also to the "Daily Mail" and chief newspapers. With over fifty years having elapsed since the Navigation Laws were repealed, I find that very few men of the present generation have made a- study of the effect of our Merchant Shipping Acts upon the prosperity of this country. As a result of repealing the Navigation Laws, over half a century ago, and showing the slow but sure undermining of our commercial sea-power during the past 50 years, I pointed out in the "Courier" article that out of 200,000 seamen at present in our mercantile marine, no less than 70,000 were foreigners. These seventy thousand displaced Britishers, are now elbow- ing a similar number of their fellow-countrymen in search of a job, and our Trade Union leaders have not—so far as I have ever seen in print said a word, or raised a protest Then, in the event of a naval war, we should find that our present system of employing more and more foreign mercantile seamen has discouraged the breading of true British fighting tars. Could we expect these 70,000 foreign seamen to make the same desperate efforts to avoid the capture of their cargoes, that 70,000 Britishers would do? Not likely. We might, rather, expect ,(and deserve) many repetitions of the recent Veronica In illustrating; the evils that the Wage-earner suffers by Free trade, I have been carried away into a little studied aspect of the question ;—but it is all plrt and parcel of the same story. In our examination of free trade doctrines, we must keep continually before us the great fact that we live on an island ; indeed it is just because we do live on an island, that this subject of f Free trade v Protection is far, very far more complex t and many-sided, than it is in the United States, or Germany, or France, or Russia. Ships and seamen must be at.the beginning and the end of every discussion of British trade, or trading policy, hence I hope that my Trade Union friends wll, in future, closely watch all legislation bearing upon sea-faring British labour, and British Shipping interests, to see that the Island policy of " England for the English. " is no longer overlooked, as it has been for so many years. At this point, I was going to take up the question of the effect upon the nation's prosperity, through i</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>VOLUNTEER ENCAMPMENT. Sunday's 6iul—ih Parade.</head>
        <p>THE VOLUNTEER ENCAMPMENT. Sunday's 6iul—ih Parade.</p>
        <p>This (Saturday) evening 'the members of the Ist V.B. Prince of Wales's Volunteer (South Lancashire Regiment) will depart for a fortnights' encampment at Levvaigne, near Ramsey, Isle of Man. They will assemble atithe Drill Hall at seven o'clock for medical inspection and afterwards march to the Central Station and entrain for Liverpool as follows :-Ist train, band bugle band, A, 13, C, cyclists and WI. Companies; 2nd train, D, E, F, H. I. Companies. A guard's van will be specially detailed for band instruments, 2nd Lieutenant F. R. Monks will attend at the station half an hour in advance of the trains to arrange carriage accommoda- tion. The volunteers will set sail for the island at ten o'clock on board the steamship "Empress Queen' and will disembark at Ramsey at day break. On Sunday morning Divine service will be held at the camp at 11.30. and work will commence on Monday. Given fine weather the men are anticipating a highly enjoyable time in Manxland, and they will no doubt return greatly benefitted by life under canvas. The advance party left on Wednesday morning to make the usual preparations, Captain B. Fairclough being in command. Captain and Quarter- master A. F. G. Potter, as usual, has charge of the com- missariat.</p>
        <p>It has for many years been the custom for the Church of England section of the Volunteer Battalion to attend divine service at the Parish Church on the Sunday morning previous to their departure for camp. Last Sunday's parade was in every way worthy of the battalion, about 400 men turning out in response to the request of the Comnuutding Officer. As usual, the band under the direction of Bandmaster Richardson, together with the bugle band headed the procession, which left the Drill Hall at ten o'clock. The smart appearance of the Volunteers was generally commented upon , Major Appleton was in command, and other officers in attendance were Captain and Adjutant Leader, Captains J. D. Fairclough, B. Fairclough, R. Smith and H. G. Roberts, and Lieutenants Owen, Avery, Fulton Smith, R. M. Roberts. and Monks. There was a large congre- gation at the Parish Church and the service was con- ducted throughout by the Rector (Rev. Canon Willis). The special hymns sung were "Soldiers of Christ arise," "0 God, our help in ages past," and" The Church's one foundation," all of which were accompanied by the band. The Rector preached a suitable sermon on the reign and character of David.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>WARRINGTON GRAMMAR SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON GRAMMAR SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL SPEECH DAY.</p>
        <p>Speech Day in connection with this school was . observed on Thursday, and was as successful as its pre- - decessors. By the time the proceedings started the room was filled with a company consisting of the rela- tives and friends of the boys. The following is a list of those present :—Rev. E. J. and Mrs. Willcocks Mr. C. White, Miss Broadbent, Mr. W. Davies, Mrs. and Miss Willis, Mrs. H. Roberts, Mr. F. Monks, Mrs. F. NV. Monks, Canon and Mrs. Penrhyn, Rev. E. C. E. and Mrs. Carleton, the Rev. F. Forbes, Mr. and Mrs. Carruthers, Rev. R. S. Moxon, Mr. J. ! Young, Mr. and Mrs. C. F. Hero,,,r, and Mr. and Mrs. S. Woods, Mrs. and Miss Coxon, Mr. and Mrs. J. Lyon . Whittle, Mr. and Mrs. S. Holford, Mr. and Mrs. McMurray, Mr. and Mrs. Skelton, Mr. and Mrs. E. Wallington, Mr. A. J. Jolley, Mr. and Mrs. Horabin, Mr. and Mrs. Wright, Mrs. Alderson, Miss Harrison,Mr. and Mrs. Thomas, Mr. and Mrs. Henshall, Mr. and Mrs. Chamberlain, Mr. and Mrs. J. T. Whittle, Mr. and Mrs. Whitehouse, Mr. and Mrs. R. Kenyon, the Misses Williams, Miss Pierpoint, Miss M. Thornton, Mr. and Mrs. Sankey, Mr: and Mrs. Ruddock Mr and Mrs. Greaves' Mr. and Mrs. J. Houghton, Miss Woods, Mr. and Mrs. Rogers, Mr. and Mrs. Weaver, Mr. E. and Airs. Houghton, Mr. R. and Miss Thompson, Mr. and Mrs. Jas. Fairclough, Miss Fairclough, Mr. and IVlrs. and Miss Madeley. Mr. and Mrs. .1. A. Gibson, Mr. and Mrs. W. B. Roberts, Mr. T. W. Roberts, Mr. and Mrs. NV. Monks, Mrs. and Miss McWhannell, Mr. and Mrs. Gandy, Mr. and Mrs. Wilcox, Miss Alice Burgess, Mr. and Mrs. C. S. Burgess, Mrs. Davey, Mrs. Carruthers, Mr. and Mrs. T. Gibson, Miss Robinson, Mrs. Pickles, Mr Mrs. and Miss Hesketh, Mr. and Mrs, Locker, Mrs: Dutton Mr. and Mrs. Dawson Mr. and Mrs. Burrow Mrs, and Miss Young, Mr. and Mrs. Baniforth, Mr. and Mrs. Collins. Mr.and Mrs. J. Gandy, Miss Gandy,. Mr, and Mrs. Atkin, Mr. and Mrs. Lord, Mr. and Mrs. E. H. Dutton, Mr. F. M. Darby, Mr. and Mrs. J. Finch, Mr. and Mrs. Price, Mrs. and Miss Haddock, Mr. Mrs. and Miss Ferguson, Mrs. C. H. Woods, Mrs. W. Parkinson, Mr., Mrs. and Miss McGregor, Mr. and Mrs. Hallwood, Mr. and Mrs. Nield, Mr. and Mrs. Kennon, Miss Bond, Mr. and Mrs. Westbrook, Mr. and Mrs. A. Browne, the Misses Adams, Mr. and Mrs. Waring, Coun- cillor and Mrs.W. .Roberts,lllr. and. Mrs. R. T. Fairclough, the Misses Fairclough, Mrs. and Miss Carlisle, Mr. and Mrs. W. Linskey, the Misses White, Mr. Charles White, " Miss Massey and friends, Mr. J. H. Fisher, Dr. and Mrs. Geary, Dr. and Mrs. Adams, Rev. G. and Mrs. Vale Owen, Rev. T. and Mrs. Barber, Rev. W. and Mrs. Pollock-Hill, Rev. NV. and Mrs. Bracecamp, the Misses Carnson, Rev. G. H. and Mrs. Carnson, Mrs. Herzog. The Headmaster at the outset read several letters of apology, including those from the Mayor, the Rector (the Res,. Canon Willis), and Mr. Henry Roberts. In presenting his anaual report the headmaster (the Rev. E. .T. Willcocks) said: It is customary for me to 1 open our proceedings this afternoon by first of all , bidding you a hearty welcome and then by laying before ' you a report ontbe progress of the School during the past year. The School is examined annually under the Dalegacy of the Oxford Local Examinations, and most of the boys in the Sixth and Fifth Forms sit for the Certificate granted by the University. Our list last year was an exceptionally good one. Of our senior ' candidates two,. viz., E. G. Burgess and L. G. Corney— our two head boys—were excused the examination known as "Responsions" at Oxford. With respect to our fifteen junior candidates, we had 13 passes in Religi- ous Knowledge, 11 in Latin. 14 in French, 13 in Mathematics, and several in English and Greek. These results speak for 'themselves, and I think show that our work is sound. Our aim is at the outset, as you are probably aware, generally culture, so that when a boy reaches the Stxth Form he is ready to be specialized with real profit in any _ . . . . .</p>
        <p>branch of learning t-hat his parents may -desire. Th-e results of the Oxford Locals for this year are, of course, not yet to hand, but it will no doubt interest you to hear what the School Examiner has to say about us : "It has given me great pleasure to make the acquaint- ance of Warrington School. The town is to be congratu- lated on having in its midst a set of buildings so well adapted for the purposes of a High School, and on the excellent opportunities which the school, as at present managed and carried on, affords to the boys of the neighbourhood. The discipline and tone of the school appeared to be excellent, so far as I could judge in the course of a week's visit. The school curriculum is unusually wide, and the work, as tested by the examina- tion which I have conducted, thoroughly_ sound. The</p>
        <p>boys are well togethe... in every form and in well nigh every subject, and the level they have obtained is dis- tinctly high. There can be no doubt that they have had sound and scholarly preparation, and the results must be very encouraging to the Read Master and his staff." Turning to education matters in] general the Bill to which I referred in my remarks last year has now become law inif till force. The Education Committee of our own County Borough has already begun to grapple strenuously with the problems that undoubtedly demand a pressing solution—but naturally the work done so far has been with regard to our Elementary and Continua- tion Schools. Von will remember that last year I advocated the provision of a Continuation School with low fees, if any, which would provide for the need, which certainly exists, of those boys who have passed</p>
        <p>the sixth standard and yet who can give two or -three additional years to education before going to work as apprentices or junior clerks or such like. The scheme of such a school is now being considered by the com- mittee. Then again I urged that a wider basis should be given to theladucation of the Grammar School. If financial aid—either private benefactions or other- wise—were given to this school which already exists and has definite and valued traditions of a high standard of work, it would be possible to develop, as has been done in other Grammar Schools, a modern, scientific side of equally permanent value. A moderate addition to'our present staff and equipment would ensure a really good and satisfactory solution of the problem. Any other course wouldmean a further increase in the ratesAwhich are already do'omed to rise in the provision of the new primary and con- tinuation schools, and surely it argues no lack of love or zeal for higher eclucationif we try to provide it in a way which is efficient and yet shows some consideration for the pockets of the ratepayers. Coming back once more to this,•our own school, one of the most inter- esting events of the year has been the formation of an Old Boys Union, and the dinner in connection therewith iThe objects aimed at are three in number, viz :—The re-union of Old Boys, the giving annually a prize or prizes for history, and the founding of a University Scholarship. The attainment of this last object naturally leads us un to the saddest event of the year. I refer to the loss of our dear old friend Mrs. Beamont. Many years ago she evinced her interest in this school by founding a scholarship of £5O per annum to be held at either Oxford or Cambridge in memory of her husband. She was always present at our annual Speech Day gatherings and I believe that last year through failing health was the first time she had been absent for many years. I know that I personally felt her absence very much at the time, it seemed as if a light had gone out from amongst us. She was always so sympathetic: in her support, so interested in her work here that her loss to me is irreparable. I trust it will be the aim of all of us who hold her remembrance dear, to emulate her simplicity, her generosity and her goodness. I have great pleasure in announcing that Mr. J. Charlton Parr has presented the school with £lOO in honour of the Coronation of our gracious King, Edward VII. The interest on this capital sum is to be given in prizes at the discretion of the Head Master. This year the prizes are awarded for the encouragement of modern languages. The successes and appointments of past and present boys during the year include A. B. Miles.—Queen's College, Cambridge, elected to a Foundation Scholarship of £4O per annum. G. E. Hasketh.—Pembroke College, Cambridge, gradu- ated, B.A. in the Mathematical Tripos. T. M. 01111.—Seven passes in the First class in the annual examination at the Camborne Mining School. J. W. Box and W. Greene.—First Class in the King's Scholarships Examination. F. A. Burton and J. Lamb.—lntermediate Examination of the Incorporated Law Society. B.Silcock and T. Porter.—Final Examination of the Institute of Chartered Accountants.</p>
        <p>C. T. Cooke.—London University Intermediate Examination for the degree of Bachelor of Law, and also a First Class in the Intermediate Examination of the Incorporated Law Society. R. A. Holford.—lntermediate Examination of the Institute of Chartered Accountants. H. H. Carleton.—Keble College, Oxford, has graduated B.A. in the First Class in the Honour School of Natural</p>
        <p>• Science. F. E. Weeks.—First place out of 223 competitors in the examination for Assistant Clerkships in the Civil Service. _ _</p>
        <p>G. H. Joseph.—University College, Liverpool. Part I. of the Final Examination for the degree of M. 8., Ch.B., Victoria University. We have also to congratulate Mr. R. E. Kenyon, B.Sc., late of University College, Liverpool, on his appointment to the Second Mastership at Shebbear College, North Devon. Also the Rev. F. H. Cooke, M.A., latelof Queen's College, Cambridge, and Leeds Clergy School, who has been made a deacon by the 13 Bishop of Durham, and licen ed to the Curacy of Bishop Auckland. Also the Rev. E. B. Carleton, 8.A., of Keble and Cuddesdon, Oxford, who as been made a deacon by the Bishop af Wakefield, and licensed to the curacy of Elland, Yorkshire. It now remains for me to assure the LOrd Bishop of Burnley, of our gratitude for his presence here to-day. He has had experience of educa- tion of many types and in many places, and his interest in it is, I know, still keen and undiminished. It is, therefo.e, with more than ordinary pleasure that I welcome him in your name this afternoon, and prize- winners will esteem their rewards of higher value in that they are received at his hands. LIST OF PRIZES.</p>
        <p>The Bishop of Burnley then distributed the prizes as follows :</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>PRIZE LIST.</head>
        <p>THE PRIZE LIST.</p>
        <p>CLASSICS :—VI. Form, Corney ; V. Form, Upper ivisio n, Owen; Lower Division, Adams; Upper IV.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <p>Form, Wallington; Lower IV. Form, Carnson ; IIL Form, Monks, ma; IL Form, Dawson; I. Form, Gar- lick, ma.</p>
        <p>MATEIEMATHICS :—VI. Set, Burgess ma; V. Set, Alderson; Upper and LowerTV. Sets, Wilcox. ARITHMETIC:—III. and IL Sets, Young; Upper and Lower Sets, Bunch, mi. ANCIENT HISTORY :—VI. Form (Roman 146 B.C. to 14 A.D.), Corney ; V. Form, Owen. ENGLISH HISTORY (1390-1603) :—Upper and Lower IV. Forms, Wallington; 111. and It. Forms, Greaves ; Upper and Lower I. Forms, Locker.</p>
        <p>SCIENCE :—VI. Set, Physics, Alderson; V. Set, Chemistry, Kennon; IV. Set, Elementary Science, Carnson.</p>
        <p>SCRIPTURE KNOWLEDGE :--Lamplugh Memorial„ VI. Form, (St. Luke with the Greek, and I. Kings), Corney; V. Form, St. Luke and I. Kings), (Rev. M.- Stevenson's prize), Alderson; IV, Form, (I. Kings xii to end and 11. Kings),( Rev. E.C.E. Carleton'spri ze,)Niel d ;- IVlassiugham Memorial, 111 and II 'Forms, (St. Luke). Raddock, ma; Upper and Lower I Forms, Locker. MODERN LANGUAGES :—VI. Set German, Dutton, ma; V. Set German, Owen; VI. Set French, Dutton, ma; V. Set French, Horobin; IV. Set French, Wilcox; 111. Set French Carnson. _ _</p>
        <p>DRA.WING :-L-Shading, McMurray; Model, Carlisle; Freehand (advanced), Dawson; Freehand (elementary), Longton. WRITING (Lower school) :—Burrows. PHYSICAL GEOGRAPHY (Modern side) :—Collins. POLITICAL ECONOMY :—Burgess, ma. ENGLISH VERSE :—Virgil Soot( VI., 808-853 (Mr. C. White's prize), Corney. GREEK JAMBICS (Canon Willis' prize) :—Corney. NATURAL HISTORY COLLECTION (Moths S:c) : Ferguson, ma. ASHTON MEMORIAL :—Sankey, ma. OXFORD LOCAL EXAMINATION, 1902.</p>
        <p>SENIOR CANDIDATES.—Honours Divison : Second class. L. G. Corney; pass division, E. G. Burgess. JUNIOR CANDIDATES.—Hotiours Division: Third class, J. Barnforth and H. Dutton; pass division, J. it. Alderson, R. S. M. F. McMurray, H. L. Pigot, A. V. Ferguson, A. R. Owen, H. Sankey, A. C. Horobin, B. Percival, A. Webster, %V. Hudson, A. Smith. Recommended for election to Boteler Scholarship.— A. R; Owen, J. R. Alderson, and M. J. Burgess. BISHOP OF BURNLEY'S SPEECH.</p>
        <p>Having distributed the prizes his Lordship, during the course of a lengthy address, said if he was to judge from the number of functions similar to that which he had had to attend he would be convinced that extreme interest was being - taken in the country in the mutter of education The strange thing about the Education Bill was that it •was brought forward entirely to support the work of the improvement of secondary schools, whereas 99 per- cent. of the speeches made while the bill was in the House of Commons had been on elementary education,. and the country still appeared to be in the dark in' regard to the fact that the bill was for secondary educa- tion. It was necessary for them to realize that the bill was passed in order to help forward progressive work and co-ordinate work in regard to secondary schools, and he hoped that before long the difficulty under which they laboured would be conquered. If the Act was worked honestly he thought people would re- cognize the good which it would do in regard to educa- tion, and instead of the Government coming in for abuse it would be looked upon as one which had done more for the progress of the country that many other Govern— ments which spent their time discussing questions not nearly so important as the question of education. It therefore rested with them to make the best of theh remarkable Act, and if they did so and carried it out honestly, he thought their children and children's chil- dren would have good cause to thank them for the work they were doing at the present moment.- If they did pay an extra rate because of their secondary schools they should look upon it inthat light and instead of objecting give up some of the luxuries of life, because they knew that good education meant the prosperity of the country. They needed to do all they' could to stimulate the growth and the training of their future public men and women. There was no country in the world with such a splendid army of public men and women as England possessed, and it was because of the voluntary work done by those public men and women that England occupied the position she did, and they must remember that Warrington and the other places would depend largely in the future upon the character of the boys in those schools. There was to much attention paid to sport—perfectly innocent in its way—and they should train up their children to look more upon the serious side of life, and one of the most important things in doing that was to teach their chil- dren the simple truths and to teach them to live for God and man. Where they had an education which was purely secular they found that they had a loss of charac- ter, and a loss of the desire for public service. He. hoped the school would continue to prosper and to turn outfthe boys cultured young gentlemen who understood what true nobility meant. (Applause.) The Bishop was heartily thanked for his address. The boys afterwards gave three plays, "L'Aila de In Rue de Lourcine " (French), "The birds of Arts, plumes" (Greek) and scenes from "A Midsmuin e rFruegnhc b. dream," in which the characters were taken by ( r ire tton nlay) V. G. Roberts, E. G. Burgess, 14. Du . R. S. M. F. McMurray, and H. 0. Aditniss A (Greek play). L. G. Corney, J. Bamforth, M. J. Burgess 4 J. Bamforth, A. R. Owen, M. J. Burgess, C. S. WoOdOP and T. B. Weaver; (English play), J. R. Alderson,. Air' C. Horobin, B. Madeley, D. Skelton, A. F'. Grice, and Keenon. The stage managers were H. Sankey, A. Ferguson, and H. F. Woods.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-07-25_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>WARRINGTON MOTORISTS' • STARTLING EXPERIENCE Immersed in the River at Sutton • Weaver.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON MOTORISTS' • STARTLING EXPERIENCE Immersed in the River at Sutton • Weaver.</p>
        <p>An extraordinary accident befel a couple of Warring.' ton motorists at Sutton Weaver, on the Warringtort side of Frodsham, on Wednesday night. It appears that Mr. J. Hulton,of Salisbury-street,who is connected with the Union Tannery, and Mr. George H. Smith, of the office of Messrs. Greenall and Co., solicitors, were travelling in a motor car from Chester to Warrington - On approaching the river Weaver which crosses the main road, they failed to notice that the gate was closed while a steamer was passing, owing, it is alleged, t° there being no red light on the Frodshani side of the river. The car crashed through the gat and into the river and the occupants were immersea The Salt Union steamer Albion and barges were passing at the time and the captain and hands succeeded ill getting the motorists on board. Divers recovered the motor car, which was eventually landed on terra firma in a badly damaged condition. Several rugs were lost. The motorists, who escaped unhurt, spent the night at 'an adjacent hotel, and on Thursday morning Mr. W. Bellian, of Warrington, brought them home in his car.</p>
