        <p>( NOUNTY BOROUGEI OF WARRING ON.—TO x.) ENGINEERS, ecc• The TOWN HALL, PARK and BA CHS COMMIT CBE of the Council of toe County Borou/h of Warrington is PREPARED TO RECEIVE TENDERS for a PRA.RN or CAMERON PUMP, capable of delivering 403 gallons per hour. Any further information may be obtained at the office of the Borough Surveyor. Town H4ll, at which place tenders must be de iverei before 12 o'clock noon, on Thursday. the 6th day of August. 1933, endorsed " Ten-ter for Pump," and addressed to the Chairmen of the Town Hall, Park and Baths Committee. Ihe lowest or any tender not necessarily accepted. THOS. LONGDIN. Borough Engineer and Surveyor. Borough Engineer and t..• urve) or's Office, July 24th, 1933.</p>
        <p>GUINNESS'S DUBLIN STOUT. BASS'S MILD AND BITTER Alas 4LLSOPP'S MILD AND BITTER ALES. WHEATLiirS HOP BITTERS. WHEATLEY'S GINGER BEER. To be had in CASH or BOTTLES from B. CUNNINGHAM' WINWICH ROAD, WARRINGTON. Also Manufacturer of HIGH CLASS MINERAL WATERS in Screw-stoppered Bottle.. Telephone No. 179. STORES. OWEN STREET. COUNTY BOROUGH OF WARRINGTON.- MUNICIPAL BYE-ELECTION. 1903 TO THE ELKO TORS OF Sr. JOHN'S WARD. LADIEs AND GENTLEMEN. At the request of the Comraitt?e of the Municipal Non-Trading Association, I have decided to offer myself as a Candidate for the vacancy occasioned by the death of the late Mr. Councillor Baird. I leave taken a very deep interest in the Non- Trading Movement from its commencement. and should you do me the honour of returning me as your representative. 1 woutd do my utmost to help on the good work which is being accomplished under the NnoTrading-banner. I am strenuously opposed to .41 illegal and Immoral trading by members of the C )uncil with the Corporation, believing as I do that all engaged in such trading cannot do their duty to the ratepayers. Self-interest under such conditions is bound to pre- dominate, and where that is in existence in public life the town must suffer. I should carefully watch all expenditure, with a view to bringing about a reduction in rates, whtch have now reached such a figure as to be almost. rainous. Unless all extravagant expenditure is speedily checked, the rates must of necessity be increased, and that would in all probability cause higher rents, which would materially affect the working classes. I pledge myself to oppose anything and every- thing in the nature of extra.vagance, and to work hand-IR-hand with the Non- Praders, who, in the Council Chamber, have been such a power for good. They have taken the ratepayers into their confidence by exposing many serious irregularities which, had it not been for them, w, told never have been brougot before the notice of the public. I appeal with every emil lence for your auepoet, and should you enable me to become your representa- tive, I promise to do my duty to the Ward and to the town as a whole. Trustinf to receive your votes, I am, Ladies and Gentlemen, Your obedient oervanl HENRY THORNTON. The Laurels, Yearnhead.</p>
        <p>TO THE BURGESSES OF ST. JJHN'S WARD. LADIES AND GENTLEMEN'. Mv sth tUBLIC MEETING will BE HELD on MONDAY NIGHT. JULY 27th on a VACANT SPACE BETWEEN DICKENSON STREET and LEONARD STREET ORFORD TANS. wh.n I shall have the opportunity of explainin4 to you.why I am a non-trader, how long I have been a non- trader and, what non-trading means or should mean and:to show you the reason why my honourable op— ponent Hr. Harry Thornton is the noroluee of the nontrading party and how long it is and why he be. came their convert. On TUESDAY. JULY 28th, I will hold the 6th and last of my present series of PUBLIC MEET- I; THE CO-OPERATIVE HALL, WINWICK- STRINT, when I will de,cribe to you a simple, legitimate, and legal means of preventing al illegal, and dishonest trading by members of the Council or their friends, and will also relieve the ratepayers of all doubt as to the honesty of the contractors, who may tender to supply any of the many articles advertised for at various times by th Corporation. I ask you one and all to attend these meetings %whether you are in favour of my oandidature or not, as the subject is of vital importance, not only to the Electors of St. Joha's Ward, but to every inhabitant D: Warrington. I am. Lailes and Gentlemen, Yours ever faithfully. JOHN MAGI.</p>
