        <p>VICTORIA COLLEGE OF ivillSlC. Warrington Centre. The following is a list of Candidates who were suc- cessful in gaining certificates at the Examinations held on July 7th at the Masonio Hill. The examiners being practical music Mr. J. H. Lewis, Mus. Doc, D.C.C. and in Theoretical Music Mr. J. M. Bently, Mus. Doc. Cantab, Hon. F.R.A.M.Teachers names are given in parenthesis. PRACTICAL MUSIC PIANO PLAYING. ADVATICEDISF.NIOR GRADE, Thomas R. Grant, (Mr. Charles Henry Dawson.) INTERMEDIATE GRADE :—Fred Finall r. A. Wood- cock) ; Edward Jones, Earlestown (Mr. C. H. Dawson.) JUNIOR GRADE:—Emma Wilson, honours (Mr. C. H. Dawson); Walter Gilman (Mr. Thomas R. Grant), Relies- town. PRIMARY GRADE :—Alice W. Lawrenson' honours (Mr. Woodcock), Earlestown; Lucy Standish, honours, Peter Peacock (Mr. C. H. Dawson). PREPARATORY GRADE :—Emily Thomas, honours (Miss G. Blair), Glazebrook ; representative in Warring- ton, C. H. Dawson, L.V.C.M., 28, Arthur-street, Bewaey- road. Also for the London College, Miss Mary Law and Miss Sarah Rigby (Mr. Dawson.) THEORETICAL EXAMINATION. INTERMEDIATE HONOURS SECTION :—Thomas Grant (Mr. C. H. Dawson, L.V.C.M., A.L.C.M.) INTERMEDIATE PASS SECTION :—Edward Jones Thomas R. Grant, Albert Woodcock, Earlestown (Mr. C. H. Dawson). JUNIOR HONOURS SECTION :—Joseph A. Hunt, Sarah Rigby (Mr. C. H. Dawson). JCNIOR PASS --Florence Whitfield, Henry Short, Ruth Lightfoot (Miss Lilly Green, A.V.C.M.), Joseph A. Hunt, Elsie Dawson, Sarah Rigby (Mr. C. H. Daw- son). PRIMARY SECTION :—Fred Finall (A. Woodcock) Earlestown; Walter Gilman (Thos. R. Grant), Earles- town ; Mary Chrimes (Miss L. Green), John Donovan, Alfred Few, Henry Gittens, Lucy Standish, Peter Peacock, Harold Plinston, John Peers (Mr C. H. •Daw- on. ) BRASS "GOLD DUST." The remarkable case in which two men endeavoured to dispose of 901 b. of brass filings, described as gold dust, for £2,285, came before Commissioner Rantoul at the Old Bailey on Wednesday. The prisoners, named David Weinstone, 42, dealer, and Rosen Soulman, 60, brickmaker, were indicted for attempting to obtain by false pretences £2,285 from Detective-sergeant Wensley. Mr. Simmonds recounted the story, which has already been fully told. The prisoners, who were betrayed to the police by a confederate, arranged to sell him the brass, which they said was gold dust. Real gold was sent as a sample to be tested, and the confederate was instructed to substitute water for aqua fortis in case an attempt should be made to test the brass itself. The jury found prisoners guilty. Soulman was sentenced to 18 months' hard labour, and Weinstone to 12 months. DR. BARTON'S AIRSHTp. The "Morning Advertiser," in describing Dr. F. A. Barton's airship, the construction of which he under- took ander encouragement from the War Office for use in War, says it is now almost complete. Dr. Barton expects that he will obtain a speed of 41 miles anhour. The airship is 140 feet long, the baloon 170 feet long, and the total weight of ship, crew, water, machinery, and nearly a ton of petrol, will be close upon seventons. IMPURITIES IN THE BL ooD.—" we have ErPen hosts of letters from people who have received great benefit from the use of Clark's Blood Mixture, It cannot be too highly esti- mated for it cleanses and clears the blood from all impurities This is a Rood testimonial from the" Family Doctor." which goes on further to say: It is the finest Blood Purifier that science raid skill have brought to light, and we can with the utmost confidence recommend it to our subscribers and the public generally." For Scrofula, Scurvy, Bezezna, Bad Legs, kin and Blood Diseases, Pimples and sores of all kinds the effects are marvellous. Thousands of testimonials. Clark's Blood Mixture is sold everywhere at 2s 9cl per bottle. Beware of worthless imitations and substitutes. Advt (4). CLARICE'S B 41 FILM are warionted to mire, in either sea, all aequired or constitutienal Discharges from the Urinary Organs, Gravel and Pains in the